window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Week 1 Content","Week 2 Content","Not Tested","A ★ marks something the instructor said outright would be on an exam."]},{"s":"Week 1 Content▾","a":"week-1-content","k":["Week 1 Content▾","First Sub-Topic","★ A thing she said you will be tested on — stated in about twenty words, with the specific number or threshold included.","A second point: high-yield, no star, because she never said it would be tested.","Second Sub-Topic","Group related facts into one bullet rather than splitting them — a bullet that carries a definition, its threshold, and its consequence is one reference item, not three.","Keep the wording tight. Drop the mechanism asides, the percentages you won't be asked for, and \"she said…\" on any bullet that already has a star.","Quick Reference Table","A compact table for material the instructor said to know exhaustively — the kind of list that would be twenty bullets otherwise.","First item — What lets you recognize it, including the discriminating test. — What is given. — What happens if it isn't.","Second item — Four columns is about the practical limit for readability. — Keep cells short. — One clause each."]},{"s":"Week 2 Content▾","a":"week-2-content","k":["Week 2 Content▾","Sub-Topic","Copy a .hint-group block per sub-topic, and an h2.block per week."]},{"s":"Not Tested▾","a":"not-tested","k":["Not Tested▾","Explicitly Excluded","Things the instructor said you do not need to memorize — exact doses, specific percentages, brand names, and so on.","Record these the moment you hear them. They are the cheapest studying you will ever skip."]}]},{"f":"week1-antimicrobials-antibiotics.html","t":"Antimicrobials & Antibiotics (ABX)","secs":[{"s":"Antimicrobials & Antibiotics (ABX)","a":"","k":["Week 1","Antimicrobial Basics","Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial","Patient Factors & Dosing","Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship","Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors","Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Antimicrobial Basics▾","a":"antimicrobial-basics","k":["Antimicrobial Basics▾","Bactericidal — Kills the bacteria outright.","Bacteriostatic — Only inhibits bacterial growth/reproduction — doesn't kill it.","Broad Spectrum — Effective against many organism types, gram-positive and gram-negative — a \"shotgun approach.\" Used when the organism isn't known yet or hasn't cultured out.","Narrow Spectrum — Effective against only a few species — a \"BB gun approach.\" Requires knowing the organism and what it's sensitive to.","Selective Toxicity — Toxic to the target bacterial cell while sparing the patient's own normal cells nearby.","Innate Resistance — The organism was never susceptible to begin with — it's always been that way.","Acquired Resistance — The organism mutated, or picked up resistance genes from another organism, after antimicrobial exposure.","Superinfection — A new infection that develops during treatment for a different one — e.g., candidiasis or C. diff while on antibiotics for something else.","Five Ways an Antimicrobial Can Work","Inhibit cell wall synthesis.","Increase cell wall permeability.","Inhibit protein synthesis (stops the bacteria from functioning, without necessarily killing it).","Inhibit DNA/RNA synthesis, or alter DNA function.","Disrupt a specific metabolic or biochemical reaction (e.g., folic acid synthesis).","Prophylactic antibiotics are sometimes given to prevent infection in higher-risk groups: certain surgeries (knee replacement, cardiac, abdominal), patients with endocarditis or a history of it, and immunocompromised patients — especially th…"]},{"s":"Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial▾","a":"choosing-starting-an-antimicrobial","k":["Choosing & Starting an Antimicrobial▾","Get Cultures First — Whenever Possible","The goal is always to get cultures and a sensitivity result before starting an antimicrobial. Giving the drug first can prevent the organism from growing on culture, making it harder to identify.","A culture order is a stat priority — go get it, even if that means delegating it to another nurse.","Exceptions: a critically ill or rapidly declining patient, meningitis (start something that crosses the blood-brain barrier), or severe sepsis — these can't wait on culture results.","Blood cultures: draw two sets, one aerobic bottle and one anaerobic bottle. Always try for a peripheral stick rather than a central line or port — surface bacteria on those devices commonly contaminate the sample. Clean the skin and the bot…","Community- vs. Hospital-Acquired — Community-acquired infections are typically easier to treat and more often viral. Hospital-acquired infections are more often bacterial and more often resistant.","Susceptibility Pattern — What the organism is actually sensitive to, per culture results.","Site of Infection — Can the drug penetrate that specific site — bone, lung, kidney, CNS? Some drugs concentrate in specific tissues better than others.","Suspected Organism — Matching the drug to the likely organism is the number one way to limit resistance.","Reading a Culture","Disk diffusion test: antibiotic disks are placed on a plate smeared with the culture. A clear zone (no bacterial growth) around a disk means that drug is effective against that organism.","Minimum inhibitory concentration (MIC): the lowest concentration of a drug that stops bacterial growth — doesn't necessarily kill it.","Minimum bactericidal concentration (MBC): the lowest concentration that decreases the bacterial colony by 99.9%. Not shown directly on a culture report — it's reported simply as susceptible or resistant."]},{"s":"Patient Factors & Dosing▾","a":"patient-factors-dosing","k":["Patient Factors & Dosing▾","Allergy vs. Adverse Effect","Nausea and vomiting are typically an adverse effect, not a true allergy.","A rash or hives can indicate a true allergy, but doesn't necessarily mean anaphylaxis.","Angioedema or anaphylaxis is a true, life-threatening allergy.","Cross-reactivity: a patient allergic to penicillin may also react to cephalosporins — more likely if the penicillin reaction was anaphylactic rather than just a rash. Sulfa allergy is its own, separate common category.","Allergies can develop at any age, including new penicillin or latex allergies later in life.","Age Extremes — Very young and very old patients typically need lower concentrations — their bodies are less able to handle standard doses.","Renal Function — Impaired kidneys reduce clearance of nephrotoxic drugs — may not tolerate standard dosing.","Hepatic Function — Impaired liver reduces clearance of hepatotoxic drugs.","Site of Infection — An abscess — isolated and walled off — takes longer to treat than a UTI, which a drug can reach more easily.","Host Defenses — Dose and length of treatment also depend on how well the patient's own immune system is fighting the infection.","PEAK after, TROUGH before: draw a peak level about 30 minutes after an IV infusion finishes (peaks are only drawn for IV drugs). Draw a trough level about 30 minutes before the next dose is due."]},{"s":"Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship▾","a":"antimicrobial-resistance-stewardship","k":["Antimicrobial Resistance & Stewardship▾","Why Hospital Patients Are At Higher Risk","Decreased immune response from underlying illness.","Invasive devices break the normal skin barrier: IV lines (central line–associated bloodstream infection), catheters (catheter-associated UTI), G-tubes (abdominal infection).","Post-op risk: atelectasis (collapsed alveoli) increases pneumonia risk; wound dehiscence or evisceration carries a high infection risk. Catheters are also common post-operatively, raising UTI risk.","MRSA — Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus — resistant to methicillin specifically.","VRE — Vancomycin-resistant Enterococcus. Can contaminate surfaces like chairs and clothing, spreading to other patients or family members.","CRE — Carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae — a whole bacterial class resistant to all carbapenems. See the danger callout under Carbapenems below.","MDRO — Multidrug-resistant organism — resistant to multiple drugs across multiple classes.","Gown, after routine patient care — 37%","Gown, after morning care — 65%","Gown, after caring for a patient colonized with MRSA/VRE — 40%","White coat, after examining a patient — 69% (VRE/MRSA)","Bare hands, after that same exam — 27%","C. diff spores can survive 5+ months (reports of years) on surfaces. And on antibiotic overprescribing: for an uncomplicated upper respiratory infection, an antibiotic helps only about 1 in 4,000 times — but causes a reaction (rash, allergy…","Why Antibiotics Fail","Wrong drug chosen for the organism.","Started too late.","Dose too low, or not continued long enough — the infection can appear to improve, then return worse.","The drug can't physically reach the infection site (e.g., doesn't cross the blood-brain barrier for a CNS infection)."]},{"s":"Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors▾","a":"cell-wall-synthesis-inhibitors","k":["Cell Wall Synthesis Inhibitors▾","All of the drugs in this section work by damaging the bacterial cell wall — weakening it so fluid rushes in, the cell swells, and it bursts (cell lysis). Many are beta-lactam drugs, named for a specific ring structure; bacteria fight back b…","\"-bactam\" or \"clavulanic acid\" in a drug name means a beta-lactamase inhibitor is riding along with the actual antibiotic — sulbactam, tazobactam, clavulanic acid, and avibactam are never given alone. Examples: amoxicillin-clavulanate (Augm…","Natural — Penicillin G, penicillin V — Gram-positive coverage; IV; ~30 min half-life (longer with kidney impairment). Allergy to one penicillin means allergy to the whole class.","Penicillinase-Resistant — Nafcillin — Drug of choice when the organism secretes penicillinase (common with staph infections); IV only.","Aminopenicillins — Ampicillin, amoxicillin — One of the first broad-spectrum penicillins. Amoxicillin is preferred orally (often paired with clavulanic acid) and common for pediatric ENT/GI/skin infections; ampicillin resistance is rising.","Extended-Spectrum — Piperacillin + tazobactam (Zosyn) — Broadest-spectrum penicillin, common in the ICU; covers pseudomonas. Watch platelet function and renal status.","1st — Cefazolin (IV), cephalexin/Keflex (PO) — Gram-positive/staph coverage — not enterococcus or strep. Common for skin infections, UTIs, and surgical prophylaxis (cefazolin). Minimal CNS penetration.","2nd — Cefuroxime, cefotetan, cefoxitin — More gram-negative activity while keeping some gram-positive coverage. Cefotetan/cefoxitin-type drugs work on intestinal/anaerobic bacteria. Not for pseudomonas or CNS infections.","3rd — Ceftriaxone, ceftazidime — Ceftriaxone: once-daily dosing, crosses the blood-brain barrier (meningitis) — use caution with liver dysfunction. Ceftazidime covers pseudomonas.","4th — Cefepime — Broad spectrum — gram-negative and gram-positive, crosses the blood-brain barrier, covers pseudomonas. Often the first choice for a very sick patient with an unclear source.","5th — Ceftaroline — Newest, broadest generation; covers multidrug-resistant staph, but not enterobacter, pseudomonas, ESBL, or Klebsiella. IV only, renally dosed.","Cross-reactivity with a penicillin allergy is rare, about 1–4% — but still avoided when the penicillin reaction was anaphylactic. Cephalosporins are pregnancy category B (considered safe). Common adverse effects across all generations: mild…","Imipenem-Cilastatin — Broadest-spectrum antibiotic class. Cilastatin blocks a kidney enzyme (dehydropeptidase) that would otherwise break imipenem down too fast; crosses the blood-brain barrier.","Meropenem — Slightly less coverage than imipenem, but doesn't need cilastatin — used more often when possible for its lower seizure risk. Can cause GI upset.","Ertapenem and doripenem also exist but aren't emphasized here. All carbapenems are IV only, infused over 60 minutes, and saved as a last resort to limit resistance.","Carbapenems & CRE: carbapenems carry a real risk of drug-induced seizures — watch closely in patients with a seizure history. When bacteria become carbapenem-resistant (CRE), it's a public health emergency: carbapenems are the broadest-spec…","Vancomycin","A glycopeptide antibiotic — kills the gram-positive cell wall in a different way than the beta-lactams (not autolysis). Good for MRSA and other drug-resistant gram-positive infections.","Oral vancomycin only works locally in the gut — used for C. diff/pseudomembranous colitis, not systemic infections.","Toxic effects: nephrotoxic (monitor renal levels on IV vancomycin), can cause reversible hearing loss and thrombocytopenia (monitor platelets). Extra caution alongside other nephrotoxic drugs (e.g., IV contrast) or neuromuscular blockers.","Red Man syndrome: flushing, itching, rash — sometimes tachycardia/hypotension — from infusing it too fast. Fix by slowing the infusion, not stopping the drug; can premedicate with Benadryl.","Peak/trough monitoring: trough about 30 minutes before the next dose; peak about 30 minutes after the infusion finishes."]},{"s":"Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors▾","a":"protein-dna-rna-synthesis-inhibitors","k":["Protein & DNA/RNA Synthesis Inhibitors▾","These drugs don't touch the cell wall — instead they interfere with transcription or translation, so the bacteria can't build proteins or copy its DNA. That either kills the cell or stops it from reproducing.","Class name patterns: \"-mycin/-micin\" = aminoglycosides (gentamicin, tobramycin). \"-floxacin\" = fluoroquinolones (ciprofloxacin, levofloxacin). \"-cycline\" = tetracyclines (tetracycline, doxycycline, minocycline).","Aminoglycosides — Gentamicin, Tobramycin","Potent, especially against gram-negative organisms; used for complicated or resistant infections — UTIs, pyelonephritis, endocarditis, pneumonia, osteomyelitis.","Two major toxicities to monitor: nephrotoxicity (usually reversible — monitor creatinine) and ototoxicity (can be permanent — ask about ringing or buzzing in the ears).","Peak and trough levels are drawn; dosing is adjusted for renal function.","Often combined with a beta-lactam or vancomycin for a synergistic effect — give the beta-lactamase inhibitor first so the aminoglycoside can get into the bacterial cell.","Gentamicin plus a neuromuscular blocker (paralytic) can cause profound respiratory distress. Also watch for CNS effects (confusion, disorientation, numbness/tingling) and permanent cochlear damage.","Lincosamides — Clindamycin","Bactericidal or bacteriostatic depending on concentration; binds ribosomes to stop protein synthesis.","Used for anaerobic infections — bone, GU, intra-abdominal, aspiration pneumonia. All Enterobacteriaceae are resistant to it.","PO or IV; monitored with peak/trough levels; same neuromuscular-blocker/respiratory-distress caution as gentamicin.","Notable risk: C. diff → pseudomembranous colitis. Watch for diarrhea, bloody stool, or severe GI distress.","Macrolides are bacteriostatic in general (bactericidal at high concentrations) and inhibit protein synthesis via ribosomes, like the aminoglycosides. Used for STIs (especially gonorrhea), respiratory and skin infections, and some unusual or…","Erythromycin — Significant GI upset (nicknamed a \"yuck drug\") — that same effect is actually useful for diabetic gastroparesis, since it increases gastric motility. Doesn't cross the blood-brain barrier; painful IV; poor oral absorption, do…","Azithromycin (Z-Pak) — Less GI upset than erythromycin; better tissue penetration and longer duration allow once-daily dosing (2 tablets day one, then 1 daily). Take on an empty stomach — food decreases absorption.","Tetracycline — PO only; take fasting (75–77% absorbed — food reduces this further). Concentrates in bone, liver, spleen, and teeth.","Doxycycline — Common for STIs, acne, and other skin infections.","Minocycline — Used for meningitis and rheumatoid arthritis; there's an extended-release form for acne.","All tetracyclines are bacteriostatic, inhibit protein synthesis by binding ribosomes, and are broad spectrum despite growing resistance. They still work on some unusual organisms: Rickettsia (Rocky Mountain spotted fever), chlamydia, tricho…","Tetracyclines & pregnancy/pediatrics: never give to pregnant or nursing women, or to children under 8. They cause enamel hypoplasia — permanent discoloration of developing teeth.","Ciprofloxacin (Cipro) — Used for complicated UTIs, some STIs, respiratory infections, gonorrhea, and anthrax. Can be given PO, IV, or topically; poor CNS penetration — avoid for meningitis.","Levofloxacin (Levaquin) — Most widely used fluoroquinolone; once daily, 100% oral bioavailability. Good for sinusitis, chronic bronchitis flares, community-acquired pneumonia. Can prolong the QT interval and lower the seizure threshold; wat…","Fluoroquinolones work by altering bacterial DNA (via the enzymes gyrase and topoisomerase) rather than inhibiting protein synthesis — effective against both rapid- and slow-growing organisms, with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect. They co…","Sulfonamides — Sulfamethoxazole-Trimethoprim (Bactrim)","Bacteriostatic; blocks folic acid synthesis.","First-line for uncomplicated UTIs and respiratory infections; also used for salmonella and shigellosis.","Often given to patients with HIV, who have a higher rate of adverse reactions.","Contains a sulfa component — contraindicated with a sulfa allergy. Reactions typically start with fever, then a skin rash.","Metronidazole (Flagyl)","Works against both bacterial and protozoal infections: amebiasis, giardiasis, trichomoniasis, intra-abdominal infections, H. pylori, Crohn's-related infections.","The go-to drug (with oral vancomycin) for C. diff/antibiotic-associated diarrhea. Only works against anaerobic bacteria.","Works by inhibiting DNA synthesis, similar to the fluoroquinolones.","Other adverse effects seen across most antibiotics: nausea/vomiting, xerostomia (dry mouth), vaginal candidiasis (yeast infection).","Metronidazole (Flagyl) & alcohol: no alcohol — none for 24 hours before starting and at least 36 hours after the last dose. Mixing the two creates a toxic metabolite (a disulfiram-like reaction)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-antivirals.html","t":"Antivirals","secs":[{"s":"Antivirals","a":"","k":["Week 1","Antiviral Pharmacology Basics","Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir","Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)","Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Antiviral Pharmacology Basics▾","a":"antiviral-pharmacology-basics","k":["Antiviral Pharmacology Basics▾","This lecture covers non-HIV antivirals. HIV gets its own antiviral content on the HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy page. Antivirals are a relatively new drug class — the first ones came out in the 1990s — and there are far fewer of them than th…","Why There Are So Few Antivirals","A virus can only replicate once it's inside a living host cell — so for an antiviral to work, it has to get inside the patient's own cells too.","Viruses don't have a cell wall of their own (unlike bacteria, which is what most antibiotics target). A virus lives by inserting its RNA or DNA into a healthy human cell and replicating from inside it.","Because the virus lives inside our own cells, a drug that kills the virus risks killing healthy cells along with it — a much harder balance than antibiotics have, since bacterial cells are a separate target.","Most antivirals only work during the replication phase, and signs/symptoms often don't show up until the virus has already finished replicating — so there's a narrow window where the drug can actually do anything.","Antiviral names end in \"-vir\": acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir, ganciclovir, oseltamivir — all covered on this page.","General mechanism: antivirals work by inhibiting viral replication, which then allows the body's own immune system to clear the virus. Antivirals are only effective against a handful of viruses.","Herpes Simplex / Varicella-Zoster — Oral herpes, genital herpes, shingles (herpes zoster), chickenpox/varicella — Acyclovir, valacyclovir, famciclovir — this page","Influenza A (some B) — The flu — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) — this page","Cytomegalovirus (CMV) — Concerning only in immunocompromised patients — Ganciclovir — this page","RSV — A common virus in children — Named in lecture, no specific drug given","Hepatitis — Viral hepatitis — Covered in the hepatitis lecture","HIV — — — See HIV & ART"]},{"s":"Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir▾","a":"herpes-simplex-varicella-zoster-acyclovir-valacyclovir-famciclovir","k":["Herpes Simplex & Varicella-Zoster — Acyclovir, Valacyclovir & Famciclovir▾","All three drugs work the same way and are in the same class. The lecture focused on acyclovir specifically — valacyclovir and famciclovir are alternatives in the same family.","Acyclovir — Three Mechanisms of Action","Interferes with viral nucleic acid synthesis — stops viral DNA and RNA synthesis.","Prevents the virus from binding to the cell, so it can't get in and can't replicate further.","Turns on the body's own immune system to help kill the virus.","Indications — Oral herpes, genital herpes, shingles (herpes zoster), chickenpox/varicella — all caused by the same virus family.","Routes — Oral tablet, liquid, topical ointment, and IV.","Not a Cure — Decreases the severity and frequency of outbreaks, reduces viral shedding, and decreases the duration of illness — but doesn't eliminate the virus. It stays dormant in the body.","Treatment Pattern — Used for both the initial infection and recurrent outbreaks — often prescribed with refills, since outbreaks can reoccur and need repeat courses.","Side Effects — GI distress, renal impairment, seizures. Use cautiously in patients with ITP (idiopathic thrombocytopenia) — a low starting platelet count.","IV acyclovir: can cause tissue necrosis — make sure the IV line is very patent, or use a central line.","Epidemiology asides the instructor flagged as not heavily tested: about 1% of the population has genital herpes, and it's about twice as common in women as in men. HSV-1 generally occurs above the waist, HSV-2 below the waist. Herpes zoster…","There is a shingles vaccine (Shingrix) — a live vaccine given to people age 50 and older to help prevent shingles. It reduces the risk of shingles and decreases the frequency and severity of outbreaks if it does occur."]},{"s":"Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)▾","a":"influenza-oseltamivir-tamiflu","k":["Influenza — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu)▾","Target — Best against influenza A; does have some action against influenza B. Also approved for the swine flu.","Mechanism — Inhibits neuraminidase, an enzyme flu viruses create to replicate — stopping viral replication.","Timing — Must be started before or within 48 hours of symptom onset.","Use Pattern — Primarily used for prophylaxis after exposure rather than treating active disease — typically reserved for elderly and immunocompromised patients after exposure, to avoid driving flu resistance to the drug.","Route — PO only.","Side Effects — Nausea, vomiting, seizures, renal impairment.","Once a patient is sick enough to be hospitalized for the flu, oseltamivir may still be tried, but if it's outside that 48-hour window it's unlikely to help — the virus has usually already finished replicating by the time someone is that sic…"]},{"s":"Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir▾","a":"cytomegalovirus-ganciclovir","k":["Cytomegalovirus — Ganciclovir▾","Treats — CMV (cytomegalovirus).","Population — Only a concern in immunocompromised patients — transplant recipients and immunosuppressed/AIDS patients. Not a concern for the general population.","Not a Cure — CMV can't be cured. Affected patients have recurrent infections.","CMV Retinitis — CMV infection in the eye — treated with an intraocular (into-the-eye) injection of ganciclovir.","Other Presentation — In other immunocompromised patients, CMV can present as a respiratory infection.","Mechanism — Inhibits viral DNA polymerase, causing chain termination — stops viral replication.","Routes — IV or PO. Usually started IV for a few weeks, then transitioned to an oral tablet.","PO Handling — Don't crush or split the tablet — it can irritate the skin. If it does get on bare skin, wash with soap and water.","Ganciclovir — four black-box warnings: hematologic toxicity (can cause pancytopenia — decreased red cells, white cells, and platelets; monitor labs), fertility impairment (in both men and women of childbearing age), fetal toxicity and birth…","Ganciclovir + imipenem: avoid combining — increased seizure risk. Imipenem's carbapenem seizure risk is covered on the ABX page","Ganciclovir is cleared through the kidneys — use caution with other nephrotoxic drugs and watch renal function."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-hiv.html","t":"HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","secs":[{"s":"HIV & Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","a":"","k":["Week 1","HIV & AIDS — the Basics","How HIV Attacks the Immune System","Stages of HIV Infection","AIDS-Defining Criteria","Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia","Transmission & Risk","Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)","PrEP & PEP","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"HIV & AIDS — the Basics▾","a":"hiv-aids-the-basics","k":["HIV & AIDS — the Basics▾","HIV — Human Immunodeficiency Virus — a retrovirus that invades the body and destroys CD4 T cells.","Retrovirus — A virus that carries only RNA (no DNA) and uses reverse transcriptase to make a DNA copy of itself inside a host cell.","HIV-1 — The type seen in U.S. practice. Discovered first; still the most prevalent type worldwide.","HIV-2 — A mutated, less pathogenic version — confined largely to West Africa, not commonly seen in the U.S.","AIDS — Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome, caused by HIV. Everyone with AIDS has HIV, but not everyone with HIV has AIDS — it develops from an untreated HIV infection or from severe immune dysfunction that builds up over years.","Who's Most Affected in the US","About 1.1 million people are newly infected annually in the U.S., and that number has been trending up.","South Africa has the highest HIV prevalence in the world — it's believed to be where the virus originated.","76% of adults and adolescents with HIV in the U.S. are men.","Black men have the highest rates of new HIV infections; Black men who have sex with men account for the most new and existing infections. (\"Men who have sex with men,\" or MSM, is the clinical term used here — it describes risk by behavior r…","New cases are also increasing among women — risk assessment shouldn't discount any group, especially in school health or other preventive-care settings."]},{"s":"How HIV Attacks the Immune System▾","a":"how-hiv-attacks-the-immune-system","k":["How HIV Attacks the Immune System▾","The HIV Life Cycle","The virus binds to the CD4 receptor on a host cell — mainly T lymphocytes, though it can also attack monocytes and macrophages.","It fuses with the cell membrane and enters the cell.","Inside, it releases reverse transcriptase, which makes a DNA copy of the virus's own RNA.","That new viral DNA is inserted into the host cell's own genetic material by another enzyme, HIV integrase.","The infected cell's machinery is hijacked to direct the synthesis and assembly of more virus.","New viral copies are released to go infect more CD4 cells, and the cycle repeats.","CD4 T cells are responsible for cell-mediated immunity — the body's long-term immune \"memory.\" Once HIV has taken over enough of them, that memory function is gone and the immune system collapses, leaving the person vulnerable to opportunis…","Because the viral DNA becomes part of the host cell's own DNA, a person is infected for life — HIV cannot be fully cleared, only suppressed to very low levels. The body does form anti-HIV antibodies, which is how infection is detected on a …"]},{"s":"Stages of HIV Infection▾","a":"stages-of-hiv-infection","k":["Stages of HIV Infection▾","Acute (Early) Infection — Rapid viral replication turns CD4 cells into \"HIV factories.\" About 40–60% of infected people don't notice or can't identify symptoms, since they're nonspecific. The person is infectious from day one — even before …","Clinical Latency (Chronic) — Viral levels stabilize as the body suppresses the \"factories\"; some healthy CD4 cells remain. Asymptomatic or only mildly symptomatic, with or without treatment. Untreated, this stage lasts about 3–12 years; wit…","Symptomatic HIV / AIDS — CD4 count continues a persistent decline over time until the bone marrow can no longer keep up, eventually reaching AIDS-defining criteria.","CD4 count and viral load move inversely: a higher CD4 count means a healthier immune system, a lower viral load means less circulating virus, and as one drops the other tends to rise. In a treated patient, viral load can stay very low or un…","Symptoms during acute infection (or as the disease progresses) look a lot like the flu or COVID: fever, sore throat, swollen lymph nodes, rash, muscle aches, night sweats, mouth sores, chills, and fatigue. Because they're so nonspecific, te…"]},{"s":"AIDS-Defining Criteria▾","a":"aids-defining-criteria","k":["AIDS-Defining Criteria▾","A diagnosis becomes AIDS, rather than just HIV, either way: a CD4 count under 200 — regardless of symptoms — or the presence of an AIDS-defining condition, regardless of CD4 count.","AIDS-Defining Conditions to Memorize","Kaposi sarcoma","Wasting syndrome","Invasive (pervasive) candidiasis","Pneumocystis pneumonia (PCP)","Tuberculosis (TB)","Invasive cervical cancer","HIV-related encephalopathy / AIDS dementia complex"]},{"s":"Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia▾","a":"oral-manifestations-hiv-associated-dementia","k":["Oral Manifestations & HIV-Associated Dementia▾","Oral Manifestations — Fungal, viral, bacterial, or cancerous oral problems appear as CD4 counts begin to drop — an early sign the immune system is struggling and a possible cue to reassess the treatment regimen. Includes oral hairy leukopla…","HIV-Associated Dementia — Also called AIDS dementia complex — cognitive symptoms of dementia. Historically affected about 40–60% of patients; far less common now with modern treatment. Thought to occur because HIV crosses the blood-brain ba…"]},{"s":"Transmission & Risk▾","a":"transmission-risk","k":["Transmission & Risk▾","Anal or Vaginal Sex, No Condom — High risk if one partner is HIV-positive.","Oral Sex — Very low to no risk.","Perinatal (Mother to Baby) — About 25–30% if the mother is untreated; less than 2% if she's treated.","Shared Injection Equipment — High risk — IV drug use, dirty needles.","Blood Transfusion / Organ Transplant — Possible, but rare now given current screening.","Not Spread By","Sharing a toilet seat","Hugging","Kissing or spit","Other casual, non-fluid contact","Highest-risk group: men who have sex with men. Other major risk factors include injection drug use, unprotected heterosexual contact, perinatal exposure, and blood exposure. Transmission risk climbs with more frequent or longer contact, and…","Face/Skin Splash With Body Fluid — Essentially zero risk, even if blood is present.","Mucous Membrane or Skin Contact — Very low risk, even with blood involved.","Needle Stick — Still under 1%.","Regardless of how low the risk, any exposure still requires completing exposure paperwork/reporting, and post-exposure prophylaxis may be offered depending on the risk level."]},{"s":"Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)▾","a":"antiretroviral-therapy-art","k":["Antiretroviral Therapy (ART)▾","The instructor was explicit here: individual drug names aren't required for the drug matrix, but exam questions will cover the generalizations and principles below — this is a fast-moving treatment area.","Fusion Inhibitors — Block the virus from entering the cell.","CCR5 Antagonists — Also block viral entry, via the CCR5 co-receptor protein.","NRTIs — Nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors — inhibit reverse transcriptase.","NNRTIs — Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors — also inhibit reverse transcriptase, working similarly to NRTIs.","Protease Inhibitors — Inhibit HIV protease.","Integrase Inhibitors — Inhibit HIV integrase.","Drug class matches the step it blocks: fusion inhibitors and CCR5 antagonists stop the virus from getting in; NRTIs/NNRTIs, protease inhibitors, and integrase inhibitors each block one of the enzymes from the life cycle above (reverse trans…","A typical ART regimen is three drugs: two NRTIs plus a third agent from a different class.","NRTI Mechanism — Inhibits reverse transcriptase, blocking HIV's ability to incorporate its RNA into the host cell's DNA.","NRTI Adverse Effects — Peripheral neuropathy, pancreatitis, lipoatrophy, and hepatic steatosis (liver effects).","NRTIs — black-box warning: lactic acidosis. Lactate levels are monitored, especially during the first couple of months after starting treatment.","ART overall doesn't have many drug-drug interactions, which is a plus — but a three-drug regimen is still a lot for a patient to manage.","Treatment Principles","Start therapy as soon as possible after diagnosis.","The goal is to decrease viral load to an undetectable level, which gives the immune system a chance to recover and produce healthy, uninfected CD4 cells.","Effectiveness is monitored with CD4 count and viral load.","An undetectable viral load means effectively no risk of transmitting HIV to others — but the person is still considered HIV-positive."]},{"s":"PrEP & PEP▾","a":"prep-pep","k":["PrEP & PEP▾","PrEP (Pre-Exposure Prophylaxis) — Ongoing antiretroviral medication (typically a combination pill) taken by an HIV-negative person in an ongoing relationship with an HIV-positive partner, to prevent acquiring HIV. Reduces transmission risk …","PEP (Post-Exposure Prophylaxis) — Antiretroviral therapy started after a known or suspected high-risk exposure (a needle stick, unprotected sex with someone found to be HIV-positive, a high-risk healthcare exposure). Given for 28 days, with…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-uri.html","t":"Upper Respiratory Infections (URI)","secs":[{"s":"Upper Respiratory Infections (URI)","a":"","k":["Week 2","Sputum & Airway Basics","Bronchitis & Influenza","Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis","Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup","Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm","Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)","Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Sputum & Airway Basics▾","a":"sputum-airway-basics","k":["Sputum & Airway Basics▾","Sputum is mucus secreted from the respiratory tract and coughed up. Cilia normally move mucus — and whatever particles it traps — out of the airway through coughing. Sputum can be cultured to check for infection, but its color alone isn't d…"]},{"s":"Bronchitis & Influenza▾","a":"bronchitis-influenza","k":["Bronchitis & Influenza▾","Acute (Simple) Bronchitis","Inflammation of the bronchi and bronchioles — not down at the alveolar level, so there's no airflow obstruction.","About 80% viral; can be bacterial.","Usually mild and self-limiting; lasts about 3–4 weeks.","Symptoms: sore throat, runny nose, muscle aches, cough (~3 weeks), possible wheezing (not always present), productive cough, possible swollen lymph nodes.","Diagnosed by symptoms and physical exam; a CBC with diff is sometimes used if there's concern about progression (age, comorbidities).","Treatment: antibiotics if bacterial is suspected; otherwise supportive care — hydration, a cough suppressant, an expectorant.","Chronic Bronchitis","Bronchitis symptoms for at least 3 months of the year, for at least 2 consecutive years.","Usually related to cigarette smoking.","Unlike acute bronchitis, it does involve airflow obstruction.","Presents as exacerbations when it's active.","Chronic bronchitis is covered in full — pathophysiology, complications, and management — on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page","Influenza — Viral; types A and B are most common, and the virus can mutate. The vaccine composition changes yearly based on predictions of which strains are likely — not always accurate, but still worthwhile.","Vaccine Benefit — Doesn't necessarily prevent infection, but reduces the risk of severe illness and death by about 36%.","Onset — Rapid — fever, chills, body aches, feeling awful all at once.","Serious Complication — Secondary bacterial pneumonia (the flu virus damages the lungs' defenses, letting bacteria move in) — a major reason the flu vaccine is emphasized in elderly and comorbid patients."]},{"s":"Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis▾","a":"histamine-allergic-rhinitis-sinusitis","k":["Histamine, Allergic Rhinitis & Sinusitis▾","What Histamine Does","Stored in mast cells (skin and soft tissue) and basophils (blood).","Skin: hives and itching.","Blood vessels: vasodilation (redness); if released systemically, can cause hypotension.","Airways: bronchoconstriction — shortness of breath.","Also affects sleep-wake cycles and increases stomach acid secretion.","Allergic Rhinitis — Inflammation and swelling of the nasal mucous membranes from an IgE-mediated histamine release, triggered by environmental allergens. Affects the upper and lower airways and the eyes. Symptoms: sneezing, runny nose, itch…","Sinusitis — Infection/inflammation of the facial sinuses — the cavity gets obstructed by fluid and edema, which lets bacteria grow. Often accompanies other URIs; more likely with nasal polyps, a deviated septum, or an allergic reaction. Vir…"]},{"s":"Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup▾","a":"pharyngitis-tonsillitis-laryngitis-croup","k":["Pharyngitis, Tonsillitis & Laryngitis/Croup▾","Pharyngitis — Inflammation of the palate, tonsils, and uvula (back of the throat). The key question is whether it's bacterial (strep) or viral — bacterial pharyngitis often shows white spots/exudate on the throat and tonsils, while viral ty…","Tonsillitis — The tonsils become so swollen they're nearly touching — a very painful sore throat with difficulty swallowing. Recurrent or severe cases sometimes lead to tonsil removal.","Laryngitis — Inflammation of the vocal cords/larynx.","Laryngotracheobronchitis (Croup) — Inflammation of the larynx, trachea, and bronchi together — produces the classic \"barking\" cough."]},{"s":"Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm▾","a":"epiglottitis-bronchospasm","k":["Epiglottitis & Bronchospasm▾","Epiglottitis: rare but potentially life-threatening — a swollen epiglottis can block the windpipe without timely treatment. Most common in children but can occur in adults. Rapid onset of fever, pain, and difficulty swallowing, plus droolin…","Bronchospasm: sudden constriction of the bronchial muscles that makes breathing difficult. Can be triggered by a history of asthma or by an active URI — patients being ventilated, recovering from anesthesia, or with a history of airway issu…","Bronchodilator and anticholinergic drug detail — mechanisms, dosing, and side effects — is covered in full on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page"]},{"s":"Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)▾","a":"antihistamines-h1-blockers","k":["Antihistamines (H1 Blockers)▾","Histamine has two receptor types: H1 receptors mediate smooth muscle contraction and capillary dilation — the target of antihistamines. H2 receptors mediate heart rate and gastric acid secretion — the target of H2 blockers, covered separate…","First-Generation (Sedating)","Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) is the main one — the one that belongs on the drug matrix. Also meclizine, promethazine (motion sickness/nausea), and dimenhydrinate (Dramamine, motion sickness) — these three don't need to go on the drug matrix.","Used for mild allergic reactions, motion sickness, and insomnia; can also treat/prevent severe anaphylactic reactions.","Given PO (common OTC) or IV.","Significant CNS depression — drowsiness, dizziness (the \"Benadryl hangover\"). Can cause paradoxical hyperactivity, especially in children.","Mild anticholinergic effect — dries up secretions (helps the runny nose) but can also cause constipation and urinary retention.","Second-Generation (Non-Sedating)","Loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine — all of these belong on the drug matrix.","Same indications as first-generation, but fewer side effects and much less drowsiness — can be taken in the morning.","Still some CNS effect possible at higher doses.","Antihistamine Cautions","Closed-angle glaucoma, cardiac disease, kidney disease/uncontrolled hypertension, peptic ulcer disease, seizures, BPH, and pregnancy — use carefully given the anticholinergic effect and the potential to raise blood pressure.","Nursing: monitor for dizziness with ambulation, monitor for urinary retention, and avoid driving or other tasks that need mental alertness — consider dosing a sedating antihistamine at night.","What actually needs to go on the drug matrix from this lecture: diphenhydramine, all three second-generation antihistamines (loratadine, fexofenadine, cetirizine), both decongestants (phenylephrine, pseudoephedrine), and both cough suppress…"]},{"s":"Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics▾","a":"decongestants-cough-suppressants-expectorants-mucolytics","k":["Decongestants, Cough Suppressants & Expectorants/Mucolytics▾","Decongestants — Phenylephrine and pseudoephedrine — sympathomimetics that activate alpha receptors, causing vasoconstriction that shrinks nasal blood vessels and opens the nasal passages. Side effects come from that sympathetic/CNS stimulat…","Cough Suppressants (Antitussives) — Dextromethorphan and benzonatate (Tessalon Perles) — both suppress the cough reflex directly in the brain. Coughing is usually a beneficial, protective response, so these are reserved for when it isn't — …","Expectorants — Guaifenesin (Mucinex) reduces the surface tension of secretions to make mucus easier to cough up — it doesn't actually decrease mucus production, and its effectiveness is debated. Encourage hydration alongside it. Minimal sid…","Mucolytics — Acetylcysteine decreases mucus viscosity, used in chronic pulmonary conditions such as cystic fibrosis (it's also the antidote for acetaminophen overdose — a different indication). Has a strong, rotten-egg smell. Carries a bron…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-obstructive-airway-disorders.html","t":"Obstructive Airway Disorders","secs":[{"s":"Obstructive Airway Disorders","a":"","k":["Week 2","Obstructive Disease Basics","Asthma","Chronic Bronchitis","Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella","Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists","Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives","Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Obstructive Disease Basics▾","a":"obstructive-disease-basics","k":["Obstructive Disease Basics▾","Why Air Gets Trapped","Narrowed airways make obstruction worse on expiration — air gets trapped in the lungs because it can't get out.","This increases the work of breathing and slows lung emptying, measured with FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in 1 second) on pulmonary function tests (PFTs).","All obstructive diseases cause a V/Q (ventilation-perfusion) mismatch — air and blood in the lungs aren't matched up well, so blood isn't oxygenated properly, leading to hypoxemia.","Trapped air also means less effective ventilation, so CO2 builds up — hypoventilation and hypercapnia.","Assessed with an ABG or VBG, exhaled CO2/capnography, and SpO2.","Dyspnea and wheezing (from inflamed airways) are hallmark findings across all three obstructive diseases. Hyperinflation from air trapping shows up on a chest X-ray."]},{"s":"Asthma▾","a":"asthma","k":["Asthma▾","Definition — Chronic inflammation of the bronchial airways — not the alveoli. That's what separates asthma from chronic bronchitis and emphysema.","Airflow Obstruction — Bronchial hyperresponsiveness causes bronchoconstriction and variable airflow obstruction — but unlike bronchitis and emphysema, it's mostly reversible.","Risk Factors — Usually a childhood-onset disease; strongly linked to allergies and a significant familial component (100+ implicated genes). Also: heavy allergen exposure, urban living, indoor/outdoor air pollution, tobacco smoke exposure, …","Common Triggers — Exercise/exertion is the most common trigger, plus secondhand smoke, climate, dust mites, pet dander, and pollen.","Asthma Pathophysiology, Step by Step","Exposure to a trigger/antigen activates an intense immune response — mast cells, eosinophils, neutrophils, basophils, T-helper cells, and B lymphocytes are all recruited.","This causes bronchial airway inflammation.","Inflammation leads to mucus hypersecretion, smooth muscle constriction (narrowing the airway), and swelling.","Result: wheezing, cough, shortness of breath, chest tightness, and an increased work of breathing.","There's an early and a late asthmatic response. The early response is immediate — inflammatory mediators cause vasodilation, capillary permeability, edema, bronchoconstriction, and mucus. A late response can follow about 48 hours later, as …","Repeated, untreated exacerbations lead to airway remodeling — irreversible long-term damage that becomes chronic asthma. Bronchoconstriction is what causes the acute dyspnea, but inflammation is what drives the long-term severity and remode…","Diagnosis — History (childhood allergies, wheezing episodes, exercise intolerance) plus PFTs — the gold standard. Look for a decreased expiratory flow rate and decreased FEV1. Often mistaken for heart failure, cystic fibrosis, or vocal cord…","Classic Symptoms — Wheezing, dyspnea, cough, chest tightness.","Severe Attack — Accessory muscle use, distant or decreased/absent breath sounds, diaphoresis, inability to speak — can progress to a silent chest and respiratory failure. Asthma attacks can be fatal.","Asthma Management Overview","Avoid known triggers.","Monitor with a peak flow meter (tracks FEV1 at home) and an asthma action plan built around green/yellow/red zones based on the patient's personal best.","PO corticosteroids are the mainstay for exacerbations; a short-acting beta agonist inhaler covers milder forms.","More severe disease adds anti-inflammatory therapy — inhaled corticosteroids, long-acting beta agonists, or leukotriene antagonists (full drug detail in the Pharmacology sections below).","Immunotherapy — allergy shots or monoclonal antibody therapy — is an option too.","Status asthmaticus: an unrelenting asthma attack — a life-threatening emergency. The patient develops a silent chest (no air movement at all), and an ABG/VBG shows CO2 greater than 70. Often requires IV epinephrine."]},{"s":"Chronic Bronchitis▾","a":"chronic-bronchitis","k":["Chronic Bronchitis▾","Definition — Hypersecretion of mucus with a chronic productive cough, present for at least 3 months of the year for at least 2 consecutive years — typically worse in winter. Timing is what makes the diagnosis.","Cause — About 90% of patients with chronic bronchitis smoke cigarettes. Smoking and vaping together carry a 6x greater risk.","Airflow Obstruction — Present — unlike acute/simple bronchitis. This is what classifies chronic bronchitis as a form of COPD.","Hallmark Sign — A persistent, productive cough — purulent if a respiratory infection is also present. These patients are more prone to respiratory infections because the mucus becomes a breeding ground.","Diagnosis — History, physical exam, chest imaging, and PFTs — decreased expiratory volume from the airflow obstruction.","Reversibility — By the time most patients seek treatment, the airway changes are usually irreversible.","Chronic Bronchitis Pathophysiology, Step by Step","Inhaled irritants cause airway inflammation and white blood cell infiltration of the bronchial walls.","This drives mucus production, inflammation, and bronchial edema.","Goblet cells respond by getting both bigger and more numerous (hyperplasia) — producing more thick, tenacious mucus.","Smoking damages ciliary function, so that mucus can't be cleared effectively.","The accumulation leads to chronic bronchospasm and, over time, pulmonary fibrosis.","Late in the disease, this can progress to pulmonary hypertension — causing syncope, dyspnea, and fatigue — and eventually right-sided heart failure, also called cor pulmonale.","Smoking cessation can halt progression, but it doesn't reverse or cure the damage already done. Management includes bronchodilators, mucus thinners/expectorants, prophylactic antibiotics during certain times of year, chest physiotherapy, an…"]},{"s":"Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella▾","a":"emphysema-the-copd-umbrella","k":["Emphysema & the COPD Umbrella▾","Definition — Permanent enlargement of the airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, with destruction of the alveolar walls.","Cause of Obstruction — Inflammation and loss of elastic recoil — not mucus production, which is what makes chronic bronchitis different.","Mechanism — An imbalance between proteases/antiproteases, plus oxidative stress and apoptosis, breaks down elastin and destroys alveoli — creating blebs/bullae that can't perform gas exchange.","Major Risk Factors — Smoking is the primary cause, plus secondhand smoke, air pollution, and frequent childhood respiratory infections.","Genetic Form — Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency — under 2% of cases. Suspect this in a patient with emphysema who has never smoked.","Diagnosis — PFTs (decreased FEV1); chest X-ray (hyperinflated lungs); ABG (respiratory acidosis — high CO2, low pH); alpha-1 antitrypsin level if there's no smoking history.","Classic Presentation","Gradually increasing dyspnea on exertion, eventually progressing to dyspnea at rest, with a prolonged expiratory phase. Often oxygen-dependent.","Malnutrition and muscle wasting — breathing becomes the body's whole priority, so other needs get pushed aside.","Barrel chest (a roughly 1:1 AP diameter) from chronic hyperinflation.","Pursed-lip breathing — provides positive pressure that helps keep airways open.","Quiet, diminished breath sounds are the hallmark finding.","\"Blue Bloater\" — Chronic Bronchitis","Tends to be more overweight","More cyanotic","More edema","More wheezing","May have an elevated hemoglobin","More hypoxic overall","\"Pink Puffer\" — Emphysema","Tends to be more malnourished/thin","Quiet, diminished breath sounds","Pursed-lip breathing","Barrel chest","More hypercapnic and dyspneic overall","COPD (chronic obstructive pulmonary disease) is the umbrella term for chronic bronchitis and emphysema together — both cause trouble getting air out.","Management: smoking cessation, bronchodilators, anti-inflammatory agents, home oxygen, and breathing/relaxation techniques. Infection prevention matters a lot here — early broad-spectrum antibiotics are often started at the first sign of in…"]},{"s":"Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists▾","a":"bronchodilators-beta-agonists","k":["Bronchodilators — Beta Agonists▾","Two big pharmacology classes treat these three diseases: bronchodilators (relax bronchial smooth muscle) and anti-inflammatories (reduce bronchial inflammation). Either class can be used across asthma, chronic bronchitis, or emphysema, thou…","Short-Acting (SABA)","Albuterol, levalbuterol.","Rescue drugs — onset within minutes, lasting about 4–6 hours; given every 4–6 hours.","First-line treatment for an acute asthma attack; treats acute bronchospasm, wheezing, chest tightness, and shortness of breath from asthma, chronic bronchitis, or emphysema.","Using more than one canister a month signals inadequate asthma control and may mean adding anti-inflammatory therapy (there are about 200 puffs per inhaler).","Also used prophylactically before exercise for exercise-induced asthma — but not recommended for regular daily use.","Long-Acting (LABA)","Salmeterol, formoterol.","Preventer/maintenance drugs — will not help an acute attack. Lasts 12–24 hours, usually dosed twice daily (often by DPI).","Always given combined with an inhaled corticosteroid — never alone.","Historically linked to increased asthma-related deaths, more so in Black and African American patients — used to carry a black-box warning, later removed after further study.","Used for moderate-to-severe asthma or worsening COPD.","Selective (Beta-2) — Albuterol, salmeterol — act only on beta-2 receptors in the lungs. This is what's preferred for pulmonary conditions, since it limits systemic effects.","Non-Selective — Epinephrine, metaproterenol — also stimulate beta-1 (cardiac effects: increased HR and BP) and alpha receptors (vasoconstriction, decreased mucosal edema/secretions). More systemic side effects overall.","Beta Agonist Cautions & Side Effects","Use cautiously with uncontrolled hypertension, cardiac rhythm problems, or high stroke risk.","Avoid combining with MAOIs or other sympathomimetics (ephedrine, pseudoephedrine) — risk of hypertension.","Can raise blood glucose — patients may need more insulin during frequent or exacerbation-driven use.","Common side effects: insomnia, restlessness, anorexia, increased heart rate (PVCs/PACs), tremors, headache.","Overdose risk: paradoxical bronchospasm — can be reversed with a beta blocker if truly needed, used cautiously.","Delivery is mostly inhaled to minimize systemic effects. MDI (metered dose inhaler) is not breath-activated and takes a slow, deep inhale technique; DPI (dry powder inhaler) is breath-activated and generally easier for children or patients …","Beta agonist names end in \"-erol\"/\"-terol\": albuterol, levalbuterol (short-acting); salmeterol, formoterol (long-acting). Short vs. long acting matters — mixing up rescue and preventer drugs is a common exam trap."]},{"s":"Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives▾","a":"bronchodilators-anticholinergics-xanthine-derivatives","k":["Bronchodilators — Anticholinergics & Xanthine Derivatives▾","Anticholinergics — Ipratropium is the main one. Blocking acetylcholine inhibits the parasympathetic nervous system, shifting toward sympathetic tone — bronchodilation, less mucus production. A prophylactic (daily) drug, not a rescue drug. O…","Xanthine Derivatives (Methylxanthines) — Theophylline, aminophylline. Inhibit phosphodiesterase, raising cAMP — which relaxes smooth muscle and blocks IgE-mediated release of allergic mediators, producing bronchodilation. Second-line, tried…","Classic anticholinergic side effects: \"dry as a bone, hot as a hare, blind as a bat, red as a beet, mad as a hatter\" — decreased secretions, increased temperature, blurred vision, flushing, and confusion.","Toxicity Signs — Insomnia, headache, tachycardia, dysrhythmias, seizures — distinct from the common side effects of nausea, vomiting, and decreased appetite.","Contraindications — Dysrhythmias, seizure disorders, hyperthyroidism, peptic ulcers.","Monitoring — Narrow therapeutic index — serum theophylline levels are monitored closely. Toxicity is treated with activated charcoal.","Smoking Interaction — Smoking decreases theophylline absorption. If a patient on theophylline quits smoking, the dose may need to be decreased and levels monitored more closely.","Other Cautions — Avoid caffeine (additive effect). Xanthine derivatives have many drug interactions overall — important to communicate clearly about who is taking one."]},{"s":"Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies▾","a":"anti-inflammatory-add-on-therapies","k":["Anti-Inflammatory & Add-On Therapies▾","Leukotriene Receptor Antagonists — Montelukast (Singulair, age 12 months+), zafirlukast (age 5+). Block leukotriene receptors on immune cells in the lungs, preventing inflammation, bronchoconstriction, and mucus production. Given PO (chewab…","Inhaled Corticosteroids — Beclomethasone, budesonide, fluticasone. Fewer systemic side effects than PO corticosteroids (which cover acute exacerbations instead). Not a rescue drug — for prevention and long-term maintenance; takes several we…","Mast Cell Stabilizers — Cromolyn is the only one. Stabilizes mast cell membranes and prevents the release of bronchoconstrictive, inflammatory substances. Used for prevention — taken 15–20 minutes before a known trigger. Not a rescue drug, …","Newer Add-On Therapies","Omalizumab — a monoclonal antibody that binds IgE, limiting the release of allergic mediators and reducing airway hyperresponsiveness. Asthma-specific; always an add-on, never given alone. Given by injection.","Roflumilast — a selective PDE4 inhibitor with potent anti-inflammatory effects in the lungs. Used for COPD exacerbation prevention, not acute treatment; given orally; works best for chronic bronchitis with a history of frequent exacerbation…","Omalizumab: carries a real risk of anaphylaxis — monitor closely for hypersensitivity reactions, especially with the first doses.","Rescue vs. Long-Term Control, Summarized","Long-term control (preventers): anticholinergics, xanthine derivatives, inhaled corticosteroids, leukotriene receptor antagonists, mast cell stabilizers, long-acting beta agonists.","Rescue (quick relief): short-acting beta agonists — albuterol. If a patient suddenly can't breathe and is wheezing, albuterol is the only right answer."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-pneumonia.html","t":"Pneumonia","secs":[{"s":"Pneumonia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors","Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP","How Pneumonia Develops & Presents","Diagnosis","Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia","Viral & Atypical Pneumonia","Treatment & Prevention","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors▾","a":"pneumonia-basics-risk-factors","k":["Pneumonia Basics & Risk Factors▾","Pneumonia is an infection in the lower respiratory tract — the pathogen has gotten past the upper airway's defenses (cilia, mucus, the cough reflex) and set up below them. It can be bacterial, viral, fungal, protozoal, or parasitic. It's tr…","Risk Factors","Age extremes — children under 5 and adults over 70–80.","Compromised immunity — long-term steroid therapy, organ transplant, HIV.","Underlying lung disease — COPD, pulmonary hypertension.","Alcohol use — the risk here is from aspiration, not a liver effect.","Altered level of consciousness or impaired swallowing — both raise aspiration risk.","Nursing home residency and immobility.","Hospitalization, especially with endotracheal intubation.","Thoracic or abdominal surgery, or anesthesia.","Influenza — the single most common overall cause of pneumonia.","Smoking — impairs ciliary clearance, a risk factor for every respiratory condition."]},{"s":"Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP▾","a":"classification-cap-hap-vap-hcap","k":["Classification — CAP, HAP, VAP & HCAP▾","CAP — Community-acquired pneumonia — develops outside the hospital. One of the most common reasons for hospitalization, and generally easier to treat than HAP.","HAP — Hospital-acquired pneumonia — develops 48+ hours after hospital admission. Worse outcomes than CAP, often associated with ICU care.","VAP — Ventilator-associated pneumonia — a subtype of HAP in intubated patients. More virulent, more contagious, and more deadly.","HCAP — Healthcare-associated pneumonia — technically grouped with CAP. Occurs in patients with frequent healthcare contact (nursing home residents, chronic hospitalizations, dialysis or chemo patients) without being truly hospital-acquired."]},{"s":"How Pneumonia Develops & Presents▾","a":"how-pneumonia-develops-presents","k":["How Pneumonia Develops & Presents▾","Pneumonia Pathophysiology, Step by Step","A pathogen reaches the lower airway — most commonly through aspiration of oropharyngeal secretions, or through inhaled droplets from someone else's cough.","This triggers an inflammatory reaction and pulmonary vasodilation.","Infection spreads into the alveoli; goblet cells are stimulated and secrete mucus.","Mucus accumulates between the alveoli and pulmonary capillaries, so the alveoli can't open and close properly.","Gas exchange becomes impaired, ciliary clearance fails, and the immune system activates.","Often Preceded By — An upper respiratory infection.","Common Symptoms — Fever, chills, cough (productive or dry, depending on cause), malaise, pleuritic chest pain (more pain with breathing).","Severe Symptoms — Dyspnea, hemoptysis.","Bacterial Cough — Typically productive and purulent — sputum may be green, rusty-colored, or described as \"currant jelly.\"","Viral Cough — Typically non-productive and scant.","Respiratory distress vs. respiratory failure: distress means the patient is still maintaining oxygenation, but only with an increased work of breathing — accessory muscle use, frequent breaks, an increased heart rate. Failure means they can…"]},{"s":"Diagnosis▾","a":"diagnosis","k":["Diagnosis▾","Physical Assessment — Cough, fever, chills, wet/rhonchorous breath sounds, pleuritic chest pain, dyspnea, exercise intolerance. Over a consolidated area, expect dullness to percussion (normally resonant), inspiratory crackles, increased tac…","Chest X-Ray — Shows an infiltrate or area of consolidation.","CBC — An elevated white blood cell count suggests a bacterial cause.","Sputum Culture & Sensitivity — The gold standard — identifies the specific pathogen and which antibiotics will work against it."]},{"s":"Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia▾","a":"bacterial-aspiration-pneumonia","k":["Bacterial & Aspiration Pneumonia▾","Staphylococcus aureus — Gram-positive; usually enters via the bloodstream/IV route. The most common gram-positive cause of hospital-acquired pneumonia, often as MRSA.","Streptococcus pneumoniae — Gram-positive; the most common organism in bacterial community-acquired pneumonia (\"pneumococcal pneumonia\"). Sputum is often brown or rusty-tinged.","Gram-Negative Organisms — Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter, Klebsiella pneumoniae — tend to cause more severe illness and are harder to treat. Often linked to central-line infections or IV drug use, and associated with hospital-acquired pneumonia…","MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) is defined in full on the ABX page's resistance & stewardship section","Aspiration Pneumonia","Aspirated GI contents trigger an inflammatory reaction; the severity depends on the pH of the aspirate — more acidic content causes a worse inflammatory response.","One way to help prevent severe aspiration pneumonia is a PPI, which lowers gastric acidity.","Risk factors: altered level of consciousness, alcohol use, immobility, NG tubes, decreased gag reflex, decreased gastric emptying.","Aspiration can be silent — a cough isn't required. High-risk patients need careful feeding observation and may need a dysphagia evaluation."]},{"s":"Viral & Atypical Pneumonia▾","a":"viral-atypical-pneumonia","k":["Viral & Atypical Pneumonia▾","Viral Pneumonia — Influenza is the most common cause overall and the most common cause of CAP; other viruses include adenovirus, RSV, and parainfluenza. Viruses impair the lungs' immune defenses, raising the risk of a secondary bacterial pn…","Pneumocystis Pneumonia (PCP) — A yeast-like fungus; associated with immunosuppression, most common in HIV.","Mycoplasma pneumoniae — \"Walking pneumonia\" — mild, with a persistent cough, headache, and earache. Has properties of both bacteria and viruses.","Legionella (Legionnaires' Disease) — Gram-negative; spread through water systems — old air conditioners, misted produce, hot tubs. Can be very deadly.","Aspergillus — Fungal; comes from old building walls, construction sites, stored grains, dead leaves, or compost. Can be severe, especially in immunocompromised patients.","Legionella pneumonia is a reportable disease. A confirmed case has to be reported to the health department so the water source can be identified, cleaned, or fixed — these patients also tend to get sicker than with other types of pneumonia."]},{"s":"Treatment & Prevention▾","a":"treatment-prevention","k":["Treatment & Prevention▾","Bacterial pneumonia is treated with antibiotics. Viral pneumonia gets supportive care unless a secondary bacterial infection develops. General supportive measures for any pneumonia: adequate ventilation and oxygenation, hydration, good pulm…","PCV13 — Protects against 13 strains of pneumococcal pneumonia.","PPSV23 — Covers an additional 23 types.","Both are generally given to elderly and immunocompromised patients."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-tuberculosis.html","t":"Tuberculosis (TB)","secs":[{"s":"Tuberculosis (TB)","a":"","k":["Week 2","TB Basics & Transmission","Latent vs. Active TB","Screening & Diagnosis","First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin","First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin","Drug-Resistant TB","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"TB Basics & Transmission▾","a":"tb-basics-transmission","k":["TB Basics & Transmission▾","Causative Organism — Mycobacterium tuberculosis is the most common cause; M. leprae causes leprosy, a lesser public-health concern than the TB strains.","Bacteria Type — An aerobic, rod-shaped bacillus — it needs a lot of oxygen to grow and proliferate, which is why TB typically affects the lungs first (it can also grow in bone or brain).","Granulomas — Walled-off areas containing macrophages and lymphocytes, with a hard outer casing and a cheese-like (caseous) center made of broken-down tissue. Sometimes called tubercles.","Growth Rate — Very slow-growing — which makes TB harder to treat, since most drugs work by targeting actively proliferating organisms.","Transmission","Spread human-to-human (by far the most common route), though also from cattle or birds.","Travels in airborne droplets, expelled by an infected person's cough or sneeze and inhaled by a new host.","From there it spreads to the lungs by traveling through the blood and lymphatic system."]},{"s":"Latent vs. Active TB▾","a":"latent-vs-active-tb","k":["Latent vs. Active TB▾","Latent TB","A person with a normal, working immune system exposed to TB usually just kills off the bacteria — no disease results.","In latent TB, the bacteria survive but stay walled off inside granulomas, dormant — no clinical signs or symptoms.","Can remain latent for life.","Active TB","Develops when the immune system can no longer contain the bacteria and it reactivates.","Reactivation triggers: HIV/immunocompromise, immunosuppressive medications (e.g., for rheumatoid arthritis or another autoimmune disease), poor nutritional status, renal failure.","Symptoms usually develop gradually — often not noticed until the disease is fairly advanced.","Early, Nonspecific Symptoms — Fatigue, weight loss, lethargy, anorexia, a low-grade fever (classically worse in the afternoon), and a productive cough that develops slowly over months.","More TB-Specific — Night sweats. Generalized anxiety can also develop as the disease progresses.","Advanced, Untreated — Dyspnea, chest pain, hemoptysis.","Extrapulmonary TB — If the bacteria implant somewhere outside the lungs: neurologic symptoms/meningitis (brain), bone pain (bone), or urinary problems (kidney)."]},{"s":"Screening & Diagnosis▾","a":"screening-diagnosis","k":["Screening & Diagnosis▾","Interferon-Gamma Release Assay — A blood draw — used for high-risk populations (for example, a nurse working in a high-risk area).","TB Skin Test — Used for non-high-risk, general-population screening (for example, a healthcare student).","Chest X-Ray — The next step after a positive screening test — a positive screen alone doesn't confirm active TB, so the chest X-ray looks for granulomas.","Sputum Stain & Culture — Looks directly for mycobacteria — confirms the diagnosis.","Patients are considered contagious until their sputum no longer grows mycobacteria. The exact timing of that is Med-Surg content, not needed for this course."]},{"s":"First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin▾","a":"first-line-tb-drugs-isoniazid-rifampin","k":["First-Line TB Drugs — Isoniazid & Rifampin▾","TB treatment uses first-line drugs (the primary regimen) and second-line drugs (reserved for drug-resistant cases — not covered by name in this course). Drug-susceptibility testing guides therapy, but while results are pending, patients are…","Isoniazid (INH) — The most widely used TB medication. Disrupts mycobacterial cell wall synthesis. Given PO. Side effects: peripheral neuropathy (teach and monitor for it, similar to diabetic neuropathy), hepatotoxicity (metabolized by the l…","Rifampin — Also first-line; used for other mycobacterial infections too (meningitis, HIV-related infections, leprosy). Works by inhibiting protein synthesis. Metabolized by the liver — can cause hepatitis, so liver function is monitored; ca…","Isoniazid — black-box warning: increased risk of liver injury and hepatitis."]},{"s":"First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin▾","a":"first-line-tb-drugs-ethambutol-pyrazinamide-streptomycin","k":["First-Line TB Drugs — Ethambutol, Pyrazinamide & Streptomycin▾","Ethambutol — First-line, bacteriostatic. Suppresses RNA synthesis, which in turn suppresses protein synthesis. Side effect: retrobulbar neuritis — inflammation of the optic nerve at the back of the eye. Watch for visual changes; these patie…","Pyrazinamide (PZA) — Bacteriostatic or bactericidal depending on concentration. Always used in combination with other TB drugs, never alone. Thought to inhibit lipid and nucleic acid synthesis. Metabolized by the liver — hepatotoxicity risk…","Streptomycin — The first drug ever available for TB — an aminoglycoside. Interferes with normal protein synthesis, producing faulty proteins in the bacteria. Side effects: ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and blood dyscrasias (affecting bleedin…","Aminoglycoside toxicity (nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity) is covered as a drug-class fact on the ABX page","All five of these — isoniazid, rifampin, ethambutol, pyrazinamide, and streptomycin — belong on the drug matrix and are fair game by name on the exam."]},{"s":"Drug-Resistant TB▾","a":"drug-resistant-tb","k":["Drug-Resistant TB▾","MDR-TB (Multi-Drug-Resistant TB)","A growing concern both nationally and globally — a significant issue in HIV/AIDS communities and other at-risk populations.","At-risk groups: people who are homeless, malnourished, using substances, living with cancer, immunosuppressed, or living in crowded or poor-sanitation housing.","In the U.S., Asian and Hispanic immigrant populations are particularly high-risk, often having contracted resistant strains in countries with less consistent access to medications.","Because TB spreads through airborne transmission, a resistant strain moving through a community is a serious public health concern.","Resistant cases move to second-line drugs, which are effective but not as effective as first-line therapy."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-anemia.html","t":"Anemia","secs":[{"s":"Anemia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology","Clinical Manifestations of Anemia","Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate","Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia","Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage","Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement","Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology▾","a":"anemia-basics-classifying-by-cell-morphology","k":["Anemia Basics & Classifying by Cell Morphology▾","What Anemia Is","Anemia is a lack of red blood cells (RBCs) — either a reduced total number of RBCs, or a decrease in the quality/quantity of hemoglobin.","Causes fall into a few buckets: impaired RBC production, acute or chronic blood loss, erythrocyte (RBC) destruction, or a combination.","Hemoglobin provides the oxygen-carrying capacity of an RBC — each chain has a heme unit with iron at its center, and hemoglobin reversibly binds oxygen and CO2 for transport. Functioning hemoglobin is required for oxygen delivery to the tis…","The bus analogy: the red blood cell is the bus, hemoglobin are the seats, iron is what the seats are made of, and oxygen is the passengers. No iron means no seats, so oxygen can't ride along — a problem with the iron, the hemoglobin, or the…","Regardless of cause, every anemia means less oxygen reaches the tissues — that's what drives the clinical manifestations.","Hemoglobin — not hematocrit — is the best indicator of anemia. Hematocrit is much more affected by a patient's fluid volume status, while hemoglobin is not.","Normocytic, Normochromic — Normal-sized cells, normal hemoglobin color/function. Points to blood loss, sickle cell disease, or aplastic anemia.","Microcytic, Hypochromic — Small (micro), pale (hypo) cells with reduced hemoglobin. This is iron deficiency anemia — without iron, hemoglobin can't be made correctly.","Macrocytic — Large, abnormally shaped cells, but normal hemoglobin color. Measured by MCV (mean corpuscular volume) — increased in macrocytic anemia. Points to a B12 or folate deficiency: without either, RBC DNA synthesis is impaired, produ…","Size + color tells you the type: micro + pale = iron deficiency. Macro + normal color = B12/folate deficiency (megaloblastic). Normal size + normal color = blood loss, sickle cell, or aplastic anemia."]},{"s":"Clinical Manifestations of Anemia▾","a":"clinical-manifestations-of-anemia","k":["Clinical Manifestations of Anemia▾","Mild — Often no symptoms at all — the body compensates well.","Mild to Moderate — Fatigue, weakness, mild tachycardia (the heart beats faster to move more oxygen), and possible shortness of breath.","Moderate to Severe — Tachycardia and tachypnea, low blood pressure, pallor, faintness, and cardiovascular symptoms with exertion. In very severe cases: bone pain (from the marrow working overtime), chest pain, heart failure, and an increase…","Why These Symptoms Happen","Weakness/fatigue: muscles and metabolism aren't getting enough oxygen to work normally.","Pallor: the body redistributes blood away from the skin toward organs that need it more (kidneys, lungs, gut, brain, heart).","Tachycardia/palpitations: a compensatory response — the heart beats faster to try to deliver more blood to the tissues, though this doesn't fix the underlying problem for long.","Bone pain: a chronic-anemia symptom, from the bone marrow working hard to produce more RBCs.","Severity also depends on how fast blood is lost, not just the hemoglobin level. Losing 2 liters instantly (trauma) causes severe symptoms even if the resulting hemoglobin matches a slow GI bleed that's gone unnoticed for months — the body h…"]},{"s":"Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate▾","a":"nutritional-deficiency-anemias-iron-b12-folate","k":["Nutritional Deficiency Anemias — Iron, B12 & Folate▾","Iron Deficiency Anemia — The most common anemia in the US. Microcytic, hypochromic. Highest risk: toddlers, adolescent girls, and women of childbearing age (especially pregnancy and menstruation). Causes: decreased dietary intake, impaired …","Iron Deficiency — Specific Findings — Glossitis (smooth, sore tongue), koilonychia (spoon nails), and pica (craving non-food items — ice chips, hard candy, or in extreme cases dirt or paper). Iron deficiency in childhood can cause cognitive…","B12 Deficiency Anemia — Macrocytic/megaloblastic — pale, large RBCs with impaired DNA synthesis. Most common cause: atrophic gastritis (often autoimmune), which destroys the stomach's parietal cells and stops production of intrinsic factor,…","B12 Deficiency — Specific Findings — Neurologic manifestations on top of the usual anemia symptoms — neuropathy and ataxia (gait/balance problems). Neurologic symptoms are the key differentiator that points specifically to B12 over other de…","Folate (B9) Deficiency Anemia — Also macrocytic/megaloblastic — folate is needed for RBC DNA production just like B12. Most common in pregnancy (needs increase greatly) and in malnourished populations, including alcoholism/liver disease (fo…","Folic acid can correct the anemia of a B12 deficiency without fixing the underlying B12 problem — masking it while the neurologic damage keeps progressing. Whenever folic acid is given, confirm the patient's B12 status is also adequate."]},{"s":"Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia▾","a":"anemia-of-chronic-kidney-disease-aplastic-anemia","k":["Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease & Aplastic Anemia▾","Anemia of Chronic Kidney Disease — The kidneys produce erythropoietin, which signals the bone marrow to make RBCs. Failing kidneys can't release enough erythropoietin, so RBC production drops — a chronic anemia that worsens as kidney functi…","Aplastic Anemia — A rare but dangerous primary bone marrow failure (\"hematopoietic failure\") — the prefix \"a-\" means \"all.\" Most commonly autoimmune (the bone marrow's stem cells are attacked); can also be congenital. Causes pancytopenia — …","Aplastic Anemia — Causes — Idiopathic; high-dose toxic exposures (chemotherapy, radiation, benzene, insecticides); bone marrow cancer; or an autoimmune reaction triggered by an infection like viral hepatitis or mononucleosis.","Aplastic Anemia — Why It's Dangerous — Beyond the usual anemia symptoms, low WBCs mean a high infection risk, and low platelets mean a high bleeding risk — all three problems at once.","Aplastic Anemia — Treatment — Whole blood transfusion (not just RBCs — replaces RBCs, WBCs, and platelets together), bone marrow transplant, immune suppression if autoimmune, or corticosteroids. Drugs that stimulate erythropoiesis are also …"]},{"s":"Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage▾","a":"hemolytic-anemias-acute-blood-loss-sickle-cell-thalassemia-hemorrhage","k":["Hemolytic Anemias & Acute Blood Loss — Sickle Cell, Thalassemia & Hemorrhage▾","Acquired Hemolytic Anemia — Premature destruction of RBCs by an external agent — an autoimmune attack, a blood type incompatibility, a drug reaction, a hereditary condition present at birth, or a physical agent like a severe burn. Immune co…","Hemolytic Anemia — Findings — Low hemoglobin with an increased reticulocyte count (immature RBCs — the bone marrow is releasing them before they're fully mature to try to keep up). Mild jaundice and hemoglobin in the urine, from the byprodu…","Sickle Cell Disease — An autosomal recessive genetic disorder, most common in the Black/African American population (especially those of Sub-Saharan African descent) — about 7–13%. Abnormal hemoglobin S distorts RBCs into a sickle shape; th…","Sickle Cell — Findings — Usual anemia symptoms plus swelling of the hands and feet (from occluded vessels) and fever, especially during a crisis.","Sickle Cell — Treatment — Oxygen, hydration, and pain management are the priorities, plus infection prevention (infection can trigger a crisis). Hydroxyurea increases the presence of fetal (non-sickle) hemoglobin. Blood transfusions treat t…","Thalassemia — An autosomal recessive genetic condition — patients lack the proteins that build hemoglobin (missing alpha or beta globin), producing microcytic, hypochromic RBCs. Beta thalassemia traces to Greek, Italian, or Jewish ancestry;…","Thalassemia — Findings & Treatment — Delayed growth, fatigue, dyspnea, swollen spleen/liver, bone deformities, and jaundice (from the shortened lifespan of these misshapen cells). Treated with regular blood transfusions, and possibly bone m…","Acute Blood Loss Anemia — From hemorrhage — trauma, or an internal bleed. Manifestations depend on the speed of blood loss: a fast, gross bleed causes severe symptoms quickly (the body has no time to compensate), while a slow, occult bleed …","Sickle cell crisis: triggered by anything that increases oxygen demand or lowers oxygen — dehydration, stress, high altitude, fever, and extreme temperatures. Causes painful vaso-occlusion from ischemia and necrosis in clogged vessels; seve…"]},{"s":"Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement▾","a":"anemia-pharmacology-iron-b12-folate-replacement","k":["Anemia Pharmacology — Iron, B12 & Folate Replacement▾","Ferrous Sulfate (PO) — Oral iron supplement — drug matrix medication. Treats/prevents iron deficiency anemia. Only about 20% of ferrous sulfate is elemental iron by weight, so the dose looks larger than the actual iron delivered. Best absor…","Ferrous Sulfate — Teaching — Take with vitamin C (helps absorption) — never with antacids or calcium (decreases absorption). Expect dark green/black stool — this is a normal, expected finding, not a sign of GI bleeding. Liquid forms can sta…","Iron Dextran (IV/IM) — IV/parenteral iron supplement — drug matrix medication, for patients with more severe deficiency or who can't tolerate oral iron. Given IM using the Z-track method (can stain skin). Not given at home.","Deferoxamine — The chelating agent used to treat iron toxicity/overdose — binds iron into an insoluble complex that's excreted in the stool.","Cyanocobalamin (B12) — B12 replacement. Given PO, subQ, IM, or intranasal — never IV. High-dose oral therapy is now known to work as well as injections for most patients, though injections are still used, especially when a stomach-absorptio…","Folic Acid — Folate replacement — prescription strength 1–5 mg, or at least 400 mcg/day over the counter. Well tolerated, with essentially no significant side effects. Routinely given in pregnancy via prenatal vitamins.","Iron dextran: the dextran component carries a risk of fatal anaphylactic reaction. Always give a test dose first and have epinephrine available before the first dose."]},{"s":"Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)▾","a":"anemia-pharmacology-erythropoiesis-stimulating-agents-esas","k":["Anemia Pharmacology — Erythropoiesis-Stimulating Agents (ESAs)▾","Epoetin Alfa (Epogen) — An erythropoiesis-stimulating agent (ESA) — stimulates the bone marrow to produce RBCs, raising hemoglobin and reticulocyte count. Used primarily for anemia of chronic kidney disease. Given IV or subQ. Requires adequ…","Darbepoetin Alfa — A longer-acting ESA, also given IV.","ESA Precautions — Contraindicated in uncontrolled hypertension. Do not give if it has been shaken or frozen; protect from light; do not dilute or mix with other medications.","ESA black-box warning: never give if hemoglobin is greater than 10 g/dL — overcorrection risks stroke, heart attack, increased cancer progression, injection-site problems, and a polycythemic, hypercoagulable state. Always check the hemoglob…","How an overshot hemoglobin from ESA therapy leads to a true polycythemic state is covered in full on the Polycythemia page"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week2-polycythemia.html","t":"Polycythemia","secs":[{"s":"Polycythemia","a":"","k":["Week 2","Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute","Relative Polycythemia","Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)","Secondary Polycythemia","Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute▾","a":"polycythemia-basics-relative-vs-absolute","k":["Polycythemia Basics — Relative vs. Absolute▾","\"Poly-\" means many — polycythemia means having too many red blood cells. There are two big buckets: relative polycythemia (a false elevation) and absolute polycythemia, which splits into primary and secondary.","Relative — A false elevation from hemoconcentration — plasma volume is low (usually severe dehydration), so RBCs look falsely elevated on a percentage basis. There isn't actually an excess of red blood cells.","Primary (Absolute) — Polycythemia vera — a true bone marrow stem cell disorder causing overproduction of red blood cells.","Secondary (Absolute) — A true increase in red blood cells as a compensatory response to chronic tissue hypoxia.","Risk Factors for Absolute Polycythemia","Chronic hypoxia — most commonly COPD (chronic bronchitis or emphysema).","Living at high altitude — can progress to chronic mountain sickness.","Long-term cigarette smoking.","Familial/genetic predisposition.","Long-term carbon monoxide exposure — tunnel workers, coal miners, other underground workers, garage attendants, and people in heavily polluted urban areas."]},{"s":"Relative Polycythemia▾","a":"relative-polycythemia","k":["Relative Polycythemia▾","Cause — An isolated decrease in plasma volume, most often from severe dehydration.","Effect — A false elevation of hemoglobin, hematocrit, and RBC percentage — the numbers look high only because the plasma they're measured against is low.","Treatment — Correct the underlying cause of the hemoconcentration (e.g., rehydration). Once the fluid deficit is corrected, the polycythemia resolves — there's nothing else to treat."]},{"s":"Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)▾","a":"primary-polycythemia-polycythemia-vera","k":["Primary Polycythemia (Polycythemia Vera)▾","Definition — A rare, slow-growing blood cancer/stem cell disorder — a single mutated stem cell overproduces red blood cells, and often white blood cells and platelets too (everything except lymphocytes), since it affects the bone marrow bro…","Epidemiology — Typically occurs in people over 60; about twice as common in men.","Pathophysiology — A chronic myeloproliferative disorder — the excess red blood cells thicken the blood.","Polycythemia Vera — Manifestations","Headache, fatigue, weight loss, dyspnea.","Hypertension and clotting problems — the excess red blood cells collide and clump in the vessels.","A ruddy (plethoric) color.","Intense, painful itching (aquagenic pruritus), especially worse with heat or water — patients are particularly bothered by warm water."]},{"s":"Secondary Polycythemia▾","a":"secondary-polycythemia","k":["Secondary Polycythemia▾","Definition — An adaptive, compensatory response to chronic tissue hypoxia — not a bone marrow disorder itself.","Mechanism — The body senses it's hypoxic; the kidneys secrete erythropoietin, and the bone marrow responds by making more red blood cells to try to carry more oxygen.","Most Common Causes — COPD and restrictive lung disease (poor gas exchange despite adequate red blood cells) and high altitude.","COPD pathophysiology (chronic bronchitis and emphysema) is covered in full on the Obstructive Airway Disorders page","Erythropoiesis-stimulating agents (like epoetin alfa) can also cause an iatrogenic polycythemic state if hemoglobin is overcorrected — full drug detail on the Anemia page"]},{"s":"Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia▾","a":"complications-shared-by-primary-secondary-polycythemia","k":["Complications Shared by Primary & Secondary Polycythemia▾","Increased Blood Viscosity & Volume — Hypertension, headache, and inability to concentrate. Cyanosis of the lips, nails, and mucous membranes can occur but is less common than the other findings.","Decreased Blood Flow — Excess red blood cells collide and clump, slowing flow — like trying to push too much water through a straw. Raises the risk of DVT (deep vein thrombosis), angina, cerebral insufficiency, and TIAs (transient ischemic …","Hypermetabolism — Night sweats and weight loss.","Other — Itching and pain in the fingers and toes.","The biggest concern with any absolute polycythemia (primary or secondary): stroke and heart attack from clotting. Too many red blood cells collide and clump in the vessels, blocking blood flow to the brain and heart."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-pharm-foundations.html","t":"Pharmacology Foundations","secs":[{"s":"Pharmacology Foundations","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation","Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion","Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life","Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pharmacology definitions and drug naming/regulation, pharmacokinetics (absorption through excretion, CYP450, half-life), pharmacodynamics, and medication safety."]},{"s":"Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","a":"intro-to-pharmacology-definitions-naming-regulation","k":["Intro to Pharmacology — Definitions, Naming & Regulation▾","Pharmacology — The study of drugs/chemicals/medications and the changes they cause in the body.","Pharmacokinetics — What the body does to the drug (absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion).","Pharmacodynamics — What the drug does to the body — its mechanism of action and overall effects.","Chemical / Generic / Trade Name — Chemical name: long, complex, used in research (e.g., acetylparaminophenol). Generic name: the one official name, lowercase, used on exams and NCLEX (e.g., acetaminophen). Trade name: the capitalized brand …","Prototype Drug — The first drug in a class, used as the comparison point for effectiveness/side effects of newer drugs in that class (e.g., ibuprofen/Advil is the NSAID prototype).","Therapeutic Effect — The intended effect of the drug (e.g., lowering blood pressure).","Side Effect — An unintended effect of a normal dose.","Toxicity / Adverse Effect — Toxicity: a harmful effect. Adverse effect: an unexpected, dangerous reaction. (Used interchangeably with \"side effect\" at this course level.)","Allergic Reaction — An unexpected, potentially dangerous immune system response — ranges from itching/hives to anaphylaxis.","How New Drugs Are Approved (FDA)","Preclinical: tested on lab animals for therapeutic/adverse effects.","Phase 1: healthy human volunteers — checking for major adverse reactions.","Phase 2: patients with the target disease — identifying rarer/less frequent side effects; the drug may be pulled here if effects are too dangerous.","Phase 3: a vast clinical market — unexpected responses may still lead to withdrawal.","Phase 4: post-market, ongoing FDA monitoring. If a pattern of severe reactions emerges, the FDA can require a black box warning — the strictest FDA warning a drug can carry while staying on the market.","Schedule I — No accepted medical use, very high abuse potential (e.g., heroin, LSD, ecstasy).","Schedule II — High abuse potential but some medical use; severe psychological/physical dependence risk (e.g., opioids, amphetamines). No automatic refills — each fill needs a new prescription.","Schedule III — Moderate-to-low potential for dependence (e.g., ketamine, anabolic steroids).","Schedule IV — Low potential for abuse/dependence (e.g., Xanax, Valium, Ambien).","Schedule V — Very low abuse potential — small amounts of narcotics/stimulants (e.g., cough syrup with a small amount of codeine).","To be sold over the counter (OTC), a drug must be safe (low risk of side effects/abuse) and the consumer must be able to diagnose the condition and monitor effectiveness themselves. Some OTC drugs (e.g., pseudoephedrine — a meth precursor) …","Dietary/Herbal Supplements, Teratogens & Pharmacogenomics","Dietary/herbal supplements can only claim an effect on body structure or function (not that they treat a medical condition) and aren't FDA-evaluated for safety/efficacy before marketing. They can still interact with medications — e.g., gink…","Teratogens (e.g., alcohol, marijuana, nicotine) cause congenital malformations. The FDA's old pregnancy risk categories (A = safest, through X = contraindicated, known fetal risk) have been replaced since 2015 by the Pregnancy and Lactation…","Pharmacogenomics — studying how a person's genes affect their drug response, to tailor medication choice/dosing (already used in some cancer and psychiatric drug selection)."]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-absorption-distribution-metabolism-excretion","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism & Excretion▾","Enteral — Via the GI tract — oral, sublingual, buccal, rectal.","Parenteral — Outside the GI tract — typically injection (intradermal, subcutaneous, intramuscular, intrathecal, intra-articular, intravenous).","Topical — Applied to body surfaces — ointments, creams, pastes, powders, transdermal patches.","Three phases of drug activity: pharmaceutical (the drug dissolves — disintegration breaks a tablet into particles, dissolution dissolves those particles into GI fluid so they can be absorbed), pharmacokinetic (absorption → distribution → me…","Bioavailability — The percentage of an administered drug that reaches systemic circulation in active form. IV drugs: 100% (no absorption barrier). Oral/GI drugs: less than 100%, due to the first-pass effect.","First-Pass Effect — An orally absorbed drug must pass through the liver before reaching systemic circulation, and the liver metabolizes a portion of it to an inactive form — reducing bioavailability. This is why oral doses (e.g., oral morph…","Distribution — Movement of the drug from blood to its site of action — best in highly vascularized areas (heart, liver, kidneys, brain); slower in muscle, skin, and fat, and impaired by conditions like peripheral vascular disease or a poorl…","Blood-Brain Barrier — Tight capillary junctions in the brain that block most drugs — only lipid-soluble drugs or ones with a transport system can cross (e.g., alcohol, glucose). Not fully developed in infants, so extra caution is needed wit…","Protein Binding — Drugs travel partly bound to plasma proteins (e.g., albumin) and partly free/unbound — only the unbound drug is active. Low albumin (malnutrition, liver disease, alcoholism) means more free drug and a higher risk of toxici…"]},{"s":"Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","a":"pharmacokinetics-metabolism-excretion-half-life","k":["Pharmacokinetics — Metabolism, Excretion & Half-Life▾","Metabolism (Biotransformation) — Chemically alters the drug into a metabolite — from active/lipid-soluble to inactive/water-soluble, ready for excretion. The liver is the main organ responsible.","CYP450 System — The liver's main drug-metabolizing enzyme system. A drug is a substrate if it's metabolized by a CYP450 enzyme. A CYP450 inducer speeds up metabolism of substrate drugs (less drug in the body, lower therapeutic effect). A CY…","Excretion — Elimination of the drug (mostly via the kidneys, through glomerular filtration, tubular secretion, and reabsorption). Kidney function directly affects how well a drug is excreted.","Half-Life (T½) — The time it takes for serum drug concentration to fall by 50%. After about 5 half-lives, ~97% of the drug is eliminated. It also takes about 4–5 half-lives to reach steady state — where the amount of drug eliminated equals …","Half-life guides dosing intervals. With a drug like morphine (half-life ~2–4 hours), levels drop meaningfully between doses — around-the-clock dosing (a scheduled baseline dose plus PRN doses for breakthrough symptoms) is used, especially f…"]},{"s":"Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","a":"pharmacodynamics-drug-safety","k":["Pharmacodynamics & Drug Safety▾","Receptor — A reactive site on/inside a cell (usually a protein) that a drug binds to, forming a drug-receptor complex that stimulates or inhibits a response.","Agonist — Binds a receptor and initiates the desired therapeutic effect (e.g., a beta-1 adrenergic agonist causing vasodilation).","Antagonist — Blocks/inhibits a natural substance from binding and acting (e.g., diphenhydramine/Benadryl blocking histamine at the H1 receptor).","Receptor-less Activation — Some drugs act through direct chemical interaction rather than a receptor (e.g., antacids neutralizing stomach acid, or magnesium sulfate acting as a laxative via an osmotic effect).","Therapeutic Index — The ratio of a drug's toxic level to its therapeutic level. A narrow therapeutic index (e.g., warfarin, digoxin) means a small margin between helpful and toxic — needs closer monitoring. A wide/high index (e.g., amoxicil…","Drug Interactions","Additive effect: two drugs with a similar mechanism of action are given together on purpose, for a stronger combined effect (e.g., two antibiotics for a complicated infection).","Synergism/potentiation: two drugs with different mechanisms combine for an effect greater than either alone (e.g., Coumadin plus aspirin).","Antidote: a drug given to antagonize another drug's toxic effects (e.g., naloxone/Narcan for opioid overdose).","Interactions can also decrease therapeutic effects — CYP450 inducers speeding up elimination, impaired GI absorption (e.g., after intestinal surgery), or activated charcoal intentionally blocking absorption.","Polypharmacy raises drug interaction risk — a special concern in older adults, who also have slower hepatic metabolism, reduced circulation/distribution, decreased GI absorption, and reduced renal excretion, raising risk for adverse reactio…","Medication safety practices: tall-man lettering for look-alike names (e.g., \"heparin\"), barcode scanning and automated dispensing (e.g., Pyxis), no trailing zeros (write \"1 mg,\" not \"1.0 mg\") and always a leading zero (\"0.25 mg,\" not \".25 m…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-cellular-adaptation-infection-immunity.html","t":"Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity","secs":[{"s":"Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","What Pathophysiology Is","Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors","Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease","Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia","Necrosis & Gangrene","Inflammation Basics","Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies","Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry","Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process","Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pathophysiology basics, causes and staging of disease, cellular adaptation, necrosis/gangrene, inflammation, adaptive immunity, and infection."]},{"s":"What Pathophysiology Is▾","a":"what-pathophysiology-is","k":["What Pathophysiology Is▾","Breaking Down the Word","Physiology is normal functioning — how the body works when there aren't any problems, at the molecular, cellular, and whole-organ-system level.","Pathology is the study of disease and injury — what abnormal processes are happening to disrupt the body from normal functioning.","Pathophysiology combines the two: the mechanisms of disease — how and why changes in body structure and function happen, and how those changes produce the signs and symptoms of a given disease.","Disease is a disruption in homeostasis — \"homeo\" (same) + \"stasis\" (balance/stability). This disruption can be physical (e.g., heart failure — the heart can't pump adequately, so the body doesn't get the blood/oxygen it needs), mental (e.g.…","Temperature Rises — Blood vessels dilate (flushed/red skin) and sweat glands secrete fluid — both mechanisms lose heat to the environment, cooling the body back toward normal.","Temperature Drops — Blood vessels constrict (pale skin) and sweat glands stop producing fluid, conserving heat. Shivering — involuntary muscle contraction — generates additional heat."]},{"s":"Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","a":"causes-of-disease-intrinsic-extrinsic-factors","k":["Causes of Disease — Intrinsic & Extrinsic Factors▾","Intrinsic Factors — Come from inside the body: genetics/genetic mutations, immune status (immunocompromised patients are more susceptible to disease), age, sex, autoimmune disorders (the body attacking itself), and over- or under-production…","Extrinsic Factors — Come from outside the body: pathogens (bacteria, viruses, parasites), environmental exposures (toxins, pollutants), lifestyle/behaviors (diet, exercise, smoking, alcohol), and stressors that overwhelm the body's defenses…","The Epidemiologic Triangle (Epi-Triad)","Pathogen (agent): the causative factor responsible for disease — a bacteria, virus, or parasite.","Host: typically the human — susceptibility depends on genetics, age, and immune status.","Environment: the external conditions that influence how the pathogen and host interact (e.g., some bacteria can't survive heat, which is why certain foods are cooked to specific temperatures).","Disease risk emerges when all three components come together."]},{"s":"Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","a":"identifying-describing-staging-disease","k":["Identifying, Describing & Staging Disease▾","Signs — Objective data — what can be seen, heard, or touched (e.g., wheezes on auscultation, a strong or weak pulse).","Symptoms — Subjective data — what the patient reports (e.g., pain, nausea, dizziness).","Diagnosis — Putting a name to the disease (e.g., pneumonia, heart failure, hypertension).","Etiology — The cause of the disease — not always known.","Prognosis — The expected disease course or outcome — the likelihood of returning to baseline.","Exposure — Contact with the agent or pathogen that sets off the disease cascade.","Sudden Onset — Signs/symptoms start quickly (e.g., a myocardial infarction, an asthma attack).","Insidious Onset — Slow and gradual — patients often adjust to symptoms until the disease has progressed significantly before it's caught (e.g., a slow GI bleed).","Latent Onset — The disease process is present but inactive, and can become active later (e.g., the chickenpox virus lying dormant before reactivating as shingles).","Prodromal — Early, nonspecific signs that a disease/condition is beginning (e.g., chills or a mild headache before a cold or flu fully sets in).","Remission / Convalescence — Remission: the disease is no longer active. Convalescence: recovering from the disease, back toward baseline.","Idiopathic — The cause of the disease is unknown (e.g., idiopathic cardiomyopathy).","Iatrogenic — Caused by the healthcare field itself — a treatment, drug, or action (or inaction) caused the disease (e.g., a colon perforation during a colonoscopy).","Exacerbation — A worsening of disease — often an acute decline in a chronic condition (e.g., an asthma attack triggered by allergen exposure).","Prefixes/suffixes worth knowing: hypo- (low) vs. hyper- (high); -penia (too few) vs. -cytosis (too many); -osis (a process/condition); -itis (inflammation, e.g. hepatitis); -pathy (disease, e.g. myopathy). Same root, opposite direction is a…"]},{"s":"Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","a":"cellular-adaptation-atrophy-to-neoplasia","k":["Cellular Adaptation — Atrophy to Neoplasia▾","Cells adapt to survive stress or injury. Some adaptations are normal/physiologic (muscle growth from exercise, uterine growth in pregnancy); others are pathologic, usually triggered by things like radiation, medications, or lack of oxygen.","Atrophy — Cells shrink in size; the whole organ can become atrophic. Physiologic atrophy is a developmental problem at birth (an organ/gland that never develops properly). Pathologic atrophy comes from decreased workload, poor blood supply,…","Hypertrophy — Cells get bigger (not more numerous), usually increasing function too. Typically from mechanical stimuli — repetitive stretching, chronic pressure, prolonged volume overload (e.g., quad muscle growth from repeated exercise is …","Hyperplasia — An increase in the number of cells (cell size stays the same) — only cells capable of dividing can do this (commonly skin, intestinal epithelium, glandular cells). Usually pathologic — most often cancers or abnormal hormonal s…","Dysplasia — Abnormal changes in the size, shape, or organization of mature cells — often follows atypical hyperplasia. Classified mild/moderate/severe, and is reversible (e.g., tobacco-related mouth/mucosal cell dysplasia can normalize afte…","Metaplasia — A reversible replacement of one mature cell type with another, less-differentiated type — related to chronic irritation/inflammation, and more strongly associated with cancer risk than dysplasia alone. It's an adaptation that h…","Neoplasia / Anaplasia — Cellular changes that no longer respond to normal regulatory processes — usually from a gene mutation. Anaplastic cells are cancer cells that have differentiated back to immature/embryonic forms; seeing \"anaplasia\" s…","Benign Tumors","Differentiated cells, less anaplastic (more mature).","Reproduce faster than normal cells, but cannot metastasize — stay contained and grow slowly.","Usually encapsulated.","Mainly a problem if located somewhere sensitive to compression, like the brain.","Malignant Tumors","Very anaplastic, non-differentiated, immature cells.","Reproduce rapidly with abnormal cells.","Metastasize — spread to other sites, near or distant, depending on proximity to lymphatic/blood systems.","Typically not encapsulated."]},{"s":"Necrosis & Gangrene▾","a":"necrosis-gangrene","k":["Necrosis & Gangrene▾","Necrosis — Cell death from disease, injury, or failure of blood supply — irreversible, with inflammation, swelling, redness, and cell bursting in the area.","Ischemic Necrosis — Necrosis from infarction and prolonged ischemia — an absence of blood supply to the area.","Liquefactive Necrosis — Dead cells/tissue are digested by enzymes and become liquefied, often forming an abscess with a liquid center. Typical in organs with a lot of lipids (lungs, brain) or where there's an abscess with heavy acute inflam…","Necrotic tissue becomes a breeding ground for bacteria — when bacterial invasion follows disruption of a major blood supply, that's gangrene. Most common in the lower extremities (toes, feet) of patients with poor circulation, such as those…","Dry Gangrene — Minimal bacteria, minimal inflammatory response. Tissue turns black, dry, and wrinkled. Spreads slowly (weeks to months); a clear demarcation line separates dead from healthy tissue.","Wet Gangrene — Liquefactive necrosis with extensive bacterial/WBC damage producing a liquid wound. Skin is moist, dark, cool, pulseless, and swollen, with blisters and a foul odor. No demarcation line; spreads much faster than dry gangrene …","Gas Gangrene — A specialized, severe form of wet gangrene caused by Clostridium perfringens (an anaerobic, spore-forming organism found in soil), typically from severe trauma or a compound fracture. Destroys connective tissue and cellular m…"]},{"s":"Inflammation Basics▾","a":"inflammation-basics","k":["Inflammation Basics▾","What Inflammation Is","Occurs with cell injury, regardless of cause — initially a protective mechanism that begins healing, working to destroy invading/harmful agents, limit their spread, and prepare damaged tissue for repair.","The five cardinal signs: redness, swelling, heat, pain, and loss of function.","Exogenous causes come from outside (surgery, trauma, burns); endogenous causes come from inside (e.g., tissue ischemia).","Acute inflammation lasts less than 2 weeks and is helpful. Chronic inflammation extends much longer, can form scar tissue, and is no longer a helpful process.","Mechanism: tissue injury releases chemical mediators that cause vasodilation and increased vascular permeability, letting white blood cells (mainly neutrophils and monocytes) move out of the blood vessel and into the injured tissue — a recr…","Serous — Mild inflammation — watery, low protein (e.g., clear fluid leaking from a healing surgical incision).","Serosanguineous — Similar to serous but pink-tinged, with small amounts of RBCs — normal immediately post-op.","Purulent — Severe inflammation, typically bacterial infection — thick, sticky, high in neutrophils/protein/debris, often discolored and foul-smelling; may need drainage (an abscess).","Hemorrhagic — Large amounts of RBCs — the most severe type, from badly damaged/necrotic blood vessels. Signals active bleeding, not just a wound.","If inflammation isn't contained, it can become systemic — driven by cytokines (interleukin-1, interleukin-6, TNF-alpha). Systemic inflammation presents as fever, an elevated white blood cell count, fatigue, and muscle catabolism (breakdown)…"]},{"s":"Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","a":"adaptive-immunity-b-cells-t-cells-antibodies","k":["Adaptive Immunity — B Cells, T Cells & Antibodies▾","Self vs. Non-Self","The major histocompatibility complex (MHC), also called the human leukocyte antigen (HLA) in humans, is a gene cluster on chromosome 6 that makes \"self\" proteins displayed on nearly every cell surface.","Specific adaptive immunity recognizes foreign invaders, destroys them, and remembers the encounter for a faster response next time.","B cells and T cells are lymphocytes responsible for this memory immunity. B cells provide humoral immunity (antibodies in body fluids); T cells provide cell-mediated immunity (recognizing antigens on cell surfaces).","An antigen is essentially a \"name tag\" on a foreign/bad cell (cancer, bacteria, fungal, inflammatory) that marks it for attack.","Memory B cells remember a prior antigen exposure and respond rapidly on re-exposure. Plasma cells are short-lived B cells that secrete antibodies against the triggering antigen.","IgG — Most prevalent (~75–80% of all antibodies). Easily crosses into interstitial fluid; produced later in an infection (or after a vaccine) — protects against bacterial and viral infection.","IgM — ~10% of antibodies. First produced after exposure/immunization, activating complement — a signal that something is newly/acutely happening.","IgA — Found in saliva, tears, secretions, breast milk, GI, and GU tract — protects at secretory/excretory sites.","IgD — Trace amounts in serum and on B cell surfaces — stimulates B cells to multiply, differentiate, and secrete other immunoglobulins.","IgE — Plays a major role in immunity against parasites and in allergic reactions (works with mast cells).","Passive Immunity — Transfer of antibody-containing plasma from a protected person to one who isn't — e.g., mother-to-fetus (IgG crosses the placenta; IgA is passed via breast milk), or injection of antibodies/immune globulins (not a vaccine…","Active Immunity — The result of an active infection or a vaccine — the body creates its own antibodies (usually via memory B cells), typically much faster on a second exposure, which is how vaccines prevent illness.","Traditional — An inactivated or killed part of the virus/bacteria/organism.","Attenuated — A weakened, still-live version — should not be given to immunocompromised patients or those with conditions putting them at risk for complications.","Toxoid — An inactivated toxin that stimulates antitoxin production (e.g., tetanus vaccine).","Conjugate — A protein/toxoid from one organism attached to a disease-causing organism to stimulate a response (e.g., Hib).","mRNA — Uses a snippet of the virus's genetic code to teach the body's antibodies to respond."]},{"s":"Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","a":"infection-basics-microorganisms-transmission-portals-of-entry","k":["Infection Basics — Microorganisms, Transmission & Portals of Entry▾","Virus — DNA or RNA with a protein shell; requires a host cell to replicate. Responsible for COVID-19, influenza, chickenpox, and more.","Bacteria — Larger than a virus, at least single-celled, usually with one strand of DNA; can reproduce inside or outside cells and suppress the body's defenses (e.g., strep throat, TB, UTIs).","Fungal — Spore-forming organisms (e.g., yeast infections).","Protozoa — Typically live in water environments (e.g., malaria).","Helminths — Parasitic worms (roundworms, hookworms).","Prions — Misfolded proteins that attach to other proteins, even smaller than viruses — cause devastating diseases like Creutzfeldt-Jakob (mad cow) disease.","Direct Contact — Kissing, sex, contact with contaminated soil/vegetation, or droplet spread (short-range aerosols from sneezing/coughing, e.g., pertussis, meningitis).","Indirect — Airborne — Droplet nuclei stay suspended in air (e.g., measles).","Indirect — Vehicle-Borne — Infectious material via food, water, or blood (e.g., hepatitis A from contaminated food).","Indirect — Vector-Borne — Carried by another organism — mosquitoes, fleas, ticks (e.g., malaria, Rocky Mountain spotted fever, Lyme disease).","Portals of Entry","Respiratory (airways/lungs) and GI (via eating/the stomach).","Genitourinary tract (e.g., STDs, catheters).","Skin — normally a strong barrier (tight cell junctions), but skin integrity issues are a major vulnerability. Translocation is the movement of bacteria across the intestinal lining (most often the peritoneal cavity) when the gut becomes \"le…","Blood — transfusion contamination or needle sticks.","Maternal-fetal transmission — some organisms (e.g., Zika, Listeria) cross the placental barrier or transmit during childbirth."]},{"s":"Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","a":"stages-of-infection-the-infectious-process","k":["Stages of Infection & the Infectious Process▾","Incubation — Time from pathogen entry to first symptoms — no symptoms yet, just growth/multiplication.","Prodromal — Onset of nonspecific symptoms (mild fatigue, low fever) — not yet fully symptomatic.","Acute Stage — Full-blown, specific signs/symptoms of the illness.","Convalescence — Symptoms improving, illness disappearing.","Resolution — The pathogen is completely eliminated. Some pathogens never reach this stage (e.g., chickenpox virus staying dormant for life).","The 6-Stage Infectious/Inflammatory Process","1. Injury — the initial insult to the area.","2. Brief vasoconstriction — stops bleeding, limits organism movement.","3. Vasodilation — blood flows freely into the area, bringing the five cardinal signs of inflammation.","4. Increased permeability — fluid leaks from vessels into tissue.","5. Emigration of leukocytes & phagocytosis — neutrophils (plus eosinophils, NK cells, monocytes) migrate in and engulf invaders, producing exudate.","6. Systemic symptoms (if the process doesn't stay localized) — a total-body response driven by the hypothalamic fever set point, which is ultimately protective (helps conserve heat, boosts immune cell/antibody function, and slows some bacte…"]},{"s":"Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","a":"diagnosing-infection-healthcare-associated-infections","k":["Diagnosing Infection & Healthcare-Associated Infections▾","Colonization — A pathogen inhabits a body site without causing signs/symptoms of infection (e.g., asymptomatic staph on the skin). Can turn into an active infection with immune compromise or tissue disruption.","Gram Stain — Identifies bacteria as gram-positive or gram-negative and their shape/arrangement. Gram-negative bacteria are more dangerous — an outer membrane/capsule hides their antigens, making them harder for the body to fight.","Sensitivity — Takes 24–72 hours; identifies the exact bacteria and which antibiotics it's sensitive/resistant to.","Urinalysis — Normal Values — pH 5–9; nitrates negative (positive suggests bacterial infection); leukocyte esterase negative (positive suggests infection); RBCs less than 5 (more suggests infection); epithelial cells suggest a contaminated s…","Nosocomial (Healthcare-Associated) Infections","Acquired in the hospital, not present at home — typically more virulent and more likely to be drug-resistant.","MRSA — methicillin-resistant Staph aureus. CRE — carbapenem-resistant Enterobacteriaceae (resistant to an entire antibiotic class). MDRO — multi-drug-resistant organism (resistant to multiple drug types).","Superinfection: a new infection that develops during treatment for another one — broad-spectrum antibiotics kill off normal flora that normally keep opportunistic pathogens in check.","C. diff (Clostridium difficile) — Normal intestinal flora killed by antibiotics lets C. diff overgrow — causes frequent, watery (often orange-tinged) diarrhea and abdominal cramping, sometimes months after treatment. Diagnosed by stool PCR …","Candidiasis — A yeast/fungal overgrowth after antibiotics kill normal skin/mucosal flora — thrush (oral), or infections in moist skin folds (under the breast, etc.). Untreated oral thrush can descend into the esophagus. Prevented/treated wi…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-hypertension-hyperlipidemia.html","t":"Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia (HTN/HLD)","secs":[{"s":"Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia (HTN/HLD)","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From","Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia","Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms","Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology","Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)","Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes","Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms","Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes","Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics","Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: cholesterol and lipoprotein basics, diagnosing hyperlipidemia, atherosclerosis, lipid-lowering pharmacology, blood pressure regulation (the RAS system), hypertension classification and complications, and antihypert…"]},{"s":"Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From▾","a":"cholesterol-basics-lipoproteins-where-cholesterol-comes-from","k":["Cholesterol Basics — Lipoproteins & Where Cholesterol Comes From▾","Cholesterol is essential — it's part of every cell membrane, a building block for hormones (estrogen, testosterone, cortisol) and vitamin D, a key ingredient in bile acid, and it helps the skin retain water. It's highly insoluble, so it has…","Exogenous Cholesterol — From diet — only about a quarter of the body's cholesterol. The 2015 USDA Dietary Guidelines dropped the old 300 mg/day dietary limit after the AHA/ACC found cholesterol intake itself isn't a nutrient of concern for …","Endogenous Cholesterol — Made by the liver — about three-quarters of the body's cholesterol, via the HMG-CoA reductase pathway. The liver responds to diet: more dietary cholesterol only minimally raises levels, but more dietary saturated fa…","Liver cells have LDL receptors. LDL from the blood binds these receptors, and HMG-CoA reductase uses it to produce cholesterol. This is a negative feedback loop: high LDL/cholesterol turns down HMG-CoA reductase production; if cholesterol p…","HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein) — The \"good\" cholesterol — more protein, less cholesterol. Higher HDL decreases risk of adverse cardiovascular events.","LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein) — The \"bad\" cholesterol — less protein, more cholesterol (\"L for lousy,\" or \"L for lower\"). Increases atherosclerosis risk.","VLDL (Very-Low-Density Lipoprotein) — Mostly triglycerides, so it isn't measured directly — high triglycerides suggest high VLDL, which also raises heart disease risk."]},{"s":"Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia▾","a":"diagnosing-hyperlipidemia-screening-targets-familial-hypercholesterolemia","k":["Diagnosing Hyperlipidemia — Screening, Targets & Familial Hypercholesterolemia▾","Hypercholesterolemia (too much cholesterol) is also called hyperlipidemia (high lipid levels, the term you'll hear most) or dyslipidemia (a lipid problem generally). Diagnosed via a fasting blood draw (fasting overnight — a recent meal can …","Total Cholesterol — Below 200. Calculated as HDL + LDL + triglycerides ÷ 5.","HDL — Above 55 (females), above 45 (males); above 60 is optimal for both.","LDL — Below 130 generally, below 100 for higher-risk patients (diabetes, other risk factors), and as low as below 70 for the highest-risk patients.","Triglycerides — Below 160.","NCLEX-style exams provide normal lab ranges as a reference — you don't need to memorize exact numbers, but you should recognize what an abnormal cholesterol panel looks like.","Familial Hypercholesterolemia","A genetically-linked, familial pattern of high cholesterol caused by a defect in liver LDL receptors — the liver can't efficiently pull LDL out of the blood, so cholesterol stays elevated despite diet, exercise, and a healthy lifestyle.","Suspect it in children or people in their early 20s with high cholesterol, especially with a strong family pattern.","Inheriting the genetic variant from both parents can cause severe hypercholesterolemia in young children, including heart disease or heart attacks.","Because the defect is in the LDL receptors themselves, this group responds poorly to medications that work by increasing LDL receptors (like statins).","General (non-familial) risk factors: age, family history, tobacco use, hypertension, diabetes, physical inactivity, and a poor diet. Age and family history can't be modified — the goal is addressing what can be controlled to lower overall c…"]},{"s":"Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms▾","a":"atherosclerosis-how-plaque-forms","k":["Atherosclerosis — How Plaque Forms▾","Atherosclerosis is a buildup of cholesterol in the vessels. Arteriosclerosis — thickening/hardening of the arterial wall — is often used interchangeably, since the arteries (which carry oxygenated blood to tissues) are the vessels of greate…","The Plaque-Formation Sequence","1. Endothelial injury. The endothelium (the vessel's inner lining) is damaged — by cigarette/tobacco use, chronic hemodynamic wall stress (hypertension), or hyperglycemia (long-term high blood sugar).","2. Increased permeability. The normally tightly-bunched endothelial cells loosen, letting LDL molecules pass under the vessel wall.","3. Inflammation & foam cells. Macrophages arrive and engulf the trapped lipids, becoming foam cells that embed in the vessel wall and appear as fatty streaks.","4. Plaque development. Inflammatory mediators, accumulated lipids, and foam cells form a lipid core that expands into the vessel and can impede blood flow.","There's no simple blood test for atherosclerosis itself — confirming it invasively (fluoroscopy with injectable dye) is highly invasive. As a proxy, clinicians check C-reactive protein (CRP), a nonspecific marker of systemic inflammation re…"]},{"s":"Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology▾","a":"atherosclerosis-consequences-blockage-severity-related-terminology","k":["Atherosclerosis — Consequences, Blockage Severity & Related Terminology▾","Large plaques with a large lipid core are prone to rupture. A rupture triggers platelet aggregation and clot (thrombus) formation, which can block the vessel entirely — anything downstream loses oxygen and nutrients. In the heart, that's a …","30–90% Blockage — Blood can still get through; the patient may be asymptomatic, especially if sedentary or if the blockage is in a smaller vessel.","~99% Blockage — Severe flow constriction — typically chest pain (angina) with minimal exertion, like getting up and walking to a chair. Needs immediate intervention; this is life-threatening.","Overlapping terminology: coronary heart disease (CHD), coronary artery disease (CAD), ischemic heart disease, and atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) all describe the same underlying problem — plaque buildup in the coronary arter…"]},{"s":"Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)▾","a":"hyperlipidemia-pharmacology-statins-hmg-coa-reductase-inhibitors","k":["Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Statins (HMG-CoA Reductase Inhibitors)▾","Before medication, clinicians try 6 months of diet and physical activity modification (unless the patient already has a history of MI, stroke, or other atherosclerosis-related organ damage). If that isn't enough, statins are first-line. Sta…","Statins reduce fatal and non-fatal atherosclerotic cardiovascular events (heart attacks, strokes), reduce disability after a non-fatal stroke, and are used for both primary prevention (before any event) and secondary prevention (after one, …","Drugs (know these) — Atorvastatin (the prototype), simvastatin, rosuvastatin.","Typical Effect — LDL decreases ~21–63%, HDL increases ~5–22%, triglycerides decrease ~6–43%.","Dosing Timing — Take at night — the liver makes more cholesterol at night, so this is when the drug is most effective. Required for simvastatin and rosuvastatin (shorter half-life); atorvastatin has a longer half-life so timing matters less…","Interactions — Avoid grapefruit — it interferes with statin metabolism and raises statin levels. Avoid alcohol (liver risk). Avoid other drugs that raise myopathy/rhabdomyolysis risk (azoles, HIV protease inhibitors, macrolides, cyclosporin…","Adverse reactions: myopathy (muscle weakness — ask patients about this), rhabdomyolysis (muscle fiber breakdown whose byproducts can cause acute kidney failure), and hepatotoxicity (liver damage with a year or more of treatment — monitor AS…"]},{"s":"Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes▾","a":"hyperlipidemia-pharmacology-ezetimibe-secondary-causes","k":["Hyperlipidemia Pharmacology — Ezetimibe & Secondary Causes▾","Ezetimibe (Zetia) — Cholesterol Absorption Inhibitor","Blocks the small intestine's ability to absorb cholesterol, from both dietary intake and bile.","A second-line therapy to statins — sometimes given in combination (a combined statin + ezetimibe pill exists, though it's typically expensive); combination therapy produces a greater LDL decrease than either drug alone.","Diet — Especially saturated/trans fat intake.","Medications — Diuretics, glucocorticoids, cyclosporine, and amiodarone can raise cholesterol.","Disease — Bile Duct Obstruction — Cholesterol is normally secreted into bile; a blocked/obstructed bile duct raises cholesterol levels.","Altered Metabolism — Hypothyroidism, obesity, and pregnancy can all raise LDL and triglycerides.","Other second-line agents you don't need to know in detail: bile acid sequestrants, niacin (sometimes combined with statins), fibric acid derivatives, and fish oil (available OTC — worth asking patients about). Statins remain the gold standa…"]},{"s":"Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms▾","a":"blood-pressure-regulation-the-ras-system-other-mechanisms","k":["Blood Pressure Regulation — The RAS System & Other Mechanisms▾","The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAS) is the primary hormonal driver of blood pressure, with two endpoints: sodium/water retention and vasoconstriction. It's a normal, appropriate physiologic response to a drop in blood pressure/vo…","The RAS Cascade","1. Decreased renal perfusion (low blood volume/pressure) triggers the kidney to release renin.","2. Renin converts angiotensinogen (released by the liver) into angiotensin I.","3. Angiotensin I travels to the lungs, where ACE (angiotensin-converting enzyme) converts it to angiotensin II — a potent vasoconstrictor.","4. Angiotensin II acts on the adrenal glands to release aldosterone, which causes the kidney (nephron) to retain sodium and water — \"salt sucks,\" water follows salt — raising blood volume and blood pressure.","Chronic stress, hypersensitivity to angiotensin II, or being a high renin-secretor can push this system into an inappropriate, pathologic overactivation. Other blood-pressure mechanisms: arterial baroreceptors (carotid sinus, aorta, left ve…"]},{"s":"Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes▾","a":"hypertension-basics-classification-primary-secondary-causes","k":["Hypertension Basics — Classification, Primary & Secondary Causes▾","Hypertension = high (hyper-) blood pressure (tension). It affects about 1 in 3 people in the US and is often called the \"silent killer\" because it's usually asymptomatic. The 2019 AHA guidelines lowered treatment thresholds after evidence s…","Normal — Below 120 and — Below 80","Elevated — 120–129 and — Below 80","Hypertension Stage 1 — 130–139 or — 80–89","Hypertension Stage 2 — Above 140 or — Above 90","Hypertensive Crisis — Above 180 and/or — Above 120","Watch the \"and\" vs. \"or\": Normal and Elevated require both numbers to qualify. Stage 1 and Stage 2 only need one number to qualify — e.g., a systolic of 120 (which alone reads as Elevated) paired with a diastolic of 85 makes the patient Sta…","Primary (Essential) Hypertension","~90–95% of cases; no single known cause — usually idiopathic, from a complex mix of genetics and environment affecting the sympathetic nervous system, the RAS system, and natriuretic peptides.","Risk factors: smoking, excess sodium, sedentary lifestyle, hyperlipidemia, stress, family history/genetics, obesity, age (over 55 for males, over 60 for females), insulin resistance, high alcohol intake.","Secondary Hypertension","Has a known, identifiable cause — renal disease, adrenal cortical or medullary tumors (pheochromocytoma), or drug-induced (oral contraceptives, corticosteroids, antihistamines, cocaine, amphetamines).","Treated by treating/removing the underlying cause."]},{"s":"Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis▾","a":"hypertension-long-term-complications-hypertensive-crisis","k":["Hypertension — Long-Term Complications & Hypertensive Crisis▾","Hypertension is usually asymptomatic until there's end-organ damage — this is why it's screened for routinely rather than waiting for symptoms.","Heart — Left ventricular hypertrophy (from the increased workload), increased myocardial oxygen demand, accelerated atherosclerosis, increased risk of aortic aneurysm.","Kidneys — A primary cause of end-stage renal disease.","Brain — Higher risk of stroke, aneurysm, and hemorrhage.","Eyes — Retinopathy, blindness.","Lower Extremities — Gangrene, intermittent claudication.","Hypertensive crisis (systolic ≥ 180 and/or diastolic ≥ 120) splits into two severities. Hypertensive urgency: no signs of end-organ damage — treat with oral medication, lowering blood pressure gradually over hours to days. Hypertensive emer…"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-diuretics","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Diuretics▾","Diuretics lower blood pressure by increasing urinary output, decreasing circulating volume, and decreasing arterial resistance — decreasing that volume decreases cardiac output. They're inexpensive and commonly first-line.","Thiazide Diuretics(hydrochlorothiazide/HCTZ) — First-line for mild hypertension. Works on the distal convoluted tubule, inhibiting sodium/potassium/chloride reabsorption and relaxing arterioles (decreasing peripheral vascular resistance). P…","Loop Diuretics(furosemide/Lasix) — Inhibits sodium reabsorption at the loop of Henle — a much stronger diuretic effect than thiazides. Also used for fluid volume overload unrelated to blood pressure (common in the ICU), and works even with …","Potassium-Sparing Diuretics(spironolactone/Aldactone) — An aldosterone antagonist — blocks aldosterone's action, so sodium/water are excreted but potassium is retained (spared). A mild diuretic on its own, often added to a thiazide/loop reg…","Nursing considerations across diuretics: monitor potassium levels closely (critical for cardiac rhythm — both hypo- and hyperkalemia can cause dysrhythmias); with thiazides/loops, consider potassium supplementation and potassium-rich foods …"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-sympatholytics","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — Sympatholytics▾","This class works by blocking the sympathetic nervous system, decreasing vasoconstriction and peripheral vascular resistance.","Beta Blockers(metoprolol, propranolol, carvedilol) — Suffix \"-olol.\" Decrease heart force, heart rate, and renin secretion; increase nitric oxide (vasodilation). Beta-1 receptors are cardioselective (heart — \"1 heart\"); beta-2 receptors are…","Alpha-2 Agonists(clonidine) — Centrally acting — decreases sympathetic outflow to both alpha and beta receptors, causing bradycardia, decreased cardiac output, and vasodilation. Not first-line (high side-effect profile); given PO or transde…","Alpha-1 Blockers(doxazosin) — Directly blocks the sympathetic nervous system, decreasing peripheral vascular resistance (venous and arterial dilation). Not first-line. Side effects: hypotension, dizziness. (Tamsulosin/Flomax is also an alph…","Never stop beta blockers or clonidine abruptly — doing so can cause rebound hypertension, a critical, dangerous rise in blood pressure with high risk for stroke or death. Wean the dose down gradually instead. Also: always take blood pressur…","Other notes: beta blockers can mask hypoglycemia (tachycardia is a common hypoglycemia symptom, but a beta blocker keeps heart rate normal) — use cautiously in diabetes. Beta blockers are also used for angina, arrhythmias, heart failure, mi…"]},{"s":"Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators▾","a":"antihypertensive-pharmacology-ras-blockers-calcium-channel-blockers-vasodilators","k":["Antihypertensive Pharmacology — RAS Blockers, Calcium Channel Blockers & Vasodilators▾","ACE Inhibitors(captopril, lisinopril) — Suffix \"-pril.\" Block angiotensin-converting enzyme, so angiotensin II (a vasoconstrictor) and aldosterone (water retention) are both reduced. First-line for hypertension, also used in heart failure; …","ARBs(losartan) — Suffix \"-sartan.\" Block angiotensin II's action at its receptor (rather than blocking its production) — vasodilation, decreased peripheral vascular resistance, increased sodium/water excretion, without affecting heart rate.…","Renin Inhibitor(aliskiren) — Directly inhibits renin, causing vasodilation, decreased blood volume, decreased sympathetic activation. PO only. Long half-life — takes several weeks for full effect. Not for use in pregnancy; watch for hyperka…","Calcium Channel Blockers(nifedipine, amlodipine, nicardipine, verapamil, diltiazem) — Block calcium channels, causing vasodilation of arterial (not venous) smooth muscle. Nifedipine/amlodipine/nicardipine are used mainly for hypertension (n…","Vasodilator(hydralazine) — Works directly on arterial and venous smooth muscle, causing relaxation and decreasing systemic/peripheral vascular resistance. PO or IV (IV for emergencies or when PO isn't tolerated). Side effects: hypotension, …","ACE inhibitors carry a risk of angioedema — swelling of the deep skin layers, around the eyes/lips, and critically the larynx, which can close the airway. This is rare overall (0.1–0.7%) but occurs more often in African American/Black patie…","ACE inhibitor side effects to also expect: first-dose hypotension (a 15–20% BP drop in the first 6–8 hours — advise taking the first dose at home, not before driving or operating machinery), a dry, persistent, non-productive cough (a common…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-diabetes-metabolic-syndrome.html","t":"Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas","Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade","Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria","Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes","Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia","Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease","Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk","Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration","Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology","Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon","Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents","Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement","Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology","Metabolic Syndrome","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: glucose regulation and the insulin-deficiency cascade, diagnosing and classifying diabetes, acute and chronic complications, insulin pharmacology and administration, oral/injectable antidiabetic agents, obesity, an…"]},{"s":"Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas▾","a":"glucose-regulation-basics-carbs-the-liver-the-pancreas","k":["Glucose Regulation Basics — Carbs, the Liver & the Pancreas▾","Carbohydrates are broken down into glucose, raising blood glucose; insulin is secreted in response and lowers it back to baseline. The liver regulates glucose storage and release: glycogenesis converts excess blood glucose into stored glyco…","The pancreas is both an exocrine gland (secretes digestive enzymes — amylase, lipase — through the pancreatic duct, not the bloodstream) and an endocrine gland (secretes hormones directly into the blood). The islets of Langerhans are the pa…","Alpha Cells — Secrete glucagon in response to low blood sugar — triggers glycogenolysis, raising blood glucose.","Beta Cells — Secrete insulin (and amylin) — lowers blood glucose by promoting cellular glucose uptake in skeletal muscle, cardiac muscle, and adipose tissue, and promotes glucose storage as glycogen in the liver. Also inhibits fat breakdown…","Glucose counter-regulation is the body's defense against hypoglycemia — a negative feedback loop where insulin (lowers glucose) works against a group of counter-regulatory hormones that raise it: glucagon, epinephrine, glucocorticoids, and …"]},{"s":"Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade▾","a":"insulin-deficiency-the-self-feeding-cascade","k":["Insulin Deficiency — The Self-Feeding Cascade▾","When there isn't enough insulin, glucose can't get into cells even though blood glucose is high (hyperglycemia) — cells are starving despite the excess sugar circulating around them. The body compensates with two other processes, both of wh…","Lipolysis (Fat Breakdown)","Fat is broken down for energy instead of glucose, releasing free fatty acids.","Unused free fatty acids convert to cholesterol/phospholipids (contributing to atherosclerosis) or are broken down to acetyl CoA, which the liver turns into acetoacetic acid → ketone bodies.","Rising ketones cause ketosis, which can progress to severe metabolic acidosis and coma.","Proteolysis (Protein Breakdown)","Protein synthesis stops and protein catabolism increases, releasing amino acids the cells use for energy.","Results in muscle wasting, multiple organ dysfunction, amino acidemia, and an increased BUN.","More common in type 1 diabetes (complete insulin deficiency) than type 2.","Osmotic diuresis & dehydration: excess blood glucose raises serum osmolality, pulling water out of cells and into the bloodstream (intracellular dehydration). Once blood glucose exceeds the renal threshold (~200 mg/dL), the kidneys can't re…"]},{"s":"Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria▾","a":"diabetes-symptoms-diagnostic-criteria","k":["Diabetes — Symptoms & Diagnostic Criteria▾","Diabetes is characterized by hyperglycemia from an absolute or relative insulin deficiency. Clinically, this presents as the classic three P's: polyuria (excessive urination, from osmotic diuresis), polydipsia (excessive thirst, from dehydr…","Hemoglobin A1c — ~5–5.5% — 5.7–6.4% — ≥ 6.5%","Fasting Plasma Glucose — ≤ 99 mg/dL — 100–125 mg/dL — ≥ 126 mg/dL","Oral Glucose Tolerance Test — Measures blood glucose at set intervals after a large glucose load — most often used to screen for gestational diabetes.","A1c reflects average blood glucose over the last 2–3 months (the percentage of hemoglobin with glucose attached) — a better long-term picture than a single fasting glucose value. The three main types of diabetes are type 1, type 2, and gest…"]},{"s":"Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","a":"type-1-vs-type-2-diabetes","k":["Type 1 vs. Type 2 Diabetes▾","Type 1 Diabetes","Most common pediatric chronic disease, usually diagnosed around age 12; adult-onset type 1 exists but is often misdiagnosed as type 2.","Usually autoimmune (T cells destroy the beta cells that make insulin), sometimes idiopathic; genetic predisposition + environmental triggers; slowly progressive.","Beta cells cannot regenerate — once destroyed, they're gone. About 80–90% of beta cells must be destroyed before insulin secretion noticeably declines.","Complete lack of endogenous insulin. ~5–10% of U.S. diabetes cases. Previously called insulin-dependent or juvenile-onset diabetes.","Symptoms: the three P's, plus weight loss/fatigue more than in type 2.","Type 2 Diabetes","Attributed to genetic and environmental factors; strongly associated with long-term obesity.","Primarily insulin resistance (cells/insulin receptors lose sensitivity, so glucose can't enter despite insulin being present), with possible decreased insulin secretion as beta cells become exhausted over time.","Risk factors: age, obesity, hypertension, poor diet, physical inactivity, family history.","Symptoms are vague/nonspecific — recurrent infections, visual changes, prolonged wound healing, fatigue. The three P's are less common than in type 1.","ADA recommends screening all adults ≥ 35 regardless of risk factors (earlier if risk factors present).","In type 2 diabetes, insulin resistance triggers compensatory hyperinsulinemia (the body makes more insulin to try to overcome the resistance) — those high insulin levels stimulate lipogenesis (fat creation) and inhibit fat breakdown. The li…"]},{"s":"Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia▾","a":"acute-complications-dka-hhns-hypoglycemia","k":["Acute Complications — DKA, HHNS & Hypoglycemia▾","Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) — Most common acute complication in type 1 diabetes. Severe hyperglycemia (blood sugar in the 400–600s), acidosis from ketone bodies, ketonuria, and possible coma. Can be fatal — treated as an emergency.","Hyperosmolar Hyperglycemic Syndrome (HHNS) — Primarily in type 2 diabetes. Extremely high hyperglycemia (blood sugar in the 600–800s) causing severe osmolality-driven fluid shifts. Results from a less profound insulin deficiency than DKA, b…","Hypoglycemia — Occurs in both types — typically blood sugar below ~55–60 mg/dL, usually from too much insulin/medication relative to food intake. A patient chronically running high (e.g., ~250 mg/dL) can feel symptomatic at a much higher gl…","Hypoglycemia is a true emergency. Signs: cold sweats, pallor, tachycardia, hunger, restlessness, anxiety — and if untreated, it can progress to seizures or coma. If a patient shows these symptoms, check the blood sugar immediately — it's ea…"]},{"s":"Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease▾","a":"chronic-complications-microvascular-disease","k":["Chronic Complications — Microvascular Disease▾","Chronic complications result from poorly controlled diabetes — mainly chronic hyperglycemia, which produces advanced glycation end products, free fatty acids, ketone bodies, and abnormal amino acid pathways that damage tissue. Microvascular…","Diabetic Neuropathy — Most common microvascular complication. Progressive peripheral nervous system disorder — ischemia, inflammation, and demyelination cause delayed nerve conduction, sensory/motor loss (pain, temperature, vibration sensat…","Diabetic Retinopathy — Leading cause of blindness worldwide; common in both types. Risk rises with duration of diabetes and poor glucose control, and with comorbidities like hypertension/dyslipidemia. Damaged retinal vessels → hemorrhage an…","Diabetic Nephropathy — Diabetes is the leading cause of chronic kidney disease/end-stage renal disease worldwide. Hyperglycemia injures the glomeruli, progressively thickening the glomerular basement membrane and allowing protein to leak in…"]},{"s":"Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk▾","a":"chronic-complications-macrovascular-disease-infection-risk","k":["Chronic Complications — Macrovascular Disease & Infection Risk▾","Macrovascular disease involves lesions in large/medium arteries — common in type 2 diabetes (but possible in type 1). Thought to result from oxidative stress, endothelial dysfunction, altered mineral metabolism, and increased cytokine produ…","Diabetes and infection are a dangerous combination — people with diabetes are more prone to cystitis, enteric infections, skin/soft tissue infections, appendicitis, pneumonia, and ear infections, due to immune dysfunction, decreased blood s…"]},{"s":"Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration▾","a":"insulin-pharmacology-types-onset-peak-duration","k":["Insulin Pharmacology — Types, Onset, Peak & Duration▾","The overall goal of diabetes pharmacology is to prevent hyperglycemia and reduce long-term microvascular/macrovascular complications, by maintaining tight glycemic control (target blood glucose ~80–140 mg/dL, A1c < 6.5%) and healthy lipid l…","Rapid-Acting(insulin lispro) — 15 min — ~1 hr — 2–4 hr — Given with meals to control postprandial glucose rise — administer as the patient starts eating (giving it earlier risks hypoglycemia if the meal is delayed). Often paired with an int…","Short-Acting(insulin regular) — 30–60 min — 2–6 hr — 3–8 hr — Given before meals for postprandial control. The only insulin that can be given IV (bolus or infusion) or IM — used in insulin pumps and IV infusions (e.g., DKA, tube feeding) wh…","Intermediate-Acting(NPH / insulin isophane suspension) — 2–4 hr — 4–10 hr — 10–20 hr — A cloudy suspension — agitate/roll gently before drawing up. Typically given twice daily with meals. Often combined with regular/rapid insulin in the sam…","Long-Acting(insulin glargine / Lantus) — ~70 min — No peak — 24 hr — Once-daily dosing, often at night, for steady all-day coverage. Never mix with other insulins in the same syringe.","Clear before cloudy: when drawing up rapid/short-acting insulin (clear) together with NPH (cloudy) in the same syringe, always draw the clear insulin first, then the cloudy — this prevents contaminating the short-acting vial with longer-act…"]},{"s":"Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology▾","a":"insulin-administration-regimens-technology","k":["Insulin Administration, Regimens & Technology▾","Insulin is dosed in units, not milligrams or milliliters, and must be drawn up with an insulin-specific syringe — mixing up a standard syringe with an insulin syringe is a known, serious medication error (1 mL of U-100 insulin = 100 units).…","Before giving any insulin, get a current blood sugar — not one from an hour ago or earlier in the day, but immediately before the dose (finger-stick/FSBG). This is one of the most important safety rules in diabetes nursing care.","Sliding Scale — Regular insulin given with meals and at bedtime (charted as AC and HS) — dose is based on the blood glucose reading at that moment, per a provider-defined scale (e.g., 2 units for BG 150–200, 8 units for BG 303). Often combi…","Insulin Pumps — Computerized devices delivering a continuous basal infusion of regular insulin, plus patient-triggered bolus doses calculated from carbohydrate intake. Mostly used in type 1, but also in type 2. Many still require a finger-s…","Continuous Glucose Monitors (CGM) — Sensor-based, often paired to a phone, giving real-time glucose readings without repeated finger-sticks.","Certified diabetes educators are a valuable resource for teaching patients — especially children — about insulin regimens like sliding scale."]},{"s":"Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon▾","a":"insulin-complications-hypoglycemia-somogyi-effect-dawn-phenomenon","k":["Insulin Complications — Hypoglycemia, Somogyi Effect & Dawn Phenomenon▾","Signs of hypoglycemia from too much insulin: tachycardia, sweating, nervousness, headache, drowsiness, and — if severe — decreased level of consciousness. Causes beyond a straightforward insulin overdose: skipped/reduced meals, GI illness (…","Somogyi Effect","Rebound hyperglycemia in the morning caused by excessive insulin at bedtime, which triggers nocturnal hypoglycemia.","The body senses the low blood sugar and releases stored glucose (counter-regulatory response) — overshooting into hyperglycemia. Severe cases can trigger ketosis.","Usually a sign of poor diabetes management (too much basal/bedtime insulin) — needs patient education and dose adjustment.","Dawn Phenomenon","Hyperglycemia in the early morning from the body's natural release of cortisol and growth hormone.","Cortisol raises blood sugar — a normal physiologic pattern, not an insulin-dosing error.","Usually less extreme than the Somogyi effect.","Other insulin complications: rare allergic reactions (less common now that insulin is chemically synthesized), and lipodystrophy/lipoatrophy — skin depression, hardening, or discoloration at injection sites from repeated use of the same spo…"]},{"s":"Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents▾","a":"oral-injectable-antidiabetic-agents","k":["Oral & Injectable Antidiabetic Agents▾","Sulfonylureas(glipizide, glyburide) — Suffix \"-ide.\" First-line oral agent. Closes potassium-ATP channels in beta cells, stimulating insulin secretion (requires some remaining beta cell function — type 2 only), increases insulin sensitivity…","Biguanides(metformin) — First-line for type 2 diabetes (FDA-approved 1994). Stops hepatic conversion of glycogen to glucose and enhances glucose uptake/use by muscle. Does not stimulate insulin release, so it doesn't typically cause hypogly…","DPP-4 Inhibitors (\"Gliptins\")(linagliptin, saxagliptin, sitagliptin) — Suffix \"-gliptin.\" Inhibits DPP-4, the enzyme that breaks down incretin hormones (GLP-1, gastric inhibitory peptide) — letting them last longer, which increases insulin …","GLP-1 Receptor Agonists(dulaglutide, exenatide, semaglutide) — Suffix \"-tide.\" Act on the incretin (GLP-1) receptor: stimulate insulin secretion, reduce postprandial glucagon, slow gastric emptying, increase satiety. Usually adjunct therapy…","SGLT2 Inhibitors(dapagliflozin) — Suffix \"-flozin.\" Blocks the kidney's SGLT2 transporters, which normally reabsorb ~90% of filtered glucose — blocked glucose is excreted in urine instead, lowering blood glucose. Oral, type 2 only. Side eff…","Glucagon — The hypoglycemia antidote. Oral tablet preferred for an awake, swallowing patient; IM/IV/subcutaneous for decreased level of consciousness. Activates liver glucagon receptors, triggering glycogenolysis. Short half-life (25–35 min…","GLP-1 = Gila monster: GLP-1 receptor agonists (the \"-tide\" drugs) were derived from a peptide in Gila monster saliva — after clinicians noticed people bitten by the venomous lizard became hypoglycemic. Gila starts with G, just like GLP-1.","Other considerations: steroids raise blood sugar — a diabetic patient started on steroids (e.g., for a sinus infection) may need their insulin regimen increased. Illness raises blood sugar (stress response) and can push a patient toward DKA…"]},{"s":"Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement▾","a":"obesity-adipose-tissue-distribution-measurement","k":["Obesity — Adipose Tissue, Distribution & Measurement▾","About 74% of U.S. adults ≥ 20 are overweight or obese (42% obese, 10% severely obese) — a costly problem (over $147 billion in obesity-related medical costs in 2008) associated with increased risk across most major disease categories. Adipo…","Subcutaneous (Peripheral) — Just under the skin — the \"pinchable\" fat. Considered the healthier storage type; less inflammatory.","Visceral — Stored between abdominal organs (e.g., the omentum). More hormonally active than subcutaneous fat — associated with impaired lipid/glucose metabolism, insulin resistance, metabolic syndrome, increased cancer risk, and cardiovascu…","Android obesity (\"apple\" shape, more common in males) concentrates fat in the abdomen — more likely visceral fat, carrying higher risk of coronary artery disease, hypertension, stroke, and diabetes. Gynoid obesity (\"pear\" shape, more common…","BMI (adults) — Weight (kg) ÷ height² (m), or weight (lb) ÷ height² (in) × 703. Obesity = BMI > 30 kg/m².","BMI (children) — Obesity = BMI ≥ the age/sex-specific 95th percentile on growth charts.","Body Fat % — A more precise gauge than BMI alone (e.g., accounts for a muscular person with a high BMI but low body fat). Ideal: women ≤ 30%, men ≤ 25%.","Obesity is the fifth leading cause of death in the U.S. and a risk factor for type 2 diabetes, cardiovascular disease, cancer, hypertension, GERD, arthritis, infection, and sleep apnea. It's polygenic (multiple genes involved — e.g., leptin…"]},{"s":"Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology▾","a":"obesity-adipokines-gi-hormones-pharmacology","k":["Obesity — Adipokines, GI Hormones & Pharmacology▾","Adipose tissue functions as an endocrine organ, secreting adipokines — cell-signaling proteins regulating appetite, energy expenditure, lipid storage, insulin secretion/sensitivity, and coagulation.","Adiponectin — A \"good\" adipokine — inversely related to body fat (decreases as adiposity increases, especially visceral fat; resistance can also develop). Increases energy expenditure, improves insulin sensitivity, has anti-inflammatory and…","Leptin — A \"good\" adipokine made by adipocytes — rises with fat mass (positive relationship). Signals satiety and increases energy expenditure after eating; low levels (fasting) stimulate intake and reduce expenditure. With obesity, chronic…","Ghrelin, GLP-1 & Peptide YY — GI-derived hormones that normally promote satiety/fullness (ghrelin also rises with fasting). All three are decreased in obesity, contributing to reduced satiety signaling.","Cholecystokinin (CCK) — Stimulates gallbladder contraction and pancreatic enzyme release; increases satiety, reduces food intake, slows gastric emptying. Research on its level in obesity is mixed (traditionally decreased, some newer studies…","Obesity also causes a chronic low-grade inflammatory state — immune cells infiltrate adipose tissue and release cytokines (TNF-alpha, IL-6) that circulate systemically, contributing to insulin resistance and metabolic syndrome.","Orlistat — Pharmacologic Treatment for Obesity","Over-the-counter. Binds/blocks gastric and pancreatic enzymes, reducing dietary fat absorption by up to 30%.","Black box warning: liver injury — avoid in patients with existing liver disease.","Side effects (from blocked fat absorption): oily spotting, flatulence, fecal incontinence — advise reducing fat intake to < 30% of daily calories to help. Also decreases fat-soluble vitamin absorption (A, D, E, beta-carotene) — patients nee…","Takes about 3 months to see effect; minimally effective without diet/exercise; significant weight rebound after stopping — it's a long-term treatment, not a short course.","Drug therapy is generally reserved for BMI > 30, or > 27 with risk factors (after ~6 months of unsuccessful diet/exercise alone). Other FDA-approved obesity medications exist (phentermine, liraglutide, naltrexone/bupropion) but aren't detai…"]},{"s":"Metabolic Syndrome▾","a":"metabolic-syndrome","k":["Metabolic Syndrome▾","Metabolic syndrome is a cluster of risk factors that together substantially raise the risk of stroke and cardiovascular disease. A patient is considered to have metabolic syndrome with three or more of: elevated waist circumference (> 40 in…","Management treats each component individually (antihypertensives for high blood pressure, lipid-lowering agents for high triglycerides, antidiabetic therapy for high glucose) alongside counseling on weight loss, exercise, healthy diet, and …"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-coagulation-dvt.html","t":"Coagulation & DVT","secs":[{"s":"Coagulation & DVT","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis","The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown","Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance","Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)","Warfarin (Coumadin)","Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban","Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin","Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors","DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad","DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications","DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: blood/plasma basics and platelets, the coagulation cascade and hemostasis, anticoagulant pharmacology (heparin, enoxaparin, warfarin, factor Xa inhibitors), antiplatelet pharmacology, and deep vein thrombosis/venou…"]},{"s":"Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis▾","a":"blood-basics-plasma-platelets-hemostasis","k":["Blood Basics — Plasma, Platelets & Hemostasis▾","Whole blood is about 55% plasma and 45% formed elements (red blood cells, white blood cells, platelets) — and blood itself is only about 8% of total body fluid. Plasma is about 92% water, plus plasma proteins — most of which are made in the…","Albumin — ~57% of plasma proteins — a carrier molecule whose most important role is regulating water/solute movement through capillaries (drives oncotic pressure).","Globulins — Includes HDL, prothrombin (a clotting factor), and hormone-transport proteins.","Clotting Factors — Fibrinogen is the most plentiful. Serum = plasma minus clotting factors.","Formed elements include erythrocytes (RBCs, carry oxygen), leukocytes (WBCs — neutrophils for early inflammation/phagocytosis, eosinophils for allergic/parasitic response, basophils for allergic reactions, monocytes/macrophages for phagocyt…","Platelets","Not true cells — anucleate cytoplasmic fragments made in the bone marrow (with additional storage in the spleen).","Normal count: 150,000–400,000. Thrombocytopenia is generally < 100,000 — high bleeding risk.","Platelet activation: a resting platelet is smooth; on sensing vessel injury, it transitions to a spiky (\"dendritic\") shape, becomes sticky, and adheres to the injury site — then platelets aggregate with each other and with the vessel wall, …"]},{"s":"The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown▾","a":"the-coagulation-cascade-clot-formation-breakdown","k":["The Coagulation Cascade — Clot Formation & Breakdown▾","The coagulation cascade (clotting system/coagulation system — same thing) is a group of proteins that activate in sequence to form a blood clot. Platelets are the primary initiators of the cascade. The cascade can be triggered through the e…","Antithrombin III — A circulating inhibitor of thrombin — stops thrombin from converting fibrinogen to fibrin, halting the clotting process.","Tissue Factor Pathway Inhibitor (TFPI) — Produced by platelets and endothelial cells — inhibits factor Xa, stopping further clot activation.","Deficiencies in these natural stop mechanisms can cause bleeding disorders. Once the vessel has healed, the clot itself is removed by fibrinolysis (\"lysis\" = breakdown), carried out by the fibrinolytic system: tissue plasminogen activator (…"]},{"s":"Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance▾","a":"anticoagulant-vs-antiplatelet-therapy-overview-bleeding-surveillance","k":["Anticoagulant vs. Antiplatelet Therapy — Overview & Bleeding Surveillance▾","Anticoagulants","Inhibit the action/formation of clotting factors.","Prevent a clot from forming, or prevent an existing clot from extending — but have no direct effect on an existing clot (don't break it up).","Antiplatelets","Inhibit platelet aggregation, preventing the platelet plug from forming.","Best for preventing heart attack and stroke.","Both classes are considered antithrombotic (prevent clot/thrombus formation), just by different mechanisms. Common indications: atrial fibrillation, heart disease/heart failure, history of DVT, prolonged immobility (e.g., ortho surgery), an…","The universal concern with anticoagulant/antiplatelet therapy is bleeding — internal (brain bleed, GI bleed — may not be obvious until it's an emergency) or external (prolonged bleeding from a cut, gum bleeding with a hard-bristle toothbrus…"]},{"s":"Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)▾","a":"heparin-low-molecular-weight-heparin-enoxaparin","k":["Heparin & Low-Molecular-Weight Heparin (Enoxaparin)▾","Heparin — Activates antithrombin, which indirectly inactivates both thrombin and factor Xa, inhibiting fibrin formation. Given IV or subcutaneously. Derived from pig lung/intestinal mucosa (may raise kosher/halal considerations; pig livesto…","Enoxaparin (Lovenox) — Low-molecular-weight heparin — cleaved into smaller fragments than heparin, giving greater factor Xa affinity, better bioavailability, and a longer half-life. Inactivates factor Xa only (not thrombin). Subcutaneous on…","Shared adverse effects: bleeding and thrombocytopenia (including HIT — see below). Use both cautiously with spinal/epidural anesthesia (hematoma risk — monitor for neurologic changes); enoxaparin carries a black box warning for spinal hemat…","Heparin safety: if giving protamine sulfate (the heparin/enoxaparin antidote — binds heparin, inactivating its anticoagulant effect) IV, push it slowly — too fast can cause profound hypotension. Also watch for heparin-induced thrombocytopen…"]},{"s":"Warfarin (Coumadin)▾","a":"warfarin-coumadin","k":["Warfarin (Coumadin)▾","Warfarin is a vitamin K inhibitor — it blocks vitamin K's role in synthesizing several coagulation factors, so those factors aren't made. Given PO only, once daily, typically in the evening. Onset is delayed ~24 hours after the dose, but du…","Adverse Effects — Bleeding, lethargy, muscle pain, and — notably — purple toes.","Antidote — Vitamin K (IV) — restores the substrate warfarin blocks. Fresh frozen plasma or whole blood can also replace the missing clotting factors directly.","Lab Monitoring — PT/INR. A person not on warfarin has an INR around 1 or less; the therapeutic range on warfarin is about 2–3.5 (goal varies by indication). Checked at baseline, then monthly once therapeutic; dose adjusted based on trend.","Pre-Op Holding — Held before surgery (including dental procedures) — anywhere from ~72 hours up to 2 weeks depending on the procedure and the patient's INR.","Significant interactions: many common medications (including OTC cimetidine) raise or lower INR — patients need to disclose all medications/supplements. Alcohol also thins the blood. Since warfarin blocks vitamin-K-dependent clotting factor…"]},{"s":"Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban▾","a":"direct-factor-xa-inhibitors-apixaban-rivaroxaban","k":["Direct Factor Xa Inhibitors — Apixaban & Rivaroxaban▾","Apixaban (Eliquis) and rivaroxaban (Xarelto) directly inhibit factor Xa. Used preemptively before knee/hip replacement, to prevent stroke in atrial fibrillation, postoperatively for thromboprophylaxis, and to treat DVT/PE. Side effects: ble…","Interactions — St. John's wort, rifampin, and phenytoin decrease effectiveness. Both are CYP substrates — grapefruit juice and CYP inhibitors increase drug levels/bleeding risk. Don't combine with other anticoagulants.","Monitoring — No routine coagulation lab monitoring needed (a major selling point vs. warfarin) — but monitor liver function, since these drugs can affect it.","Antidote — Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) — recombinant factor Xa; a newer, extremely expensive antidote (early doses ran roughly $50,000), so it's used only when truly needed."]},{"s":"Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin▾","a":"antiplatelet-pharmacology-aspirin","k":["Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Aspirin▾","Aspirin is classified as an NSAID but is the only NSAID used for its antiplatelet effect: it blocks prostaglandin synthesis via the COX pathway, which also blocks platelet aggregation, making platelets less sticky. Used to prevent/treat MI …","For an acute MI, the patient chews a baby aspirin rather than swallowing it whole — and it must be a non-enteric-coated (plain, white) tablet, not the orange enteric-coated pill, since enteric coating is meant to survive intact to the intes…","Side effects: bleeding, nausea/vomiting, drowsiness, confusion. Enteric-coated/extended-release formulations reduce GI upset for routine (non-emergency) use — but never crush an enteric-coated tablet. Daily aspirin for primary cardiovascula…"]},{"s":"Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors▾","a":"antiplatelet-pharmacology-clopidogrel-ticagrelor-direct-thrombin-inhibitors","k":["Antiplatelet Pharmacology — Clopidogrel, Ticagrelor & Direct Thrombin Inhibitors▾","Clopidogrel (Plavix) and ticagrelor (Brilinta) are ADP inhibitors — they alter the platelet membrane so it doesn't receive the aggregation signal, decreasing platelet accumulation. Used to reduce stroke risk, for TIA prophylaxis, and post-M…","Clopidogrel — Black box warning: certain genetic variants reduce conversion to the active metabolite, raising cardiovascular event risk — ask about genetic/family history before starting. Effectiveness is reduced by amiodarone, calcium chan…","Ticagrelor — Black box warning: increased bleeding risk with aspirin doses over 100 mg.","Direct thrombin inhibitors — argatroban and bivalirudin — irreversibly bind and inactivate thrombin, so it can't convert fibrinogen to fibrin. Given IV only; used to treat HIT and in patients undergoing PCI who are at high HIT risk from hep…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad▾","a":"dvt-vte-terminology-and-virchow-s-triad","k":["DVT & VTE — Terminology and Virchow's Triad▾","A thrombus is a blood clot still attached to the vessel wall. Once it detaches, it's a thromboembolus; if it originated in the venous system, it's a venous thromboembolism (VTE). Thrombi form more often in veins than arteries because venous…","Virchow's Triad — Three Factors Promoting Venous Thrombosis","Venous stasis — slow blood flow. Risk: older age, immobility (hospitalized/post-op patients), heart failure.","Venous endothelial damage — injury to the vein lining, from trauma, certain IV medications, or surgery.","Hypercoagulable states — inherited clotting disorders, malignancy, pregnancy, oral contraceptives/hormone replacement, recent surgery, and genetic factors (prothrombin/antithrombin mutations).","Additional risk factors: ortho surgery (especially hip/knee replacement, hip fracture — high immobility, sometimes in casts), spinal cord injury, OB/GYN conditions, obesity, smoking, certain cancers, inflammatory bowel disease, prior person…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications▾","a":"dvt-vte-signs-symptoms-complications","k":["DVT & VTE — Signs, Symptoms & Complications▾","Classic DVT findings: pain, redness, warmth, and swelling in the affected extremity (most often a lower extremity) — always compare the affected limb to the other side. Some patients are entirely asymptomatic. Significant obstruction to ven…","Pulmonary Embolism (PE) — A thrombus lodges in the pulmonary circulation, blocking gas exchange.","Chronic Thromboembolic Pulmonary Hypertension — Rare — repeated pulmonary clots raise pulmonary circulation pressure over time; presents with shortness of breath.","Post-Thrombotic Syndrome — Affects 8–70% of patients — chronic inflammation and venous hypertension from the thrombus blocking flow and damaging the vein wall. Presents with pain, aching, fatigue, swelling sensation, cramps, itching, heavin…","Phlegmasia cerulea dolens (\"painful blue inflammation\") — rare, but a true emergency: major leg veins become totally or near-totally occluded, causing sudden massive swelling, intense deep pain, and severe cyanosis. Left untreated, it can p…"]},{"s":"DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment▾","a":"dvt-vte-prevention-treatment","k":["DVT & VTE — Prevention & Treatment▾","Anticoagulation Therapy — For active DVT/VTE — see the coagulation modifier drugs above.","Mechanical Prevention — Sequential compression devices (SCDs) and TED hose (thromboembolism-deterrent stockings); aggressive/early mobilization of hospitalized and post-op patients.","Lab Monitoring — PT/INR and other bleeding-time labs for at-risk patients.","IVC Filter — An inferior vena cava interruption device — mechanically catches traveling clots before they reach the heart, lungs, or brain.","Embolectomy — Surgical removal of the embolus — effective, but a higher-risk procedure not commonly used."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-blood-cells-cbc.html","t":"Blood Cells & CBC Interpretation","secs":[{"s":"Blood Cells & CBC Interpretation","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit","White Blood Cells — Types & Function","Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left","CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts","Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia","Infectious Mononucleosis","Myelodysplastic Syndrome","Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic","Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin","Multiple Myeloma","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: red and white blood cell basics, CBC/CBC-with-differential interpretation, leukocytosis/leukopenia/neutropenia, hematopoietic growth factors, and white blood cell disorders (mononucleosis, myelodysplastic syndrome,…"]},{"s":"Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit▾","a":"red-blood-cells-hemoglobin-hematocrit","k":["Red Blood Cells, Hemoglobin & Hematocrit▾","Erythrocytes (RBCs) are the most abundant blood cell, shaped like a biconcave disc (thick outer rim, thin center). They carry hemoglobin, which binds oxygen and delivers it to tissues — RBCs are the primary cell responsible for tissue oxyge…","RBC Count — Total erythrocytes in the blood. — ~4.2–6.2 million/mm³","Hemoglobin (Hgb) — Amount of hemoglobin in the blood — reflects oxygen-carrying capacity. Low with bleeding, folate/B12 deficiency, certain cancers, kidney/liver disease. High with polycythemia, COPD, high altitude, heavy smoking. — Males 1…","Hematocrit (Hct) — The percentage of blood volume made up of packed RBCs. Low with anemia, bleeding, or fluid overload (dilution). High with polycythemia, COPD, dehydration, shock, congenital heart disease — since hematocrit is a percentage…","Other RBC labs exist (mean corpuscular volume/MCV — RBC size; mean corpuscular hemoglobin/MCH — hemoglobin amount per RBC by weight) but are used less at the bedside — when an abnormal RBC lab comes back flagged, look it up and connect it t…"]},{"s":"White Blood Cells — Types & Function▾","a":"white-blood-cells-types-function","k":["White Blood Cells — Types & Function▾","Leukocytes (WBCs) exist to protect the body — immune function and inflammation. There are five types, and their percentages always add up to 100%.","\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\" — Neutrophils, Lymphocytes, Monocytes, Eosinophils, Basophils: the five types of white blood cells.","Granulocytes","Have a nucleus with several lobes and granules that release inflammatory/immune mediators.","Neutrophils — most prevalent at a fresh injury/infection site; the first responders.","Eosinophils — role in phagocytosis tied to antigen-antibody reactions; rise with allergic reactions and parasitic infections.","Basophils — role in phagocytosis; rise with allergic/hypersensitivity and inflammatory reactions.","Agranulocytes","Fewer granules, don't release the same inflammatory mediators — but still participate in immune/inflammatory processes.","Lymphocytes (T-cells, B-cells) — primary immunity cells, usually reside in lymphoid tissue rather than circulating blood.","Monocytes/macrophages — potent phagocytosis, clean up cellular waste/debris; monocytes still secrete some cytokines despite being agranulocytes.","Eosinophils increase with \"worms, wheezes, and weird diseases\" — parasitic infections, allergic/asthma-type reactions, and other hypersensitivity conditions."]},{"s":"Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left▾","a":"neutrophils-lymphocytes-shift-to-the-left","k":["Neutrophils & Lymphocytes — Shift to the Left▾","Neutrophils come in two forms: segs (mature, \"segmented\" neutrophils) and bands (immature neutrophils). Neutrophils rise with acute bacterial infection and trauma — in the acute stage, the bone marrow releases more immature bands than usual…","Lymphocytes rise with chronic bacterial infection and acute viral infection — in chronic bacterial infection, T- and B-cells act as memory cells; in acute viral infection, they act as active fighters. Think: neutrophils for something acute …"]},{"s":"CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts▾","a":"cbc-with-differential-interpreting-percentages-vs-absolute-counts","k":["CBC with Differential — Interpreting Percentages vs. Absolute Counts▾","A standard CBC reports WBC count, RBC count, hemoglobin, hematocrit, and platelets. A CBC with differential (\"diff\") goes further, breaking the total WBC count into the proportion of each of the five WBC types — those five percentages alway…","Leukocytosis — Total WBC count above normal (normal ≈ 5,000–10,000/mm³).","Leukopenia — Total WBC count below normal.","Neutropenia — Low neutrophil count specifically, measured by the absolute neutrophil count (ANC).","Because the \"diff\" percentages are relative to one another, a rising percentage in one WBC type mathematically pushes another type's percentage down — even if that second type's absolute count hasn't changed at all. Example: WBC count 10,00…"]},{"s":"Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia▾","a":"leukocytosis-leukopenia-neutropenia","k":["Leukocytosis, Leukopenia & Neutropenia▾","The total WBC count reflects how intense an immune/inflammatory process is — a large elevation suggests a bigger process than a modest one. Fever often accompanies infection and is a protective mechanism (not a disease itself) — it can slow…","Neutropenia (ANC < 1,000, most often from cancer treatment — chemo or radiation) leaves a patient with little to fight infection, making even a routine infection potentially life-threatening. Neutropenic precautions: meticulous hand hygiene…","Hematopoietic growth factors (granulocyte colony-stimulating factors, G-CSF) treat significant leukopenia/neutropenia: filgrastim (Neupogen) is a leukopoietic growth factor made via recombinant DNA technology that promotes granulocyte proli…"]},{"s":"Infectious Mononucleosis▾","a":"infectious-mononucleosis","k":["Infectious Mononucleosis▾","A self-limiting, lymphoproliferative infection of B lymphocytes, most often caused by Epstein-Barr virus (EBV). Most common in adolescents/young adults (college students) — about 1 in 4 teens/young adults who get EBV develop mono. Spread ma…","Clinical Manifestations — Lymphadenopathy (swollen neck/axillary nodes), hepatitis, splenomegaly, extreme fatigue, fever, sore throat, body aches.","Diagnosis — Elevated WBCs, ~95% lymphocytes.","Course — Acute phase ~2–3 weeks; full resolution takes 2–3 months. Avoid contact sports during this time (splenic rupture risk from splenomegaly).","Treatment — No specific treatment — supportive care (antipyretics, rest, hydration). No vaccine; risk reduction means not sharing food/drinks, kissing, or personal items like toothbrushes with an infected person."]},{"s":"Myelodysplastic Syndrome▾","a":"myelodysplastic-syndrome","k":["Myelodysplastic Syndrome▾","A group of related hematologic disorders — also called bone marrow failure disorder — where blood cells made in the bone marrow fail to mature properly, accumulate abnormally in the marrow, or have a shortened lifespan. Most common in older…","Hallmark finding: cytopenias (low counts across blood cell types), which can produce anemia, infection, and spontaneous bleeding/bruising. Diagnosed via labs and bone marrow biopsy. Treatment depends on severity — mild/chronic low-grade ane…"]},{"s":"Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic▾","a":"leukemias-acute-vs-chronic-lymphocytic-vs-myelocytic","k":["Leukemias — Acute vs. Chronic, Lymphocytic vs. Myelocytic▾","Leukemias are malignant neoplasms of immature, undifferentiated cells derived from a single hematopoietic cell line — these leukemic cells proliferate in the bone marrow, circulate in the blood, and infiltrate the spleen/lymph nodes. Classi…","Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia (ALL) — Most common childhood leukemia (~80% of childhood acute leukemias); peak age 3–7. 5-year survival: ~71.7% overall, ~91.9% in children.","Acute Myelocytic Leukemia (AML) — Primarily an adult disease (median age 64; ~80% of adult acute leukemia cases). 5-year survival: ~29.4% adults, ~68.7% children.","Chronic Lymphocytic Leukemia (CLL) — Most common leukemia in older adults; ~30% of all U.S. leukemia cases. Signs: fatigue, weight loss, anorexia, recurrent infections (leukemic B cells don't make normal antibodies). 5-year survival: ~88.2%…","Chronic Myelocytic Leukemia (CML) — ~15% of U.S. leukemia cases; typical onset age 40–50 (can occur in children). High granulocyte count and splenomegaly; signs include fatigue, weight loss, diaphoresis, bleeding, abdominal discomfort. 5-ye…","Acute leukemias have a sudden, \"stormy\" onset with signs tied to decreased mature WBCs/RBCs/platelets — bone pain, bruising, fever, fatigue, infection, unexplained pain (in children, sometimes a sudden refusal to walk). Diagnosed when immat…","Treatment aims for remission: cytotoxic chemotherapy first (to destroy rapidly dividing cells), followed in some cases by bone marrow/stem cell transplant (donor types: volunteer donor, identical twin, or the patient's own cells; typically …"]},{"s":"Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin▾","a":"malignant-lymphomas-hodgkin-vs-non-hodgkin","k":["Malignant Lymphomas — Hodgkin vs. Non-Hodgkin▾","Lymphomas are neoplasms of cells derived from lymphoid tissue.","Hodgkin Disease (Hodgkin Lymphoma)","~30% of malignant lymphomas; occurs across all ages.","Painless, progressive, rubbery enlargement of a single lymph node or group — most often cervical (neck).","Diagnostic hallmark: Reed-Sternberg cells (originate from B cells) on biopsy.","Spreads/grows in a predictable pattern — this predictability supports a relatively good prognosis.","Other findings: fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise. Treated with chemotherapy, radiation, or stem cell transplant.","Non-Hodgkin Lymphoma","No Reed-Sternberg cells. Can arise from lymph nodes or any lymphoid tissue; most common in older adults.","Spread is unpredictable and often early to liver, spleen, bone marrow — less certain prognosis, often diagnosed at a later, more widespread stage.","Painless lymph node enlargement plus extranodal involvement (skin, GI tract, bone/bone marrow), fever, night sweats, weight loss, malaise, itchiness.","Treated with chemotherapy, radiation, stem cell transplant (refractory cases), or immune-modulating drugs (e.g., rituximab)."]},{"s":"Multiple Myeloma▾","a":"multiple-myeloma","k":["Multiple Myeloma▾","Also called plasma cell myeloma — a malignant disorder of mature, antibody-secreting B lymphocytes (plasma cells). Produces abnormal immunoglobulins/fragments, including a monoclonal antibody (M protein), and increases osteoclast activity, …","Epidemiology — Typically diagnosed at age 65+; more common in African American/Black patients than white/Caucasian patients. Unknown etiology.","Presentation — Slow, insidious onset. Bone pain is usually the first symptom (sometimes discovered via a fracture or back pain workup); also anemia, recurrent infections, and bleeding (bone marrow depression); hypercalcemia (from bone break…","Diagnosis — Labs, imaging, bone marrow exam. Findings: monoclonal antibody protein in serum/urine, pancytopenia (all blood counts low), hypercalcemia, Bence Jones protein (urine), elevated serum creatinine (kidney involvement), and osteolyt…","Treatment — Ranges from watchful waiting to steroids, chemotherapy, biologic therapy, stem cell transplant, and bisphosphonates; ensure adequate hydration."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-fluid-electrolytes.html","t":"Fluid & Electrolytes","secs":[{"s":"Fluid & Electrolytes","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures","Electrolyte Basics","Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia","Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate","Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia","Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV","Hyperkalemia Treatment","Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement","Calcium Imbalances","Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment","Phosphorus Imbalances","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: fluid compartments and fluid movement (diffusion, osmosis, colloids, hydrostatic/oncotic pressure), electrolyte basics, and imbalances/replacement for sodium, potassium, magnesium, calcium, and phosphorus."]},{"s":"Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures▾","a":"fluid-compartments-movement-pressures","k":["Fluid Compartments, Movement & Pressures▾","About 50–60% of adult body weight is water — fluid containing dissolved/suspended substances like glucose, electrolytes, and proteins. It's stored in two spaces: intracellular (inside cells — about 70% of body fluid) and extracellular (outs…","Osmosis — Water, across a semi-permeable membrane. — Low solute concentration → high solute concentration, until concentrations equalize (or hydrostatic pressure opposes further movement).","Diffusion — Molecules/solutes. — High concentration → low concentration, until concentration is equal. Liquid volume stays the same.","The body constantly works toward homeostasis — balancing solvents (fluid) and solutes (electrolytes/particles). Osmotic pressure drives water across the cell membrane toward the more concentrated side: if solutes build up on one side of a m…","Colloids","Substances that raise colloid osmotic pressure (also called oncotic pressure) and pull fluid from the interstitial compartment into the plasma/blood compartment.","Three primary colloids: albumin (the main one), globulin, and fibrinogen — measured together as total protein level (a measure of oncotic pressure, not an indicator of dietary protein intake).","Colloid oncotic pressure decreases with age and with malnutrition; can be replaced with colloid replacement products (more detail in Med-Surg/critical care).","Hydrostatic Pressure — Blood pressure, at the capillary level. — Pushes fluid out of the vascular system into the interstitial space, supplying nutrients to tissues/cells.","Oncotic (Colloid Osmotic) Pressure — Plasma colloids (mainly albumin). — Pulls fluid into the vascular space from tissue — plasma proteins attract water, which helps remove metabolic waste from cells/tissues."]},{"s":"Electrolyte Basics▾","a":"electrolyte-basics","k":["Electrolyte Basics▾","Electrolytes are substances that carry an electrical charge in solution. They influence fluid balance, acid-base balance, nerve impulses, muscle contraction, heart rhythm, and many other cell functions — and because electrolytes work togeth…","Intracellular — Potassium, magnesium, phosphorus.","Extracellular — Sodium, chloride, bicarbonate, calcium.","Sodium, potassium, calcium, and magnesium are positively charged (cations); phosphorus, chloride, and bicarbonate are negatively charged (anions). Positive and negative charges balance each other out — a process called electroneutrality — w…","Memory hooks for which body system each major electrolyte imbalance tends to hit first: sodium → brain (confusion, altered LOC), potassium → heart (cardiac rhythm disturbances), magnesium → muscle (\"M for magnesium, M for muscle movement\"),…"]},{"s":"Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia▾","a":"sodium-imbalances-hyponatremia-hypernatremia","k":["Sodium Imbalances — Hyponatremia & Hypernatremia▾","Normal sodium: 136–145. Below 136 is hyponatremia; above 145 is hypernatremia. Sodium is the main extracellular cation and the primary driver of fluid balance — \"where salt goes, water follows.\" Sodium governs osmolality (concentration in b…","Hyponatremia (< 136)","Causes: GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, fistulas, NG suction), renal losses (adrenal insufficiency, diuretics), skin losses (burns, wound damage), fasting/not eating or drinking, polydipsia (water intoxication), or excess hypotonic fluid.","Signs/symptoms: confusion and altered LOC (cells swell as water shifts in — brain cells are especially sensitive), anorexia, muscle weakness; if severe/untreated, seizures and coma. CNS symptoms appear first.","Hypernatremia (> 145)","Causes: essentially never dietary intake (near-impossible to eat enough salt) — instead IV fluids/tube feeds high in sodium, saltwater near-drowning, or inadequate water intake/excess water loss (severe diarrhea, high fever, heat stroke, pr…","Signs/symptoms: altered LOC, confusion, seizure, coma, extreme thirst, dry/sticky mucous membranes, muscle cramps — cells shrink as water shifts out.","Hyponatremia specifically breaks down into two causes with opposite fluid pictures, both showing the same CNS symptoms:","Dilutional Hyponatremia — Too much water taken in — sodium is diluted. — Hypervolemia: increased blood pressure, weight gain, rapid/bounding pulse, increased urine specific gravity.","Depletional Hyponatremia — An actual loss of sodium and fluid. — Hypovolemia: decreased blood pressure, tachycardia, dry skin, weight loss, decreased urine specific gravity."]},{"s":"Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate▾","a":"sodium-correction-sodium-bicarbonate","k":["Sodium Correction & Sodium Bicarbonate▾","Sodium imbalances must be corrected slowly — think sodium, think brain, think slow. Correcting hyponatremia too fast (especially one that's lasted > 48 hours) causes brain dehydration and risks osmotic demyelination syndrome, which can lead…","Depletional Hyponatremia — Sodium replacement (PO or IV — often normal saline); withhold diuretics until the imbalance is fixed.","Dilutional Hyponatremia — Fluid restriction instead of sodium replacement — fluid levels come down and sodium concentration corrects.","Hypernatremia (sodium excess) — Remove sodium, via fluids.","Hypernatremia (water loss) — Add water.","Sodium bicarbonate dissociates to provide bicarbonate ions, neutralizing acid concentration and raising blood/urine pH — its main indication is metabolic acidosis, though it also raises plasma sodium concentration. It's reserved for people …"]},{"s":"Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia▾","a":"potassium-imbalances-hypokalemia-hyperkalemia","k":["Potassium Imbalances — Hypokalemia & Hyperkalemia▾","Normal potassium: 3.5–5. Below 3.5 is hypokalemia; above 5 is hyperkalemia. Potassium is an intracellular cation — 98% of the body's potassium lives inside cells, maintained by an ATP-driven active transport pump. It's the main driver of ca…","Hypokalemia (< 3.5)","Causes: renal/GI losses (diarrhea, vomiting, ileostomy drainage, diuresis/furosemide), acid-base disorders (alkalosis shifts potassium from extracellular into intracellular space). Rarely from inadequate dietary intake alone.","Signs/symptoms: cardiac rhythm disturbances (can be lethal if severe), muscle weakness, leg cramps, decreased bowel motility → constipation, nausea, ileus.","Hyperkalemia (> 5)","Causes: decreased potassium output (renal failure, low urine output), massive cell injury releasing intracellular potassium (burns, crush injuries, sepsis), or certain drugs (potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors, ARBs).","Signs/symptoms: peaked T waves on ECG, muscle weakness, cramps, abdominal cramping, diarrhea, vomiting."]},{"s":"Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV▾","a":"potassium-replacement-oral-iv","k":["Potassium Replacement — Oral & IV▾","Potassium chloride (KCl) is given to treat/prevent potassium depletion when dietary measures aren't enough — there's no real \"mechanism of action\" beyond replacing what's missing. Oral forms (powder mixed into liquid, or liquid) taste awful…","IV potassium must always be diluted and can never be pushed IV — undiluted IV potassium can cause ventricular fibrillation, a lethal rhythm incompatible with life. It must be given slowly (usually over an hour or more): max 10 mEq/hr periph…"]},{"s":"Hyperkalemia Treatment▾","a":"hyperkalemia-treatment","k":["Hyperkalemia Treatment▾","Kayexalate (Sodium Polystyrene Sulfonate) — A cation exchange resin — binds potassium in the digestive tract and swaps it for sodium, lowering serum potassium by about 0.5–1 mEq/L over 4–6 hours. Given PO, or rectally as a powder/suspension…","D50 + Regular Insulin — A combination (10 units regular insulin + 1 ampule of 50% dextrose) that shifts potassium temporarily from outside cells to inside — used in urgent/emergent situations (e.g., cardiac dysrhythmia from hyperkalemia) fo…","Insulin's most important safety rule — always check a current blood sugar before the dose — applies here too. With the D50/insulin cocktail, recheck blood sugar about 5 minutes after giving the D50 (or sooner if hypoglycemia symptoms appear…"]},{"s":"Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement▾","a":"magnesium-imbalances-replacement","k":["Magnesium Imbalances & Replacement▾","Normal magnesium: 1.3–2.1. Below 1.3 is hypomagnesemia; above 2.1 is hypermagnesemia. Magnesium and potassium are closely linked — magnesium blocks/controls potassium's movement out of cardiac cells, stabilizing cardiac and smooth muscle.","Hypomagnesemia (< 1.3)","Causes: diuresis, GI/renal losses, limited intake (starvation, fasting), alcohol abuse, pancreatitis, hyperglycemia.","Signs/symptoms: overactive-muscle picture — hyperactive reflexes, confusion, cramps, tremors, seizures, and nystagmus (rapid, uncontrolled eye movement — side to side, up/down, or circular; usually bilateral).","Hypermagnesemia (> 2.1)","Causes: increased intake accompanied by renal failure (e.g., milk of magnesia in a patient with chronic renal failure — think elderly patients).","Signs/symptoms: opposite of low magnesium — lethargy, floppiness, muscle weakness, decreased reflexes, flushed/warm skin; very high levels can drop pulse/blood pressure.","Magnesium sulfate (IV) or magnesium oxide (PO) replace magnesium — one of the few electrolytes that can be given IV push if absolutely necessary, though IV administration can burn. Beyond replacing a deficiency, magnesium sulfate is also us…"]},{"s":"Calcium Imbalances▾","a":"calcium-imbalances","k":["Calcium Imbalances▾","Normal calcium: 9–10.5. Below 9 is hypocalcemia; above 10.5 is hypercalcemia. About 99% of body calcium is stored in bone (thyroid/parathyroid hormones control release from and absorption into bone); of the small remainder, about 40% is pro…","Hypocalcemia (< 9) — Inability to mobilize calcium from bone (hypoparathyroidism, hypomagnesemia), increased renal loss (renal failure), increased binding, decreased intake/absorption or vitamin D, acute pancreatitis, thyroid/parathyroid su…","Hypercalcemia (> 10.5) — Hyperparathyroidism, certain cancers (lung, breast, hematologic), immobility, antacid overdose (e.g., Tums). — Acts as a sedative: fatigue, lethargy, confusion, weakness; very high/prolonged levels can cause seizure…","Two Classic Signs of Low Calcium","Trousseau's sign — inflate a blood pressure cuff to ~20 mmHg above the patient's systolic pressure for about 3 minutes; a resulting carpal (wrist/hand) spasm is a positive sign.","Chvostek's sign — gently tap the facial nerve just in front of the ear; ipsilateral twitching of facial muscles is a positive sign."]},{"s":"Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment▾","a":"calcium-phosphorus-treatment","k":["Calcium & Phosphorus Treatment▾","Hypocalcemia — IV calcium chloride (ionized, preferred form) or calcium gluconate — both are vesicants, given via central line. Oral calcium (elemental calcium, calcium carbonate/Tums) is also used. May also need vitamin D — an active form …","Hypercalcemia — Adequate hydration (3–4 L/day) to increase urine output, promote renal calcium excretion, and reduce kidney stone risk; diuretics (sodium excretion is accompanied by calcium excretion); dialysis if the patient has renal fail…","Phosphorus has an inverse relationship with calcium — low calcium accompanies high phosphate, and vice versa. Normal phosphorus: 3.0–4.5 (below 3.0 is hypophosphatemia, above 4.5 is hyperphosphatemia). About 85% of body phosphorus is in bon…"]},{"s":"Phosphorus Imbalances▾","a":"phosphorus-imbalances","k":["Phosphorus Imbalances▾","Hypophosphatemia (< 3.0) — Decreased absorption (antacid overdose), severe diarrhea, increased kidney elimination, malnutrition (alcoholism, TPN use, recovery from malnutrition — re-feeding syndrome, where reintroducing nutrition too fast i…","Hyperphosphatemia (> 4.5) — Kidney failure, phosphate-containing laxatives/enemas, massive trauma or heat stroke (intracellular phosphate shifts out), hypoparathyroidism. — Usually asymptomatic on its own — symptoms seen are actually from t…","Treatment: for low phosphate, IV (given slowly over a long period) or oral replacement — use oral intake cautiously in chronic kidney disease/hypocalcemia (risk of phosphate calcifications in joints). For high phosphate, treat the cause — c…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-pain-seizures.html","t":"Pain & Seizures","secs":[{"s":"Pain & Seizures","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors","The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types","Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory","Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators","Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain","Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin","NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism","NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome","Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity","Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression","Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl","Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone","Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects","Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification","Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles","Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: pain pathophysiology (nociception, pain classification), non-opioid and adjuvant analgesic pharmacology (NSAIDs, acetaminophen, tramadol, gabapentin/pregabalin), opioid pharmacology (morphine, hydromorphone, fentan…"]},{"s":"Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors▾","a":"pain-basics-definition-neuroanatomy-nociceptors","k":["Pain Basics — Definition, Neuroanatomy & Nociceptors▾","As a nurse, pain is whatever the patient says it is — a complex experience shaped by physical, cognitive, spiritual, emotional, and environmental factors, plus previous experience and genetics. Acute pain is protective: it promotes withdraw…","Three parts of the nervous system are involved in pain: afferent pathways (begin in the peripheral nervous system, travel to the spinal cord and up into the cortex), interpretive centers (cortical/subcortical brain areas that interpret the …","Nociceptors","Pain receptors — free nerve endings in the afferent peripheral nervous system. Stimulation causes nociceptive pain.","Respond to mechanical (pressure/distortion), thermal (heat/cold), and chemical (acids, inflammatory chemicals) stimuli — but only once a stimulus reaches a certain intensity/threshold.","Unevenly distributed — dense in skin, dental pulp, meninges, and some internal organs (more acute pain felt there); sparse in the brain, alveoli, and deep tissue (less acute pain felt there)."]},{"s":"The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types▾","a":"the-physiology-of-pain-four-phases-nerve-fiber-types","k":["The Physiology of Pain — Four Phases & Nerve Fiber Types▾","Neurotransmitters modulate pain transmission — excitatory ones (norepinephrine, acetylcholine, dopamine, serotonin) enhance/transmit pain signals and sensitize nociceptors; inhibitory ones (GABA — gamma-aminobutyric acid) suppress it. Endor…","Transduction — A painful stimulus is converted to an action potential at the sensory receptor. Chemical mediators (e.g., prostaglandins) released with injury/inflammation lower the pain threshold.","Transmission — The pain impulse conducts along A-delta and C fibers into the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, then up through the brain to the thalamus.","Perception — The message reaches the somatosensory cortex — conscious awareness of pain's location/intensity. Influenced by attention, distraction, anxiety, fear, fatigue, prior experience, genetics, age, and culture.","Modulation — Synaptic transmission of the pain signal is amplified or dampened — e.g., by endorphins closing spinal cord \"gates,\" similar to how opioid analgesics work.","A-delta Fibers","Small diameter (not the smallest); transmit sharp, well-localized, \"fast\" pain (stinging, cutting, pinching).","Can trigger a spinal reflex withdrawal before pain is even perceived (e.g., a needle stick).","C Fibers","Even smaller diameter, far more numerous; found in muscles, tendons, organs, and skin.","Transmit dull, aching, burning, poorly-localized pain — often constant."]},{"s":"Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory▾","a":"pain-threshold-tolerance-the-gate-control-theory","k":["Pain Threshold, Tolerance & the Gate Control Theory▾","Pain Threshold — The lowest intensity of pain a person can recognize. Has \"perceptual dominance\" — intense pain at one site can raise the threshold (mask pain) elsewhere. Raised by stress, exertion, acupuncture, and sexual activity.","Pain Tolerance — The greatest intensity of pain a person can handle — varies widely between and within people. Decreased by repeated pain exposure, fatigue, anger, boredom, apprehension, sleep deprivation. Increased by alcohol, persistent o…","Opioid Tolerance — An adaptation from opioid exposure that changes drug receptors over time — does not by itself indicate addiction.","The gate control theory: stimulating large-diameter nerve fibers (A-alpha, A-beta) via touch, massage, distraction, or activity can \"close the gate\" on pain signals traveling through the smaller A-delta/C fibers, attenuating the pain signal…"]},{"s":"Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators▾","a":"pain-inflammation-chemical-mediators","k":["Pain & Inflammation — Chemical Mediators▾","Tissue injury triggers chemical mediators (from plasma) that produce inflammation's signs — pain, swelling, redness, warmth, immobility. Key mediators: histamine and arachidonic acid metabolites (prostaglandins, leukotrienes). Prostaglandin…","Acute Pain","Sudden onset; resolves once the injury heals or mediators clear. Lasts seconds to under 3 months.","Stimulates the autonomic nervous system: increased heart rate/blood pressure, sweating, anxiety, dilated pupils.","Chronic Pain","Lasts > 3–6 months, beyond expected healing time; often out of proportion to observable tissue damage. Can be ongoing or intermittent (e.g., migraines).","Does not produce the same ANS response as acute pain, but causes significant psychosocial/emotional burden. Linked to CNS/peripheral nervous system sensitization changes."]},{"s":"Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain▾","a":"classifying-pain-nociceptive-vs-neuropathic-referred-phantom-pain","k":["Classifying Pain — Nociceptive vs. Neuropathic, Referred & Phantom Pain▾","Nociceptive — Cutaneous/Somatic — Pain receptors in/around skin, joints, tendons, musculoskeletal system. — Aching, constant; can be sharp/well-localized (A-delta) or dull/less-localized (C fibers, e.g., a broken bone).","Nociceptive — Visceral — Organs, often with inflammation; transmitted via C fibers. — Poorly localized, deep, diffuse; cramping, splitting, nausea/vomiting, diaphoresis. Often produces referred pain.","Neuropathic — Originates within the central nervous system — a lesion/injury to the nerves themselves; more associated with chronic pain. — Shooting, burning, electric-shock, sharp, numbness, motor weakness; poorly localized. Often does not…","Referred pain is felt at a distance from its actual source, since the brain can't always distinguish the origin — classically, chest/jaw/neck/shoulder pain during a myocardial infarction. Phantom pain originates at the site of an amputated …"]},{"s":"Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin▾","a":"atypical-analgesics-tramadol-gabapentin-pregabalin","k":["Atypical Analgesics — Tramadol, Gabapentin & Pregabalin▾","Tramadol (Ultram) — A \"miscellaneous analgesic\" — binds weakly to mu opioid receptors and inhibits norepinephrine/serotonin reuptake. PO only (plain, combined with acetaminophen, or extended-release). Used for moderate-severe pain. Mild sid…","Gabapentin (Neurontin) & Pregabalin (Lyrica) — Technically anticonvulsants, used for pain management — specifically neuropathic pain. Mechanism not fully known; thought to suppress neuronal firing. Complement/enhance opioid analgesia and ca…"]},{"s":"NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism▾","a":"nsaids-cox-1-vs-cox-2-mechanism","k":["NSAIDs — COX-1 vs. COX-2 Mechanism▾","NSAIDs are anti-prostaglandins — they block the cyclooxygenase (COX) enzyme needed to produce prostaglandins. Used for mild-moderate pain (headache, myalgia, arthritis, post-op pain), inflammation, and fever.","COX-1 — Protects gastric mucosa; needed for thromboxane synthesis (platelet aggregation) — largely beneficial, \"housekeeping\" prostaglandins. — Loses GI protection and platelet function — the source of NSAID GI/bleeding side effects.","COX-2 — Responsible for inflammation and fever. — Stops inflammation/fever — the therapeutic effect, without necessarily disrupting COX-1's protective roles.","Non-selective NSAIDs (aspirin, ibuprofen, naproxen, ketorolac) block both COX-1 and COX-2 — effective, but with the full GI/bleeding side-effect profile. Selective COX-2 inhibitors (celecoxib/Celebrex) block only COX-2, preserving COX-1's G…"]},{"s":"NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome▾","a":"nsaids-individual-drugs-black-box-warnings-reye-syndrome","k":["NSAIDs — Individual Drugs, Black Box Warnings & Reye Syndrome▾","Aspirin — Non-selective, irreversible. Combined with caffeine = Excedrin; enteric-coated forms exist (never crush). Antithrombotic (more so than ibuprofen/naproxen). Not part of the NSAID cardiovascular black box warning (unlike the others)…","Ibuprofen — Non-selective — easiest on the stomach of the non-selective NSAIDs; the most commonly used.","Naproxen — Non-selective — hardest on the kidneys, but longer-acting than ibuprofen (once-daily dosing).","Ketorolac (Toradol) — Non-selective — the most potent NSAID, given IV/IM (rarely PO). Analgesic effect comparable to morphine without the addiction/respiratory-depression risk — useful for opioid-avoidant or opioid-addicted patients. Very h…","Celecoxib (Celebrex) — Selective COX-2 inhibitor — less GI toxicity than non-selective NSAIDs (not zero), higher cardiovascular thrombotic risk.","Since 2006, the FDA requires NSAID black box warnings for cardiovascular risk (all except aspirin) and GI risk (bleeding/ulceration, can be fatal — watch elderly patients and long-term users especially). Aspirin also carries its own risk of…","Reye syndrome: never give aspirin to children — especially children under 15 with a viral/flu-like illness. It's rare but causes severe swelling of the liver and brain, with a high mortality rate. Acetaminophen and ibuprofen are the appropr…"]},{"s":"Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity▾","a":"acetaminophen-pharmacology-dosing-limits-toxicity","k":["Acetaminophen — Pharmacology, Dosing Limits & Toxicity▾","Acetaminophen (Tylenol/APAP) is not an NSAID — no anti-inflammatory activity, but does have analgesic and antipyretic effects (acts on the hypothalamus; heat dissipates via vasodilation). Mechanism is largely unknown, thought to decrease CN…","Acetaminophen overdose causes hepatotoxicity — a toxic metabolite is normally detoxified by liver glutathione stores; an overdose depletes glutathione, letting the toxic metabolite accumulate and cause hepatic necrosis. Adult limit: 4 grams…"]},{"s":"Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression▾","a":"opioid-basics-classes-assessment-respiratory-depression","k":["Opioid Basics — Classes, Assessment & Respiratory Depression▾","All opioids are high-alert drugs, derived originally from the opium poppy; today mostly synthetic. Three chemical classes: morphine-like, meperidine-like, and methadone-like — a patient's allergy to one class doesn't necessarily apply to an…","Respiratory depression is the biggest concern with any opioid — it's what kills in an overdose. If respiratory rate is < 10 breaths/minute, assess sedation level and use physical stimulation to try to arouse the patient; if significant stim…"]},{"s":"Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl▾","a":"individual-opioids-morphine-hydromorphone-fentanyl","k":["Individual Opioids — Morphine, Hydromorphone & Fentanyl▾","Morphine — The prototype opioid — a mu-receptor agonist mimicking endogenous endorphins. Schedule II, high abuse potential. Given PO, IV, epidurally, or rectally; used for moderate-severe pain. MS Contin = extended-release (12–24 hr), good …","Hydromorphone (Dilaudid) — Semi-synthetic, similar to morphine but 7× more potent (1 mg IV hydromorphone ≈ 7 mg IV morphine — typical doses 0.2–1 mg). Schedule II; PO or IV; used for severe pain. Same adverse effect profile as other opioids…","Fentanyl — Fully synthetic, extremely potent (0.1 mg IV fentanyl ≈ 10 mg IV morphine — dosed in micrograms). Used for moderate-severe (especially severe) pain, surgical induction, and chronic pain (transdermal patch, worn ~72 hr — not for o…"]},{"s":"Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone▾","a":"individual-opioids-meperidine-codeine-oxycodone-hydrocodone-methadone","k":["Individual Opioids — Meperidine, Codeine, Oxycodone, Hydrocodone & Methadone▾","Meperidine (Demerol) — Synthetic, Schedule II, weaker/shorter-acting than morphine with less respiratory depression — but rarely used today. A toxic metabolite accumulates with repeated dosing and can cause seizures, so it's not recommended…","Codeine — Schedule II alone, Schedule III combined with acetaminophen. Used for mild-moderate pain, but more often as an antitussive (cough suppressant). PO only. Not given to children under 18 (life-threatening breathing problems). Strong …","Oxycodone — Schedule II, semi-synthetic derivative of codeine, ~10× more potent — PO only, for moderate-severe pain. High abuse potential (OxyContin = time-release form). Combined with acetaminophen = Percocet.","Hydrocodone — For mild-moderate pain and cough relief — about 6× more potent than codeine (less potent than oxycodone), usually combined with acetaminophen (Lortab, Norco, Vicodin). PO only.","Methadone — Fully synthetic, Schedule II — the opioid of choice for opioid-addiction detoxification/treatment. Long half-life (dosed q12–24h vs. q4–6h for most opioids); still addictive, but with less euphoric \"high.\" PO."]},{"s":"Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects▾","a":"naloxone-the-opioid-naive-patient-common-opioid-side-effects","k":["Naloxone, the Opioid-Naive Patient & Common Opioid Side Effects▾","Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist — it binds mu receptors without activating them, blocking other opioids from doing so. No analgesic effect, and doesn't itself cause respiratory depression, which is why it's the drug of choice to r…","Constipation — Very common — proactively give stool softeners, encourage hydration/nutrition. Untreated, it can progress to paralytic ileus (especially post-op).","Nausea/Vomiting — Common enough that some patients refuse opioids over it — may need a concurrent antiemetic.","Care should be collaborative — some patients decline opioids due to personal or family history of substance use disorder, fear, or simply disliking the effect; respect those preferences and consider adjuvants/alternatives. These side effect…"]},{"s":"Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification▾","a":"seizure-terminology-pathogenesis-classification","k":["Seizure Terminology, Pathogenesis & Classification▾","Seizure — A brief episode of abnormal electrical activity in brain neurons — can have motor, sensory, and/or cognitive manifestations.","Convulsion — A more severe seizure with involuntary spasmodic muscle contractions.","Epilepsy — A disease involving recurrent, paroxysmal seizure activity — requires more than one seizure with no evidence of a reversible metabolic cause.","Myoclonic Seizure — Brief, shock-like jerks of a muscle/muscle group, often occurring in a row — easily mistaken for a tic, tremor, or clumsiness.","A seizure starts at the seizure focus — a group of neurons (often near scar tissue/gliosis) firing abnormally and autonomously, which can \"recruit\" nearby neurons and spread the seizure locally or globally. About 50% of seizures are primary…","Partial Seizure — Focal Seizure (one area of the brain)","Simple Seizure — Aware (patient's awareness intact during the seizure)","Complex Seizure — Impaired Awareness","Grand Mal Seizure — Generalized Onset, Tonic-Clonic","Classification uses three features: where it originates (focal, generalized, or unknown), the patient's level of awareness, and other features like motor involvement. Generalized onset seizures involve both brain hemispheres at once. Subtyp…","Status epilepticus — a series of seizures without a postictal recovery period, or a single seizure lasting 30 minutes or more. A true emergency, especially with tonic-clonic seizures: it can progress to respiratory arrest, hypoxia, permanen…"]},{"s":"Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles▾","a":"antiepileptic-drug-aed-therapy-principles","k":["Antiepileptic Drug (AED) Therapy — Principles▾","AEDs (also called anticonvulsants) aim to control/prevent seizures while maintaining quality of life — a constant risk/benefit balance, since these drugs carry serious side effects. About 70% of patients become seizure-free on a single AED;…","Never abruptly stop an antiepileptic drug — doing so will cause seizures. AEDs also carry a black box warning for increased risk of suicidal ideation, worsening depression, and unusual mood changes — educate every patient starting one, and …","Therapy typically starts as monotherapy, slowly titrated with serum drug level monitoring to find the therapeutic range that balances seizure control against toxicity — providers switch to a different single drug rather than stacking multip…"]},{"s":"Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management▾","a":"individual-aeds-rapid-seizure-management","k":["Individual AEDs & Rapid Seizure Management▾","Phenytoin (Dilantin) — First-line for tonic-clonic and focal seizures. PO usually, IV for emergent use. Highly protein-bound (use cautiously with malnutrition, CKD, low albumin) and a CYP450 inducer, so many drug interactions. Therapeutic r…","Valproic Acid (Depakote) — Works for essentially every seizure type (generalized, absence, myoclonic, tonic-clonic, and focal) — unusual versatility. Also used for bipolar disorder. PO or IV, highly protein-bound. Contraindicated in liver d…","Topiramate (Topamax) — Adjunct for focal and secondary generalized tonic-clonic seizures. Mechanism poorly understood (structurally similar to fructose). PO only. Watch for visual changes (can cause acute angle-closure glaucoma); can intera…","Levetiracetam (Keppra) — Adjunct for focal seizures (with or without generalization) — widely used inpatient, less so outpatient. Mechanism not well understood, generally well tolerated. PO or IV.","Rapid seizure management: most seizures stop spontaneously within about 2 minutes without intervention. For an actively epileptic or seizing inpatient, IV access is recommended for rapid medication administration; the inpatient gold standar…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-dementia-depression-sud.html","t":"Dementia, Depression & SUD","secs":[{"s":"Dementia, Depression & SUD","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive","Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment","Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types","Alzheimer Disease","Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia","Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine","Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders","Depression Pathophysiology","Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder","PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD","SSRIs & SNRIs","TCAs & MAOIs","Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines","Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors","SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment","Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: delirium and dementia (Alzheimer's, vascular, frontotemporal) and dementia pharmacology, depression and anxiety pathophysiology (panic, GAD, PTSD, social anxiety, OCD), depression/anxiety pharmacology (SSRIs, SNRIs…"]},{"s":"Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive▾","a":"delirium-hyperactive-vs-hypoactive","k":["Delirium — Hyperactive vs. Hypoactive▾","Delirium is a transient disorder of cognitive function, consciousness, and perception — onset can be sudden or gradual. Causes include drug/alcohol intoxication or withdrawal, hypoglycemia and other metabolic derangements (liver/kidney dysf…","Hyperactive Delirium — Autonomic nervous system overactivity; develops over 2–3 days. Seen in the ICU, post-surgery, during withdrawal, and in hospitalized elderly patients. Risk factors: benzodiazepines, narcotics, acute infection/sepsis, …","Hypoactive Delirium — Right-sided frontal/basal ganglion disruption; more common with metabolic disorders (liver or kidney failure). — Decreased alertness, attention span, and perception; forgetful, apathetic, slow speech, frequent sleep. E…"]},{"s":"Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment▾","a":"delirium-vs-dementia-delirium-treatment","k":["Delirium vs. Dementia & Delirium Treatment▾","Onset — Acute — hours to days. — Chronic — slow, progressive over years.","Course — Usually reversible once the cause is treated. — Irreversible once symptoms present; no cure.","Typical Setting — Hospitalized, often elderly, patients. — Long-term, community or care-facility living.","Dementia patients are at higher risk for delirium, so consistent routines are crucial for a dementia patient to help prevent it. Delirium treatment centers on identifying and removing the cause and risk factors — using the lowest dose and s…"]},{"s":"Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types▾","a":"dementia-pathophysiology-types","k":["Dementia — Pathophysiology & Types▾","Dementia is an acquired deterioration and progressive failure of cerebral functioning — impaired intellectual processes, memory, language, judgment, orientation, and decision-making. Patients are often not cognitively aware of their own dec…","Pathophysiology involves neuron degeneration, brain tissue compression, atherosclerosis of cerebral vessels, brain trauma, infections, and inflammation, and CNS infections (HIV, Creutzfeldt-Jakob disease) causing atrophy — plus a genetic pr…","Types of Dementia","Dementia is an umbrella term — mixed dementia (more than one type at once) is common.","Alzheimer's disease — the leading cause of severe cognitive dysfunction in the elderly (detailed next).","Vascular dementia — the second most common type (detailed next).","Lewy body dementia — named as a type in this lecture but not detailed here.","Frontotemporal dementia — formerly Pick disease (detailed next)."]},{"s":"Alzheimer Disease▾","a":"alzheimer-disease","k":["Alzheimer Disease▾","Alzheimer disease affects about 6 million people in the U.S., about two-thirds of them women. The cause is unknown. Early-onset Alzheimer's has a more common genetic link; late-onset (sporadic) Alzheimer's is far more common but has no spec…","Risk Factors","Age over 65","Family history","Existing mild cognitive impairment","Down syndrome","Unhealthy lifestyle","Head trauma","Social isolation","Pathophysiology","Neuritic/amyloid plaques build up between neurons, and neurofibrillary tangles of tau protein accelerate degeneration.","Changes are centered in the cerebral cortex and hippocampus — the brain's memory areas.","Loss of synapses, acetylcholine, and other neurotransmitters drives memory/cognitive decline and brain atrophy.","These brain changes occur decades before symptoms appear. Initial symptoms are subtle — progressive forgetfulness. Progression is variable, and the disease ends in a non-verbal, vegetative state, then death."]},{"s":"Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia▾","a":"vascular-frontotemporal-dementia","k":["Vascular & Frontotemporal Dementia▾","Vascular Dementia — The second most common type of dementia — related to cerebrovascular disease (large artery disease, cardioembolism, small vessel disease, stroke), causing cerebral hypoperfusion. — Diabetes, hyperlipidemia, hypertension,…","Frontotemporal Dementia — Formerly called Pick disease; rare. Familial when onset is under age 60, from mutations in tau-encoding genes. Three syndromes: behavioral variant (personality/judgment), progressive nonfluent (language/writing), a…"]},{"s":"Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine▾","a":"dementia-medications-donepezil-memantine","k":["Dementia Medications — Donepezil & Memantine▾","Alzheimer's medications target three problems: decreased acetylcholine, the buildup of plaques and tangles, and excess NMDA receptor stimulation.","Donepezil — A cholinesterase inhibitor — increases acetylcholine by inhibiting the enzyme (acetylcholinesterase) that breaks it down. Used for mild-moderate Alzheimer's. — Mild side effects overall — GI (ulcer/GI bleed risk, avoid NSAIDs) a…","Memantine — The only NMDA receptor antagonist on the market — blocks excess glutamate/NMDA stimulation, decreases calcium influx, and normalizes nerve transmission to prevent further neuronal damage. Used for moderate-severe Alzheimer's; ca…"]},{"s":"Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders▾","a":"neurotransmitters-in-mood-anxiety-disorders","k":["Neurotransmitters in Mood & Anxiety Disorders▾","Over 200 neurotransmitters exist, but depression and anxiety pathophysiology centers on four:","Dopamine — Attention, motivation, pleasure, reward. — Deficit → Parkinson's-like symptoms, apathy, anhedonia.","Norepinephrine — Alertness, energy, fight-or-flight. — High → anxiety, stress, hyperactivity. Low → lack of focus, energy, motivation.","Serotonin (5-HT) — Involved in obsessions/compulsions. — Deficit → OCD-like symptoms, increased impulsivity, suicide risk, aggression.","GABA — The principal inhibitory neurotransmitter — promotes relaxation. — Dysfunction linked to anxiety/panic disorder and major depressive disorder."]},{"s":"Depression Pathophysiology▾","a":"depression-pathophysiology","k":["Depression Pathophysiology▾","Depression affects more than 17 million people in the U.S. — about twice as common in women (men underreport it) — and is more common in white people and people who have never married. It's associated with cardiovascular disease (a 64% grea…","Types","Major depressive disorder","Persistent depressive disorder (dysthymia)","Premenstrual dysphoric disorder","Substance/medication-induced","Due to another medical condition","Postpartum","Symptoms","Anhedonia","Fatigue or restlessness/irritability","Poor concentration","Low self-esteem, negative thinking","Sleep disturbances (either direction)","Appetite disturbances (either direction)","Diagnosis requires multiple symptoms, intense and persistent enough to impair psychosocial functioning. Treatment combines medications, psychotherapy/CBT, psychoeducation/support groups, and — for refractory cases — brain stimulation therap…"]},{"s":"Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder▾","a":"panic-disorder-generalized-anxiety-disorder","k":["Panic Disorder & Generalized Anxiety Disorder▾","There are many distinct types of anxiety disorder, each treated differently. Panic disorder involves two psychological symptoms: anticipatory anxiety (fearful expectation that panic will start) and avoidance anxiety (personal strategies use…","Panic Disorder — Palpitations, chest pain, smothering feeling, dizziness, nausea, fear of losing control, tingling, hot/cold flushes. — 1st line: CBT + antidepressants (SSRIs/SNRIs/TCAs/MAOIs). 2nd line: benzodiazepines, for breakthrough on…","Generalized Anxiety Disorder (GAD) — Chronic, lasting more than 6 months — excessive, uncontrolled, unrealistic worry, muscle tension, autonomic hyperactivity, exaggerated startle, poor concentration. Risk factors: substance use, genetics, …"]},{"s":"PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD▾","a":"ptsd-social-anxiety-disorder-ocd","k":["PTSD, Social Anxiety Disorder & OCD▾","PTSD — Three core symptoms: hyperarousal, avoidance of reminders, and re-experiencing events — a chronic stress-response activation from exposure to a life-threatening event. Vivid flashbacks, nightmares/night terrors, emotional blunting, i…","Social Anxiety Disorder — Intense fear of criticism, humiliation, negative evaluation, or embarrassment in social situations, leading to withdrawal/avoidance or intense discomfort. Caused by inherited traits, amygdala structural issues, and…","OCD — Obsessions (repetitive unwanted thoughts, e.g., contamination) plus compulsions (repeated rituals, e.g., cleaning, or checking that a stove is off — a harm obsession). Time-consuming and distressing to the patient and to those around …"]},{"s":"SSRIs & SNRIs▾","a":"ssris-snris","k":["SSRIs & SNRIs▾","Despite the name, antidepressants treat both depression and anxiety. SSRIs and SNRIs are used first-line because they're the safest class. All antidepressants carry a suicide risk that must be monitored before, during, and throughout treatm…","SSRIs — Block serotonin reuptake at the presynaptic nerve ending, leaving more serotonin available in the synapse. Exam drug: fluoxetine (Prozac). Over 20 SSRIs exist (all second-generation); no common suffix. Therapeutic benefit takes abou…","SNRIs — Block reuptake of both serotonin and norepinephrine. Exam drug: venlafaxine (Effexor). No common suffix; contraindicated with MAOIs. — Similar to SSRIs, plus sweating, blurred vision, and increased LFTs — watch liver function tests …","Serotonin syndrome: risk increases when a patient takes two serotonergic drugs together (especially an SSRI/SNRI with an MAOI). Symptoms — including altered mental status and confusion — appear 2–72 hours after the interacting dose. Always …","Abruptly stopping an SSRI or SNRI can cause withdrawal syndrome: dizziness, headache, sensory disturbances (patients may describe their hands or feet as \"on fire\"), shaking, tremors, anxiety, and dysphoria. This is a major teaching point — …"]},{"s":"TCAs & MAOIs▾","a":"tcas-maois","k":["TCAs & MAOIs▾","Tricyclic antidepressants (TCAs) and MAOIs are older drug classes, prescribed less often than SSRIs/SNRIs because of more intense interactions and side effects.","TCA anticholinergic effects mnemonic: \"hot as a hare\" (overheated), \"dry as a bone\" (dry mouth/eyes/secretions), \"blind as a bat\" (blurry vision), \"red as a beet\" (flushed), and \"mad as a hatter\" (confusion).","TCAs — Blocks reuptake of norepinephrine and serotonin. Exam drug: amitriptyline (Elavil). Used for 50+ years — good efficacy, tolerable side effects, inexpensive; also used for neuropathic pain, nocturnal enuresis, and TMJ. — The anticholi…","MAOIs — Inhibits the monoamine oxidase enzyme that normally converts norepinephrine, serotonin, and dopamine to inactive products — making more of each available. Exam drug: phenelzine (Nardil). Used less often, generally for refractory or …","TCA overdose can be fatal. This is a major concern precisely because TCAs are prescribed to patients with depression/anxiety who already carry a higher suicide risk — and the medication itself can sometimes increase suicidal thoughts. Scree…","MAOI + tyramine-rich foods → hypertensive crisis — the most serious MAOI risk. Tyramine is found in aged cheese, smoked meats, yeast/bread, and red wine; patients on an MAOI must avoid these entirely. The same hypertensive crisis risk appli…"]},{"s":"Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines▾","a":"atypical-antidepressants-benzodiazepines","k":["Atypical Antidepressants & Benzodiazepines▾","Bupropion (Wellbutrin) — Amphetamine-like structure with a stimulant effect and decreased appetite; unclear mechanism of action. Faster onset than most antidepressants (1–3 weeks). Also used for smoking cessation, ADHD, and neuropathic pain…","Ketamine — Traditionally used for extreme pain control. Low-dose intranasal ketamine now treats refractory/extreme depression with a rapid effect — useful for actively suicidal patients. Adverse effects: dissociation/hallucinations (a \"K-ho…","Trazodone — A second-line agent for depression that blocks serotonin reuptake, but with minimal antidepressant effect. More commonly used for anxiety or as a sleep aid.","Benzodiazepines (\"benzos\" — historically nicknamed \"the lams and the pams\" for their common -lam/-pam suffixes, though not universal) enhance GABA's inhibitory effects in the CNS. Indicated for GAD and panic disorder, short-term only — they…","IV benzodiazepines as the inpatient gold standard for active seizure management — covered in Pain & Seizures","Adverse Effects — CNS depression/decreased LOC (respiratory depression risk, especially IV), addictive with a withdrawal risk, memory loss.","Nursing Considerations — Contraindicated in pregnancy (teratogenic); Schedule IV with moderate abuse risk; short-term prescriptions only (e.g., 10 pills, no automatic renewals); avoid grapefruit/fatty foods (CYP450 interaction).","Antidote — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepine effects.","Never combine a benzodiazepine with another CNS depressant — Benadryl, alcohol, opioids, and barbiturates — the combination risks fatal respiratory depression."]},{"s":"Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors▾","a":"substance-use-disorder-pathophysiology-risk-protective-factors","k":["Substance Use Disorder — Pathophysiology, Risk & Protective Factors▾","Substance use disorder (SUD) is a disease — a complex interaction of environmental, biological, genetic, and brain-chemistry factors. It is explicitly not a personal failure or weakness, despite that being a common and outdated misconceptio…","Dopamine drives the reward pathway: substance use causes a large, rapid dopamine surge that greatly exceeds the normal pleasure response. Over time, the brain's normal dopamine pathways undergo adaptive downregulation, requiring more freque…","Risk Factors","Aggressive childhood behavior","Lack of parental supervision","Poor social skills","Drug experimentation","Drug availability at school","Community poverty","Protective Factors","Good self-control","Parental monitoring/support","Positive relationships","Good grades","School anti-drug policies","Neighborhood resources","These risk and protective factors are proxies for larger social determinants of health. Addiction risk also depends on the substance itself: type, route of administration, effect, age of first use, availability, and cost."]},{"s":"SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment▾","a":"sud-treatment-screening-medication-assisted-treatment","k":["SUD Treatment, Screening & Medication-Assisted Treatment▾","Treatment is highly individualized and therapy-centered — CBT, contingency management, motivational enhancement, family therapy, and 12-step programs — alongside pharmacological interventions, addressing the whole person rather than the sub…","Methadone — A full synthetic opioid analgesic and mu agonist, used for opioid use disorder (including heroin). Occupies opioid receptors to reduce withdrawal symptoms and cravings. Lower abuse potential than opioids generally, but given via…","Buprenorphine + Naloxone (Suboxone) — Buprenorphine is a partial opioid agonist that blocks receptors and prevents cravings (alone, it's called Subutex, and can be used standalone). Naloxone (Narcan) is an opioid antagonist. Combining an ag…"]},{"s":"Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol▾","a":"withdrawal-syndromes-opioids-vs-benzodiazepines-alcohol","k":["Withdrawal Syndromes — Opioids vs. Benzodiazepines/Alcohol▾","Withdrawal timelines are explicitly testable. Opioid withdrawal (including heroin) begins about 12–24 hours after the last dose, peaks around 72 hours, and lasts 5–10 days. It feels miserable — flu-like symptoms including nausea, vomiting, …","Benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal both act on GABA receptors and share similar signs, symptoms, and treatment. Non-dangerous symptoms: sleep disturbances, dry retching, irritability, nausea, increased tension, weight loss, anxiety, palp…","Unlike opioid withdrawal, benzodiazepine and alcohol withdrawal can be life-threatening. Dangerous symptoms include seizures, hallucinations, delirium tremens (DTs) — hallucination, disorientation, tachycardia, hypertension, fever, agitatio…","Opioids — 12–24 hours after last dose — ~72 hours — 5–10 days","Benzodiazepines — 6–12 hours to 1–4 days after last dose — ~week 2 — Subsides after peak; commonly abused benzos include Xanax, Klonopin, Valium, Ativan, and Ambien.","Alcohol — ~8 hours after last drink — 1–3 days — Tapers over 1 week, up to several weeks"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-gi-gu-skin-hypersensitivity.html","t":"GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","secs":[{"s":"GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification","Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor","Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections","Antifungal Pharmacology","Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo","Herpes Zoster (Shingles)","Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis","MRSA","Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening","Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma","Eczema & Psoriasis","Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology","GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals","GI Pharmacology — Laxatives","Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions","Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy","Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity","Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity","Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology","Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: skin disorders (fungal, viral, and bacterial skin infections; skin cancer; eczema and psoriasis) and antifungal pharmacology, basic GI pharmacology (constipation and diarrhea), hypersensitivity reactions (Types I–I…"]},{"s":"Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification▾","a":"fungal-skin-infections-transmission-classification","k":["Fungal Skin Infections — Transmission & Classification▾","Fungi are spore-producing organisms that live naturally on skin, in soil, on plants, and on household surfaces — usually harmlessly. Fungus becomes a problem three ways: implantation (entering through broken skin after an injury), inhalatio…","Mycosis — A fungal infection in general — dermatophytes cause superficial mycoses (affecting only the surface layers of skin).","Tinea — Mycosis caused by dermatophytes — fungi that love keratinized tissue (hair, skin, nails). Also called \"ringworm,\" not because a worm is involved, but because it can cause a ring-shaped rash.","Candidiasis — Mycosis caused by Candida albicans, a yeast.","Tinea and candidiasis are the two most common fungal infections. Superficial fungal infections are typically treated with topical antifungal creams/ointments; severe, extensive cases need systemic (PO or IV) antifungals."]},{"s":"Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor▾","a":"tinea-infections-pedis-capitis-versicolor","k":["Tinea Infections — Pedis, Capitis & Versicolor▾","Tinea Pedis (Athlete's Foot) — Fungal infection of the feet, typically starting between the toes and spreading to soles/toenails. Dry, scaling, itchy lesions; can see maceration, fissures. Spread by contact with infected skin/fungus in the …","Tinea Capitis (Cradle Cap) — Affects the scalp, eyebrows, eyelashes — scaly red lesions and hair loss (can cause permanent alopecia, though hair often regrows). The most common dermatophyte infection in the pediatric population. — Topical a…","Tinea Versicolor — Affects the upper back, chest, and arms — caused by an overgrowth of yeast that normally lives on the skin (not contagious). Risk factors: hot climate, heavy sweating, oily skin, weakened immune system. Produces bleached,…"]},{"s":"Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections▾","a":"candidiasis-systemic-fungal-infections","k":["Candidiasis & Systemic Fungal Infections▾","Candidiasis (thrush, yeast infection) is caused by Candida albicans, normally found on mucous membranes, skin, the GI tract, and the vagina — it becomes pathogenic particularly in critically ill and immunocompromised patients. Risk factors:…","Candidiasis affects only the outer layers of mucous membranes and skin — typically the mouth, vagina, an uncircumcised penis, nail folds, and large skin folds (intertriginous areas). Presents as white lesions in the mouth or beefy-red spots…","Systemic Fungal Infections","Require aggressive oral or IV antifungal treatment and typically infect internal organs — intestines, urinary tract, lungs, brain.","Common lung infections: histoplasmosis, blastomycosis, pneumocystis.","Cryptococcus affects both the lungs and meninges."]},{"s":"Antifungal Pharmacology▾","a":"antifungal-pharmacology","k":["Antifungal Pharmacology▾","Nystatin — A polyene, used to treat superficial candidiasis (thrush, oral mucosa, vagina, skin). Available as cream, powder (skin folds), or liquid (swish-and-spit or swish-and-swallow). Too toxic to give IV. — Topical route: mild skin irri…","Amphotericin B — A polyene — the agent of choice for systemic mycoses. Binds ergosterol in the fungal cell membrane, making it leaky and destroying the fungal cell wall. Given PO or IV, typically every other day for several months (can take…","Flucytosine — A pyrimidine that inhibits fungal DNA synthesis. Rarely given alone — almost always paired with amphotericin B to allow a lower amphotericin B dose (and fewer side effects). — —","Fluconazole (an Azole) — Interferes with ergosterol synthesis, disrupting fungal cell wall integrity. Used for superficial and less-serious systemic (e.g., pulmonary) fungal infections. Rapidly/completely absorbed orally, reaching bone, CNS…","Griseofulvin — An older, inexpensive drug that inhibits fungal mitosis by binding keratin (doesn't affect the cell wall/membrane). Used for dermatophyte infections of the scalp, skin, or nails resistant to other treatment. — Bone marrow sup…","Remembering azole toxicity: azoles have an \"L\" in the name — for Liver toxicity. Monitor AST/ALT.","Fluconazole nursing considerations: needs an acidic stomach environment to absorb properly, so separate it by at least 2 hours from anything that reduces stomach acidity (antacids, PPIs). Never mix IV fluconazole with other IV medications. …","Amphotericin B is a high-alert drug — it must be diluted and infused slowly. It's hard on the kidneys (monitor BUN/creatinine) and can cause cardiac dysrhythmias (put the patient on a cardiac monitor during IV administration)."]},{"s":"Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo▾","a":"pigmented-lesions-melasma-vitiligo","k":["Pigmented Lesions — Melasma & Vitiligo▾","Melasma — Dark macules on the face, more common in women — especially during pregnancy (the \"mask of pregnancy\") or with oral contraceptive use. A dermatological nuisance, not a health problem. — Avoid sun exposure, bleaching creams, retino…","Vitiligo — An acquired condition — abnormalities in melanin production cause pigment to disappear from patches of skin. Usually appears around age 21 or earlier, can appear suddenly. May be associated with autoimmune conditions (pernicious …"]},{"s":"Herpes Zoster (Shingles)▾","a":"herpes-zoster-shingles","k":["Herpes Zoster (Shingles)▾","Shingles is caused by reactivation of the varicella zoster virus (the chickenpox virus), which lies dormant in a dermatome after a chickenpox infection. Reactivated by immunosuppression, significant stress, or illness — most common in peopl…","Key Features","Follows dermatomal lines but is asymmetric and does not cross midline — appears on only one side of the body.","Vesicles are extremely painful; the rash clears in about 2–3 weeks.","Most contagious when vesicles are weeping — can transmit chickenpox (not shingles) to someone who has never had chickenpox or the vaccine.","Treated with antivirals. A notable complication is postherpetic neuralgia — persistent, indefinite pain in the same area — affecting about 13% of patients over 60, which is why the shingles vaccine is recommended starting around age 50–60."]},{"s":"Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis▾","a":"bacterial-skin-infections-impetigo-abscess-furuncle-carbuncle-cellulitis","k":["Bacterial Skin Infections — Impetigo, Abscess, Furuncle/Carbuncle & Cellulitis▾","Impetigo — Acute, contagious infection caused by staphylococcus/streptococcus (carried in the nose) — common in children, spreads in daycares/schools. Vesicles and pustules with a honey-colored crust on a red base. — Topical antibacterial (…","Abscess — Inflamed, red skin with a collection of pus underneath — raised, palpable borders, tender, warm, may fluctuate (feel fluid-filled) with purulent discharge. — Incision and drainage (I&D) plus antibiotics (PO or IV); drainage may be…","Furuncle vs. Carbuncle — Furuncle: bacterial infection of a single hair follicle (an \"ingrown hair\"). Carbuncle: deeper, more painful swelling involving several hair follicles. — I&D plus antibiotics, same as an abscess.","Cellulitis — Bacterial infection of the skin and surrounding tissue (usually staph/strep), often starting from an injury or animal/insect bite, common on the lower extremities. Swollen, red, warm, tender; can blister if severe. Not contagio…"]},{"s":"MRSA▾","a":"mrsa","k":["MRSA▾","Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus (MRSA) is a staph strain resistant to methicillin and the penicillin family as a whole.","Hospital-Acquired MRSA","Associated with invasive procedures — surgery, IV tubing, central lines, artificial joints.","Treated with IV vancomycin or linezolid.","Community-Acquired MRSA","Often begins as a painful boil; transmitted person-to-person. Highest risk: high school wrestlers, childcare workers, crowded living conditions.","Treated with Bactrim or dicloxacillin.","MRSA presents with warm skin, purulent drainage, possible fever, and can develop an abscess quickly — it can burrow deep into tissue and spread to bones, joints, surgical wounds, the bloodstream, heart valves, and lungs. It's not limited to…"]},{"s":"Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening▾","a":"skin-cancer-precancerous-lesions-abcde-screening","k":["Skin Cancer — Precancerous Lesions & ABCDE Screening▾","Actinic Keratosis — Benign, precancerous lesion from sun/UV damage — rough, scaly, red plaques. More common in light-skinned people.","Solar Lentigo — Benign, precancerous \"liver spots\"/\"age spots,\" also from sun exposure.","Both are watched, measured, and followed over time to catch malignant transformation early. Skin cancer disproportionately affects Caucasians (20× more likely to be diagnosed than African-Americans) — but African-Americans are 4× more likel…","ABCDE melanoma screening: Asymmetry, Border (uneven, notched, crusty), Color (variation — white, brown, blue), Diameter (larger than a pencil eraser, >6mm), Evolving (changing size, shape, or color; new bleeding/scabbing)."]},{"s":"Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma▾","a":"skin-cancer-basal-cell-squamous-cell-melanoma","k":["Skin Cancer — Basal Cell, Squamous Cell & Melanoma▾","Basal Cell Carcinoma — Most common skin cancer, least malignant — usually doesn't metastasize, slow-growing, most curable. Increased risk with increased sun exposure. — Small, flesh-colored/pink dome-shaped bump that progresses to a translu…","Squamous Cell Carcinoma — Second most common — curable with early treatment, but can metastasize to lymph nodes/organs if not caught early. — Redness and scaling, slightly elevated, irregular border, shallow chronic ulcer.","Melanoma — Rare but the most deadly skin cancer — cancer of melanocytes, spreads both radially (wide) and vertically (deep) into the dermis, and can occur in the eyes and under nails. Highest rate among white males. Risk factors: family his…"]},{"s":"Eczema & Psoriasis▾","a":"eczema-psoriasis","k":["Eczema & Psoriasis▾","Eczema (Atopic Dermatitis) — Not contagious; thought to be caused by an overactive immune system, with a hereditary tendency — more common alongside asthma or hay fever. Itchy rash on the face, backs of knees, wrists, hands, feet; dry, thic…","Psoriasis — Chronic condition beginning in young adulthood (18–24); not contagious. Thought to involve an overactive/autoimmune immune response; linked to obesity and cardiovascular disease (connection not fully explained). Skin cells grow …"]},{"s":"Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology▾","a":"constipation-diarrhea-pathophysiology","k":["Constipation & Diarrhea Pathophysiology▾","Constipation is small, infrequent, or difficult bowel movements — a general guideline is fewer than 3 bowel movements per week. Common causes: a low-fiber diet, lack of exercise (exercise stimulates peristalsis), slowed peristalsis (especia…","Diarrhea is increased frequency and fluidity of bowel movements — often the first sign of a larger GI disorder. Acute diarrhea can result from infection (viral/bacterial), emotional stress, or medications (antibiotics, antacids, chemotherap…","Osmotic — Increased osmotically active solutes (e.g., magnesium sulfate) pull sodium and water into the gut. — Common with concentrated tube feedings.","Secretory — A bacterial toxin increases secretion and inhibits reabsorption. — Vibrio cholerae, Staphylococcus aureus.","Exudative — Active bowel inflammation exudes mucus, blood, and protein into the stool, pulling water in with them. — Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis.","Motility Disturbance — Decreased small-intestine absorption delivers an abnormally large fluid volume to the colon. — Dumping syndrome (post-gastrectomy), irritable bowel syndrome.","Diarrhea's consequences go beyond the underlying cause: skin breakdown (especially in hospitalized or low-motility patients), fluid/electrolyte imbalance, and nutritional concerns from poor absorption — all bigger risks in the very young, v…"]},{"s":"GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals▾","a":"gi-pharmacology-antidiarrheals","k":["GI Pharmacology — Antidiarrheals▾","Antidiarrheals are chosen based on the specific cause when known, and are always given orally (no IV options). Three groups: adsorbents, anti-motility drugs, and probiotics. (Opioids have anti-motility properties too, but are rarely used fo…","Adsorbents — Bismuth subsalicylate (Pepto-Bismol, an aspirin derivative) and activated charcoal (also used in overdose). Coats the GI tract wall and binds the causative agent. — Increased bleeding time, constipation. Harmless: dark stools/t…","Anti-Motility — Loperamide (Imodium) — an anti-peristaltic agent. Diphenoxylate with atropine (Lomotil) — anticholinergic/antispasmodic, slows peristalsis and dries gastric secretions; prescription-only due to anticholinergic effects (loper…","Probiotics — Lactobacillus-based products (the organism making up most normal gut flora, often destroyed by antibiotics) and Saccharomyces boulardii (Florastor, used specifically for C. diff). Replenish gut flora and suppress diarrhea-causi…"]},{"s":"GI Pharmacology — Laxatives▾","a":"gi-pharmacology-laxatives","k":["GI Pharmacology — Laxatives▾","Laxative choice is individualized by the patient's age, constipation severity, and contributing factors, alongside dietary fiber and increased activity. Laxatives are among the most misused OTC medications — inappropriate use can cause depe…","Bulk-Forming — Psyllium (Metamucil) — Works like dietary fiber — absorbs water, increases stool bulk, distends the bowel to trigger motility. No systemic effects. Must be taken with liberal water (prevents esophageal obstruction/impaction).…","Emollient — Docusate sodium (Colace), mineral oil — Docusate lubricates fecal material/intestinal walls and softens stool — used for fecal impaction and to prevent opioid-induced constipation. Mineral oil lubricates the intestines (PO or PR…","Hyperosmotic — Glycerin, lactulose, polyethylene glycol (GoLYTELY) — Draw water into the colon. Glycerin is mild, often given as a suppository in children. Lactulose also reduces blood ammonia — used in hepatic encephalopathy. Polyethylene …","Saline — Magnesium salts (mag citrate, milk of magnesia, Epsom salts), sodium salts (fleet enema) — Draw water into the colon via osmotic pressure — produces a watery stool within 3–6 hours. Use magnesium salts cautiously with renal insuffi…","Stimulant — Bisacodyl (Dulcolax), senna — Induces intestinal peristalsis directly; effect is dose-proportional. The class most likely to cause dependence. Bisacodyl can be given PO or PR."]},{"s":"Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions▾","a":"hypersensitivity-basics-type-i-ige-mediated-reactions","k":["Hypersensitivity Basics & Type I (IgE-Mediated) Reactions▾","A hypersensitivity reaction is a normal immune response inappropriately triggered, excessive relative to the trigger, or producing effects damaging to the body. Triggered by a specific antigen-antibody or antibody-lymphocyte reaction. Types…","Memory aid: B cells make antiBodies (Types I–III); T cells provide long-Term, delayed immunity (Type IV).","Hypersensitivity reactions are antigen-specific and typically don't occur on first exposure — the first exposure sensitizes the patient (antibody production begins), and the reaction happens on a later exposure.","Mechanism — First exposure to an antigen (e.g., pollen) triggers a B cell to mature into a plasma cell, which produces IgE antibodies. These IgE antibodies attach to mast cells and wait. On re-exposure, the antigen links to the mast-cell-bo…","Effects of Mediators — Vasodilation (drop in BP), laryngeal edema/swelling (the most severe reaction — anaphylactic shock), urticaria (hives), increased vascular permeability (fluid leak, worsening hypotension), bronchial smooth muscle cons…","Common Triggers — Environmental (pet dander, pollen, dust, mold), foods (nuts, shellfish, eggs — most are proteins), medications (penicillin, contrast dye). Note: a shellfish/seafood allergy is a red flag for a possible contrast dye allergy…","Etiology — Genetic component — 30% risk if one parent is allergic to an antigen, 50% if both. Theorized to be an inherited overactive immune system.","Type I reactions can be local (e.g., allergic rhinitis — about 50% of the US population reacts to one of the ten most common allergens) or systemic (anaphylaxis — a medical emergency, covered next). An allergy shot works by building toleran…"]},{"s":"Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy▾","a":"anaphylaxis-epinephrine-hypersensitivity-pharmacotherapy","k":["Anaphylaxis, Epinephrine & Hypersensitivity Pharmacotherapy▾","Systemic Type I reactions release chemical mediators throughout the body and are considered a medical emergency — laryngeal edema can make breathing difficult or impossible, and widespread vasodilation causes vascular collapse with severe h…","Anaphylaxis is life-threatening — laryngeal edema can obstruct breathing, and widespread vasodilation causes vascular collapse. Treat with antihistamines, glucocorticoids, and epinephrine. Educate patients to wear a MedicAlert bracelet, car…","Epinephrine is a sympathomimetic (mimics fight-or-flight): a vasopressor, bronchodilator, anti-asthmatic, and vasoconstrictor, acting on both alpha (arteries) and beta (heart, lungs, arteries, skeletal muscle) receptors. It inhibits mast ce…","Epinephrine concentration matters — mixing it up can be fatal. Anaphylaxis dosing uses a 1:1,000 concentration (adult dose 0.2–1 mg; pediatric 0.01 mg/kg); cardiac arrest uses a more dilute 1:10,000 concentration. Giving an IM-concentration…","Immunosuppressants","Decrease immune response — used in transplant patients to reduce rejection of foreign tissue.","Increases infection risk.","Antihistamines","Reduce allergic symptoms by blocking histamine's mediator activity — don't stop the underlying reaction."]},{"s":"Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity▾","a":"type-ii-cytotoxic-type-iii-immune-complex-hypersensitivity","k":["Type II (Cytotoxic) & Type III (Immune Complex) Hypersensitivity▾","Type II — cytotoxic reaction (memory aid: what happens if a patient gets the wrong blood type). IgG/IgM antibodies bind directly to antigens on a foreign cell's surface, forming an antibody-antigen complex that attacks and lyses the target …","During a blood transfusion, watch for fever/chills, flushing, changes in heart rate or blood pressure, chest or back pain, restlessness, anxiety, and headache — if any of these appear, stop the transfusion immediately. Take the patient's te…","Type III — immune complex reaction (memory aid: rheumatoid arthritis). Antigen-antibody complexes (IgG/IgM) form in circulation and deposit into tissue, where the resulting inflammation — not the complex itself — causes tissue damage. Neutr…"]},{"s":"Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity▾","a":"type-iv-delayed-hypersensitivity","k":["Type IV (Delayed) Hypersensitivity▾","Type IV — delayed hypersensitivity (memory aid: poison ivy, a positive TB skin test, a jellyfish sting, a jewelry reaction, Crohn's disease). Unlike Types I–III, this involves no antibodies — it's mediated by T lymphocytes, and reactions ar…","Mechanism — A small incomplete protein (hapten) attaches to a human protein to form a complete antigen. An antigen-processing cell presents this to a T lymphocyte, which — after a delay — recognizes and attacks. Cytotoxic (CD8) T cells atta…","Common Antigens — Plant oils (poison ivy/oak/sumac), adhesives, cosmetics, clothing, nickel alloys (jewelry), tuberculin antigen (the Mantoux/TB skin test), organ transplant/skin graft tissue, and gluten in Crohn's disease.","Timing — Clinical manifestations generally peak 48–72 hours after antigen exposure — this is why a TB skin test has to be \"read\" 48–72 hours after placement. The two most common Type IV reactions are contact dermatitis and tuberculin hypers…","Organ transplant/graft rejection specifically can be a Type I, II, or IV reaction depending on timing and mechanism."]},{"s":"Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology▾","a":"urinary-tract-infections-pathophysiology-risk-factors-pharmacology","k":["Urinary Tract Infections — Pathophysiology, Risk Factors & Pharmacology▾","UTIs are more common in females due to a shorter urethra and incomplete bladder emptying. E. coli is the most common causative organism. Bacteriuria (bacteria in the urine) is colonization and does not automatically mean infection; urethrit…","Risk Factors","Catheterization (CAUTI)","Female anatomy (shorter urethra, perineal irritation)","Older age (up to 50% of nursing home residents have bacteriuria)","Pregnancy; spermicide/diaphragm/condom use","New sexual activity (~25% of females get a UTI within their first sexually active year)","Urinary obstruction/reflux, immobility, incontinence, decreased cognition, hygiene-access barriers","Protective Factors","Acidic urine","Prostatic secretions (males) and urethral gland secretions (females) — prostatic secretions are more protective","Unidirectional urine flow and the bladder's one-way valve","A functioning immune system","Males are less likely to get a UTI, but more likely to have recurrent UTIs (bacteria can hide deep in the prostate); BPH also raises urinary retention risk. Atypical presentations: children often present with fever, irritability, poor appet…","Trimethoprim-Sulfamethoxazole (Bactrim) — First-line UTI antibiotic — avoid in patients with sulfa allergies.","Ciprofloxacin — Alternative for patients with sulfa allergies.","Nitrofurantoin — Used for recurring lower UTIs.","Phenazopyridine (Pyridium) — A urinary analgesic — relieves UTI pain but does not treat the infection itself (the antibiotic does). Harmless but notable side effect: turns urine a reddish-orange color.","Key teaching point for any antibiotic course: finish the entire prescription, even after symptoms improve."]},{"s":"Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence▾","a":"overactive-bladder-urinary-incontinence","k":["Overactive Bladder & Urinary Incontinence▾","The bladder is smooth muscle that normally holds about 300–500 mL before signaling the need to urinate. Overactive bladder occurs when bladder muscle contracts involuntarily even at low urine volumes, creating urgency. Causes: neurological …","Mirabegron is an antispasmodic that selectively stimulates beta-3 adrenergic receptors to relax bladder smooth muscle (PO, 25–50 mg/day). Most serious adverse reaction: hypertension (monitor BP; use cautiously in patients who already have h…","Urge — Involuntary urine leakage immediately after a sudden urge — no time to reach the toilet. Caused by an overactive detrusor (bladder smooth) muscle; frequency increases with age. Contributing factors: bladder infections, outlet obstruc…","Stress — Involuntary urine loss with increased intra-abdominal pressure (sneezing, coughing, lifting) — from loss of pelvic floor muscle/fascial support (often from childbirth trauma or pelvic surgery), which disrupts the normal bladder-ure…","Mixed — Combination of stress and urge incontinence — common together, especially in older females.","Overflow — The bladder becomes so full it leaks/overflows — more common in males with an enlarged prostate obstructing outflow.","Functional — The urinary system itself works fine, but a physical condition, mobility limitation, cognitive disorder, or lack of toilet access prevents timely toileting.","Transient — Sudden and reversible — often related to a UTI, constipation, or fecal impaction.","Incontinence is not a normal part of aging, though age-related lower urinary tract changes can contribute. It carries psychosocial impact plus real skin risk — acidic urine causes inflammation/breakdown, and damp, warm, damaged skin becomes…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-stress-steroids.html","t":"Stress & Steroids","secs":[{"s":"Stress & Steroids","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Homeostasis vs. Allostasis","Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress","Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation","Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways","SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects","Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine","HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone","Resistance Stage","Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload","Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations","Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution","Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes","Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies","Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone","Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term","Corticosteroid Patient Education","Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone","Self-Test Flashcards","NUR 325 review, covering: homeostasis and allostasis, stress versus stressors, General Adaptation Syndrome (alarm, resistance, exhaustion), the sympathetic/adrenergic and HPA-axis stress pathways, allostatic load and overload, chronic stres…"]},{"s":"Homeostasis vs. Allostasis▾","a":"homeostasis-vs-allostasis","k":["Homeostasis vs. Allostasis▾","Homeostasis — From Greek homeo (same/similar) + stasis (stable/standing still). The state where all body systems are balanced around an ideal set point (temperature, vital signs, blood pH). Each person's normal set point falls within a rang…","Allostasis — From Greek allo (variable/different) + stasis (stable/standing still). The dynamic process the body uses to achieve and return to a steady state — the body's overall attempt to get itself back to homeostasis. Allostasis recogni…","Word roots: homeo-stasis = staying the same (the balanced set point itself); allo-stasis = the variable, active process that gets the body back to — or adjusts — that set point.","Examples of homeostasis in action: blood pH depends on CO₂ concentration, which the respiratory system regulates; core temperature is hypothalamus-mediated; and blood pressure is sensed by stretch receptors called baroreceptors, which trigg…"]},{"s":"Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress▾","a":"stress-stressors-eustress-distress","k":["Stress, Stressors, Eustress & Distress▾","Stressor — The actual force or challenge causing stress (e.g., an exam).","Stress — The physical, chemical, or emotional tension the body and mind feel in response to a stressor (e.g., how you feel in your body before that exam).","Eustress — Positive stress — feeling happy, accomplished, excited, or nervous in a good way. Perceived as within one's coping ability; can motivate, sharpen focus, and improve performance.","Distress — Negative stress — causes anxiety and concern, can be short- or long-term, and decreases performance. Perceived as outside one's coping ability.","The stress response is meant to be helpful in the short term — its job is to get the body through the stressor and back to homeostasis. Problems develop when the stress response is activated repeatedly or does not let up: chronic stress las…"]},{"s":"Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation▾","a":"factors-influencing-coping-adaptation","k":["Factors Influencing Coping & Adaptation▾","What shapes a person's coping ability","Genetics — some inherent difference in how efficiently a person relaxes and responds to stress.","Culture and environment — different values, viewpoints, and surroundings shape how stress is handled; a child raised by a parent who panics under stress can learn to panic too.","Prior experience — a bad past experience (e.g., at the dentist) creates anticipatory stress at future encounters.","Pre-existing health status — an existing acute or chronic disease adds extra allostatic demand, pushing a person closer to the limit of what they can manage.","Ability to manage stress — support systems, coping mechanisms, and logical thinking that help a person work through stress.","Coping mechanisms are strategies directed at the stressor that help a person withstand it; a coping strategy is effective if it resolves the situation and the feelings around it. Healthy examples: meditation, exercise, social support, talki…"]},{"s":"Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways▾","a":"alarm-stage-fight-or-flight-the-two-activation-pathways","k":["Alarm Stage — Fight-or-Flight & the Two Activation Pathways▾","General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) is the body's similar physiologic response to a stressor regardless of what the stressor actually is (physical, emotional, or psychological) and unfolds in three stages: alarm, resistance, and exhaustion. I…","The alarm stage is the initial burst of arousal and energy that helps the body evade or confront a stressor — the \"fight-or-flight\" response. The hypothalamus releases corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH), which activates two pathways simu…","Sympathetic (adrenergic) pathway — CRH activates the sympathetic nervous system (SNS) → SNS stimulates the adrenal medulla → adrenal medulla releases catecholamines (norepinephrine and epinephrine) — the \"adrenergic response.\"","HPA axis — CRH stimulates the anterior pituitary → anterior pituitary releases adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) → ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex → adrenal cortex releases glucocorticoids (cortisol)."]},{"s":"SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects▾","a":"sns-vs-pns-fight-or-flight-vs-rest-and-digest-effects","k":["SNS vs. PNS — Fight-or-Flight vs. Rest-and-Digest Effects▾","Sympathetic (SNS) — \"Fight or Flight,\" Adrenergic","Pupils dilate (better see the threat)","Salivation stops (dry mouth)","Heart rate increases","Bronchioles dilate (more oxygenation to muscles)","Peristalsis and gastric secretions decrease (digestion isn't needed right now)","Liver breaks down glycogen for glucose (glycogenolysis) — muscles need fuel","Bladder contraction is inhibited","Parasympathetic (PNS) — \"Rest and Digest,\" Cholinergic","Saliva production stimulated","Bronchioles constrict","Peristalsis picks back up","Bile is released","Bladder contracts"]},{"s":"Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine▾","a":"catecholamines-norepinephrine-epinephrine","k":["Catecholamines — Norepinephrine & Epinephrine▾","Norepinephrine — Primary constrictor of smooth muscle in blood vessels — regulates blood flow distribution to organs and maintains blood pressure via vasoconstriction. Dilates pupils (improves night and far-distance vision). Decreases gastr…","Epinephrine — Enhances heart contractility, increases heart rate and venous return (raising cardiac output). Relaxes bronchial smooth muscle to dilate airways. Increases glycogenolysis (glucose release from the liver) and inhibits insulin s…","Together, norepinephrine and epinephrine also influence the immune system through cytokine production."]},{"s":"HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone▾","a":"hpa-axis-cortisol-aldosterone","k":["HPA Axis — Cortisol & Aldosterone▾","The HPA axis (hypothalamus → anterior pituitary → adrenal cortex) works on a negative feedback loop that normally keeps cortisol within a normal range. The adrenal cortex releases both glucocorticoids and mineralocorticoids in response to A…","Cortisol (primary glucocorticoid; the body's endogenous steroid) — Works with catecholamines to maintain normal blood pressure and cardiac output. Inhibits reproductive hormones (male and female) — in times of stress, the body shunts energy…","Aldosterone (primary mineralocorticoid) — Acts in the kidney to reabsorb sodium and excrete potassium and hydrogen ions; water follows sodium, so aldosterone promotes fluid retention, raising extracellular volume and blood pressure.","Aldosterone's role in the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system and its blocking drugs — covered in Hypertension & Hyperlipidemia","Glucocorticoids and catecholamines also work together in the brain to help form memories of a stressor — useful because it teaches the brain how to respond to that stressor in the future, but this same mechanism can create a traumatic memor…"]},{"s":"Resistance Stage▾","a":"resistance-stage","k":["Resistance Stage▾","In the resistance stage, stress is ongoing and the body keeps trying to cope and adjust — but this response takes a lot of energy and is not an unlimited supply. One of two things happens:","Stressor resolves","The parasympathetic nervous system takes over — a cholinergic response replaces the adrenergic one.","Heart rate and blood pressure lower; immune function, sleep, and digestion improve.","Hormone levels still take time to drop back to normal — a person may feel jittery or anxious for a short while afterward (e.g., after narrowly avoiding a car accident).","Stressor continues","The body starts running out of supplies — blood sugar runs low, and norepinephrine, epinephrine, and cortisol stores are depleted.","The body can no longer sustain the same response and moves toward exhaustion."]},{"s":"Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload▾","a":"exhaustion-stage-allostatic-overload","k":["Exhaustion Stage & Allostatic Overload▾","In the exhaustion stage, the body is no longer able to return to homeostasis. Constant HPA-axis stimulation can cause the adrenal cortex to hypertrophy, while the underused lymph tissue atrophies — raising infection risk. There are no energ…","Chronic stress (living in a war zone, ongoing racism or discrimination, poverty, or being a caregiver for a sick family member) can drive the body to this exhaustion point. If stress continues unrelieved for long enough, the adrenal cortex …","Note: it was once believed that stress directly causes bleeding ulcers. That's not accurate — stress can worsen an existing ulcer, but it does not cause bleeding ulcers to develop in the first place.","Allostatic Load vs. Allostatic Overload","Allostatic load — the normal wear-and-tear demand that daily life and stress place on the HPA axis, sympathetic nervous system, and immune system as they work to maintain the body and adapt to stressors.","Allostatic overload — when those systems are chronically over- or under-activated beyond their capacity, reaching a point of exhaustion that damages organs and tissues and allows stress-induced disease to develop."]},{"s":"Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations▾","a":"chronic-stress-signs-symptoms-disease-associations","k":["Chronic Stress — Signs, Symptoms & Disease Associations▾","Physical signs & symptoms of allostatic overload","Hair loss; tension and stiff neck; tension headaches","Asthma triggers; palpitations; tics (involuntary movements)","Digestive complaints — upset stomach, nausea, diarrhea, constipation, loss of appetite","Irritable bladder; acne; irregular menstrual cycles or impotence","Sleep disturbances (either insomnia or excessive sleep)","Sleep deprivation — the inability to get quality sleep — itself increases stress levels, and it is common in hospitalized patients (a noisy, interrupted, privacy-poor environment). It can cause irritability, apathy, difficulty with memory, …","Chronic overstress is strongly associated with higher rates of coronary artery disease, hypertension, stroke, tension headaches, rheumatoid arthritis, other autoimmune disease, irritable bowel syndrome, ulcers, sexual disorders, and type 2 …"]},{"s":"Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution▾","a":"cortisol-s-metabolic-effects-glucose-protein-fat-redistribution","k":["Cortisol's Metabolic Effects — Glucose, Protein & Fat Redistribution▾","Glucose metabolism — Cortisol raises blood glucose — the opposite of insulin. It promotes gluconeogenesis, decreases peripheral glucose utilization, inhibits glucose uptake by muscle and adipose tissue, and promotes glucose storage as glyco…","Protein metabolism — Breaks down muscle tissue (muscle wasting), breaks down skin integrity (skin tears more easily), and breaks down bone tissue (fragile bones/osteoporosis).","Fat redistribution — Breaks down fat from the extremities and redistributes it to the face and trunk. With chronic cortisol exposure, this produces a characteristic \"moon face\" and \"buffalo hump.\""]},{"s":"Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes▾","a":"steroid-pharmacology-indications-routes","k":["Steroid Pharmacology — Indications & Routes▾","We can also give steroids as medications — systemic corticosteroids given orally or IV, or inhaled steroids for a more localized effect with little to no systemic action.","Replacement — The adrenal gland isn't producing enough on its own — either from primary adrenal dysfunction or because it can no longer keep up after prolonged stress.","Anti-inflammatory — Very effective at decreasing inflammation — used for systemic inflammatory responses, allergies, asthma (typically inhaled, for a localized lung effect), COPD exacerbations (often IV, transitioned to oral), chronic infla…","Immunosuppression — Smaller doses given to intentionally reduce immune response — e.g., after organ transplant to help prevent rejection. Patients are typically weaned off if no rejection issues develop."]},{"s":"Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies▾","a":"steroid-replacement-dosing-strategies","k":["Steroid Replacement Dosing Strategies▾","Steroid replacement is dosed to mimic the body's normal, physiologic pattern of cortisol release, which peaks in a burst around dawn with several smaller bumps through the day. Common approaches include alternate-day therapy, multiple small…","The body's natural early-morning cortisol/growth hormone surge and its effect on blood glucose (dawn phenomenon) — covered in Diabetes & Metabolic Syndrome","Sick-day dosing: a patient on long-term steroid replacement needs an increased dose during illness, surgery, or any major physiologic stress — their own adrenal glands cannot ramp up cortisol production the way a healthy body would. Failing…"]},{"s":"Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone▾","a":"glucocorticoid-drugs-hydrocortisone-prednisone-dexamethasone","k":["Glucocorticoid Drugs — Hydrocortisone, Prednisone & Dexamethasone▾","Hydrocortisone — PO or IV (parenteral) — Biochemically identical to endogenous cortisol — a blood test cannot distinguish a patient's own cortisol from the hydrocortisone given as a drug. Has some mineralocorticoid (aldosterone-like) activi…","Prednisone — PO — Typically dosed around 5–10 mg/day for chronic disease states (e.g., long-term low-dose use in COPD).","Dexamethasone — PO or IV — Used to assess adrenal function — unlike hydrocortisone, dexamethasone does not cross-react with the cortisol lab assay. Any cortisol measured in a patient taking dexamethasone reflects only their own (endogenous)…","Rule of thumb: give hydrocortisone to replace cortisol (it looks identical to the body's own on a lab test); give dexamethasone to test the adrenal gland (it's invisible to the cortisol assay, so any result reflects only the patient's own p…"]},{"s":"Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term▾","a":"corticosteroid-side-effects-acute-long-term","k":["Corticosteroid Side Effects — Acute & Long-Term▾","Acute Effects","Increased intraocular pressure — a particular concern in patients with glaucoma","Fluid retention and hypertension — a concern in patients with heart failure or other fluid-sensitive conditions","Mood swings","Weight gain with central fat deposits (abdomen, face, back of the neck)","Increased hunger (from cortisol's catabolic state)","Long-Term Effects (months to years of use)","Cataracts (clouded lens)","Hyperglycemia — can worsen or unmask diabetes","Increased infection risk (immunosuppression)","Osteoporosis and fractures","Suppressed adrenal gland hormone production — causes fatigue, appetite loss, nausea, and muscle weakness","Thin, papery skin with easy bruising and delayed wound healing"]},{"s":"Corticosteroid Patient Education▾","a":"corticosteroid-patient-education","k":["Corticosteroid Patient Education▾","Never stop long-term corticosteroids abruptly. This is why patients are placed on a taper — a gradually decreasing dose over time. Long-term steroid use suppresses the body's own adrenal hormone production, and abruptly withdrawing the drug…","Additional teaching points for long-term steroid therapy","Take the medication at the same time each day — consistency helps mimic the body's natural hormonal pattern.","Wear a medical alert bracelet.","Monitor weight and blood glucose regularly, since both can increase with extended steroid use.","Report illness or major stress promptly so the dose can be adjusted (see sick-day dosing above)."]},{"s":"Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone▾","a":"mineralocorticoid-replacement-fludrocortisone","k":["Mineralocorticoid Replacement — Fludrocortisone▾","Mineralocorticoids aren't often replaced on their own, because hydrocortisone already carries some aldosterone-like activity. But prednisone and dexamethasone do not have that aldosterone effect — so patients on those drugs can develop salt…","Fludrocortisone is the only mineralocorticoid replacement available — it's the aldosterone substitute, used to augment glucocorticoid replacement (with prednisone or dexamethasone) when salt wasting develops."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"review-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Pathopharm Review","Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics","The 11 Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new — it's a map of the other 11 Pathopharm Review pages, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one like it's unrelated to the rest. A handful of big ideas (a few key hormones, immune…"]},{"s":"Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","a":"five-clusters-not-eleven-separate-topics","k":["Five Clusters, Not Eleven Separate Topics▾","Eleven pages is a lot to hold in your head at once. Most of them naturally fall into one of five groups — knowing the group can matter more than memorizing all eleven titles.","Foundations — Pharmacology Foundations; Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — The vocabulary and basic mechanisms (PK/PD, cellular injury, inflammation, immunity) that every other topic quietly assumes you already know.","Cardiometabolic & Stress Hormones — HTN/HLD; Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome; Stress & Steroids — All three run on the same hormonal wiring — the RAS system, cortisol, and catecholamines regulate blood pressure, blood glucose, and the stress re…","Blood & Fluid Balance — Coagulation/DVT; Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation; Fluid/Electrolytes — The lab-value-and-safety-rule-heavy cluster — clotting factors, blood cell counts, and electrolyte levels, with a lot of \"check this number before…","Neuro & Psych Pharmacology — Pain/Seizures; Dementia, Depression & SUD — Both live in the CNS — shared neurotransmitters (norepinephrine, serotonin, GABA), shared drug-interaction dangers (CNS depressant combinations), and both loaded with …","Multisystem — GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — The broadest single page on the site — it pulls in immune-system basics from Foundations and steroid pharmacology from Cardiometabolic, on top of its own skin/GI/GU content.","If you're not sure where to start a review pass, working through the clusters in this order touches every recurring theme once before moving to the next, instead of jumping between unrelated systems."]},{"s":"The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-11-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The 11 Topics at a Glance▾","1 — Pharmacology Foundations — The core vocabulary: PK/PD, drug naming, FDA approval, controlled substances, routes, and how drugs move through (and interact within) the body.","2 — Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — What disease actually is at the cellular level, plus the building blocks of inflammation, immunity, and infection that every later topic assumes.","3 — HTN/HLD — Cholesterol, atherosclerosis, and blood pressure regulation (the RAS system), plus the drug classes used to manage both.","4 — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Insulin, glucose regulation, type 1 vs. type 2, DKA/HHNS, and the full range of diabetes and obesity pharmacology.","5 — Coagulation/DVT — How clotting works, the anticoagulant/antiplatelet drugs that interrupt it, and DVT/VTE.","6 — Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation — Red and white blood cells, reading a CBC with differential, and the blood cancers/disorders that show up on one.","7 — Fluid/Electrolytes — Fluid compartments and the five major electrolyte imbalances, with the IV-safety rules that go with correcting them.","8 — Pain/Seizures — Pain pathophysiology and pharmacology (non-opioid through opioid), plus seizure pathophysiology and antiepileptic drugs.","9 — Dementia, Depression & SUD — Delirium vs. dementia, the mood/anxiety neurotransmitters, depression/anxiety pharmacology, and substance use disorder/withdrawal.","10 — GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — The broadest page on the site: skin infections and antifungals, basic GI pharmacology, all four hypersensitivity types, and an intro to urologic problems.","11 — Stress & Steroids — How the body responds to stress (General Adaptation Syndrome, the HPA axis) and the corticosteroid drugs that mimic or replace that response."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","These aren't duplicated content — each idea is fully taught in exactly one place. But they resurface as supporting detail elsewhere, which can feel like déjà vu if you don't know why.","Cortisol & the HPA axis — Stress & Steroids — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome (cortisol as a counter-regulatory hormone; the dawn phenomenon), HTN/HLD (cortisol mentioned as a cholesterol-derived hormone)","Aldosterone & the RAS system — HTN/HLD — Stress & Steroids (aldosterone as part of the adrenal cortex's stress response; salt wasting without it)","Potassium regulation — Fluid/Electrolytes — HTN/HLD (potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitor hyperkalemia risk), Stress & Steroids (salt wasting/hyperkalemia without aldosterone effect)","Glucose regulation & hyperglycemia — Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Stress & Steroids (cortisol raises glucose; illness/steroids as a hyperglycemia trigger), Fluid/Electrolytes (D50/insulin used to treat hyperkalemia)","Immune system & inflammation basics — Cellular Adaptation, Infection & Immunity — Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation (the white blood cells that carry it out), GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (the four hypersensitivity types), Stress & Steroids (co…","Catecholamines (norepinephrine/epinephrine) — Stress & Steroids — Dementia/Depression/SUD (norepinephrine as a mood neurotransmitter), Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome (epinephrine as a counter-regulatory hormone), GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (e…","Corticosteroid pharmacology — Stress & Steroids — GI/GU/Skin/Hypersensitivity (steroids used to treat anaphylaxis and inflammatory skin conditions)"]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","Every danger callout across all 11 pages, condensed to a phrase. This is meant for a quick scan, not a replacement for the full explanation — click through to a topic for the details behind any rule.","HTN/HLD — Hypertensive emergency (BP ≥180/120 + end-organ damage) needs IV meds now. Never stop beta blockers/clonidine abruptly — rebound hypertension. ACE inhibitors can cause angioedema, which can close the airway.","Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome — Hypoglycemia is a true emergency — check blood sugar immediately. Always get a current blood sugar before giving insulin, not one from earlier.","Coagulation/DVT — Push protamine slowly — too fast causes profound hypotension; watch for heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT). Phlegmasia cerulea dolens is a true emergency that can progress to amputation.","Blood Cells/CBC Interpretation — Neutropenia — treat any infection as potentially life-threatening; use neutropenic precautions.","Fluid/Electrolytes — Correct sodium imbalances slowly — too fast risks permanent neurologic damage. Never IV push undiluted potassium — can cause fatal ventricular fibrillation.","Pain/Seizures — Never give aspirin to a child with a viral illness — Reye syndrome. Acetaminophen max 4 g/24 hr (2 g if liver disease/alcohol use). Opioid respiratory rate < 10 needs immediate assessment. Status epilepticus is a true emerge…","Dementia, Depression & SUD — Serotonin syndrome risk when combining serotonergic drugs. TCA overdose can be fatal — screen suicide risk first. MAOI + tyramine-rich food → hypertensive crisis. Never combine a benzodiazepine with another CNS …","GI, GU, Skin & Hypersensitivity — Amphotericin B is high-alert — nephrotoxic and cardiotoxic. Anaphylaxis is life-threatening. Epinephrine concentration mix-ups (1:1,000 vs. 1:10,000) can be fatal. Stop a blood transfusion immediately at th…","Stress & Steroids — Increase steroid dosing during illness or major stress (sick-day dosing). Never stop long-term corticosteroids abruptly — risk of adrenal crisis.","Patterns you'll notice across these rules","\"Never stop this abruptly\" shows up three separate times — beta blockers/clonidine (HTN/HLD), antiepileptic drugs (Pain/Seizures), and corticosteroids (Stress & Steroids). Different drugs, same underlying principle: the drug has been suppre…","\"Get a current number immediately before dosing\" appears for both blood sugar before insulin (Diabetes/Metabolic Syndrome) and blood pressure/heart rate before a beta blocker (HTN/HLD) — the same instinct to verify the current state rather …","\"This can look mild at first but escalates fast\" is really the same warning behind anaphylaxis, a transfusion reaction, ACE inhibitor angioedema, hypertensive crisis, and status epilepticus — five different diseases teaching the same nursin…"]}]},{"f":"topic-template.html","t":"Topic Page Template","secs":[{"s":"Topic Page Template","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Glossary Table","Checklist Table","Callouts","Comparison Cards","Lecture Diagram","Self-Test Flashcards"]},{"s":"Glossary Table▾","a":"glossary-table","k":["Glossary Table▾","First term — The definition, straight from the lecture. — The consequence, complication, or clinical significance.","Second term — Keep the wording close to how the instructor said it. — If the lecture didn't say it, it doesn't go here.","Third term — Three to six columns works; more than that gets cramped on a phone. — Tables stack into cards below 640px automatically."]},{"s":"Checklist Table▾","a":"checklist-table","k":["Checklist Table▾","Do the first thing — Because of this reason.","Then the second thing — Because of this other reason."]},{"s":"Callouts▾","a":"callouts","k":["Callouts▾","MNEMONIC (blue): an acronym, a memory hook, or a rule of thumb. This is the \"here's the trick for remembering it\" box.","DANGER (red): a true emergency, a priority action, or a hard safety rule. Reserve it — if everything is red, nothing is.","Cross-reference (accent): this topic is covered again here — review in Must Know if needed","A plain note. Use it for a short framing sentence under a heading, or a caveat that isn't worth a callout."]},{"s":"Comparison Cards▾","a":"comparison-cards","k":["Comparison Cards▾","Option A","First distinguishing feature","Second distinguishing feature","Third distinguishing feature","Option B","How it differs on the first point","How it differs on the second point","How it differs on the third point","Full-Width Single List","A lone card outside a .two-col fills the width.","Use this instead of a one-column table — a table with a single column wastes a lot of horizontal space."]},{"s":"Lecture Diagram▾","a":"lecture-diagram","k":["Lecture Diagram▾","Images live in images/<topic>/ and never appear inline — they open in a lightbox from a pill button. Uncomment the block below once you have a real image."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]}];
